\id EZK EN_UST en_English_ltr Wed Sep 09 2020 11:03:22 GMT-0500 (Central Daylight Time) tc
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Ezekiel
\toc1 The Book of Ezekiel
\toc2 Ezekiel
\toc3 Ezk
\mt1 Ezekiel
\c 1
\p
\v 1 When I, Ezekiel, was thirty years old, I was living alongside the Kebar River, among the Israelite people whom the Babylonians had forcibly removed from the land of Judah. It was on the fifth day of the fourth month that the sky looked as though it was opening, and I beheld God’s very presence.
\v 2 That day was almost five years after the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar forcibly removed King Jehoiachin from Jerusalem.
\v 3 God spoke his messages directly to me, Ezekiel the priest, Buzi’s son, at the time when I lived in the land of the Chaldeans, along the Kebar River. Yahweh empowered me to receive what God wanted me to see and hear.
\p
\v 4 Then I looked, and what I saw was a windstorm coming out of the north. It took the shape of a large cloud with a massive fire burning within itself. A brilliant light radiated all around the storm, and its center glowed like hot metal in a fire.
\v 5 In the very center of the windstorm appeared what seemed to be four creatures. Here is what they looked like—they resembled humans,
\v 6 but every one of them had four different faces and four wings.
\v 7 Their two legs were straight, like human legs, but their two feet resembled the hooves of calves, and they glowed like the glimmer of polished bronze.
\v 8 Each creature had human hands, which rested underneath the wing that was on each of their four sides. As for their four faces and four wings—
\v 9 each creature’s wing touched the wing of another, so that, together, the four creatures formed a square. Whenever they moved, they did not turn, but instead moved forward in the direction of one of their four faces.
\b
\v 10 Now, their faces looked like this: In front, each creature had a face that resembled a human face. The face on the right side resembled a lion’s face. The face on the left side resembled an ox’s face, and the face in the back resembled an eagle’s face.
\v 11 That was what their faces looked like. As for their wings, each creature had his four wings raised and spread out above them. Two of each creature’s wings touched two of the wings of the other creatures that were on either side of it. The other two wings they folded so as to cover their bodies.
\v 12 Without turning as they went, these creatures always moved in the direction of one of their faces. They advanced in whichever direction their spirit guided them.
\v 13 The form of the four creatures resembled glowing charcoal in the middle of a blazing fire or like flaming torches. Their fire flashed back and forth among the four creatures. It gave off a brilliant, fiery light, and streaks of lightning darted out of the fire.
\v 14 Yes, the creatures were moving back and forth so rapidly that they resembled lightning strikes!
\b
\v 15 Then I looked at the creatures, and what I saw was a single wheel on the ground beside each of the four-faced creatures.
\v 16 Each wheel was identical, and they all glistened like the gleam of a fine topaz. In terms of their construction, each wheel was made of a single wheel intersecting another wheel.
\v 17 Without rotating as they went, the wheels likewise always moved in one of the four directions corresponding to one of the faces of the four-faced creatures.
\v 18 As for the outer rims of the wheels—they were towering and terrifying, and each one of the four creatures’ wheels was completely covered with eyes!
\b
\v 19 Whenever the creatures advanced, their wheels advanced alongside them. Likewise, whenever the creatures ascended above the ground, so too did their wheels.
\v 20 The four creatures’ wheels advanced in whichever direction their spirit guided them. Likewise, the wheels rose alongside their creature, because the wheels contained the creature’s spirit that gave them life.
\v 21 Whenever the four creatures advanced, their wheels also advanced. Whenever the creatures stopped moving, their wheels also stopped. And whenever the creatures rose above the ground, their wheels also rose alongside. This was because the wheels contained the creatures’ spirit that gave them life.
\b
\v 22 Above the heads of the creatures, there was something that resembled a vaulted dome. It was awesome and glistened like crystals of ice, arching high over their heads.
\v 23 Under the vaulted dome, each creature’s four wings were stretched out—two were touching the wings of the other creatures that were on either side of it, and two were folded to cover the creature’s body.
\v 24 Then I heard the noise that their wings made when the four creatures advanced. The noise of their wings sounded like the crashing of ocean waves, or like the voice of the all-powerful God. It sounded like the din of a massive crowd of people, or like the uproar of an army marching to war. Whenever the creatures stopped, they lowered their outstretched wings.
\b
\v 25 Then the creatures stopped and lowered their outstretched wings. A voice coming from above the vaulted dome that was over their heads began to speak.
\v 26 Then, above the vaulted dome that arched high over the heads of the four creatures, there was something that resembled a king’s royal chair. It looked like someone had hewn it from sapphire stones. And something sat high above on that sapphire throne—a creature that looked human.
\v 27 I noticed that, above what seemed like his waist, the bodily form of this human-like creature resembled the glowing of hot metal, completely enclosed by fire. Then, I noticed that the bodily form below what seemed like his waist resembled a blazing fire. Radiating all around, brilliant light
\v 28 shone like a rainbow in the clouds on a rainy day. This is what the brilliant radiance that glowed all around looked like. This light displayed how powerful and glorious Yahweh is while he appeared to me. When I saw all of this, I bowed with my face to the ground. Then, I heard the human-like creature who was sitting on that sapphire throne begin to speak.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 The one sitting on the throne said to me, “Human, stand up! Then I will speak to you.”
\v 2 As he said this, God’s Spirit came upon me and enabled me to stand up. Then I heard the one sitting on the throne speak to me.
\b
\v 3 He said, “Human, I am about to send you to the Israelite people. Every one of the family clans of Israel has rejected my authority and refused to obey me as my people, like they promised to do. They, like their ancestors, have never stopped openly defying me—even to this very day!
\v 4 My children, the people of Israel, are obstinate and stubborn. I am sending you to talk to them on my behalf. You must declare to them what I, the Lord Yahweh, am speaking to them directly.
\v 5 As for them, when you declare my messages, the Israelite people may indeed heed what you tell them, or they may yet refuse to listen. After all, these people reject my authority and openly defy me! Nevertheless, they will eventually admit that a prophet has been among them, and that Yahweh himself is speaking to them!
\p
\v 6 But as for you, human, you must not be afraid of them, nor fear what they say. These people will be as hurtful to you as thorns and thistles are to your skin! Living among them will be like living among scorpions! Still, you must not allow what they say about you to make you afraid, or let their presence terrify you. After all, these people reject my authority and openly defy me!
\v 7 Nonetheless, declare my message to them. The Israelite people may indeed heed what you tell them, or they may still refuse to listen. After all, they openly defy me!
\p
\v 8 As for you, human, you must heed whatever I say to you. You must not openly defy me like these people frequently do! Now, open your mouth! Eat what I am about to give you.”
\b
\p
\v 9 Then, as I watched, I saw him stretch out his hand to me, and I saw that he was holding a scroll that contained writing.
\v 10 He unfurled the scroll so that I could see it. On both sides of it, someone had written the words of poems that expressed despair, grief, and warning.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 The one sitting on the throne said to me, “Human, eat it. Yes, eat this scroll! Then, go! Declare my message to the Israelite people.”
\v 2 I opened my mouth wide, and he fed the scroll to me.
\p
\v 3 Then he said to me, “Human, chew and swallow the scroll that I have given to you. Fill your stomach with it.” I did so, and it tasted as sweet as honey in my mouth.
\p
\v 4 Then he said to me, “Human, it is time for you to go to the Israelite people, so that you might declare my message to them.
\v 5 The people to whom I am sending you do not speak a language that is so complex that it is difficult to speak or understand. I am sending you to your own Israelite people.
\v 6 Again, I am not sending you to many different people who speak languages that are too complex, whose language you cannot understand. If I were to send you to those people, they would heed your message!
\v 7 Yet the Israelite people will never want to heed what you say. After all, they do not even want to heed what I, Yahweh, say! This is because all of them are obstinate and stubborn.
\v 8 But, pay attention! I have enabled you to be as obstinate to them as they are to me, and as stubbornly opposed to them as they are to me!
\v 9 I have enabled you to be more resolute and dangerous to the Israelite people than a thorn that is extremely hard! Still, you must not allow them to make you afraid or let their presence terrify you. After all, these people reject my authority and openly defy me!”
\b
\p
\v 10 Then he said to me, “Human, think very carefully about everything I say to you, and listen to it attentively.
\v 11 So now, go to your own people, your fellow Israelites, whom the Babylonians have forcibly removed from the land of Judah, so that you can talk to them. You must declare to them what I, the Lord Yahweh, am speaking to them directly. You must do this whether the Israelite people heed what you tell them or whether they refuse to listen.”
\b
\p
\v 12 Then God’s Spirit lifted me up. Behind me, I heard something that sounded like a massive earthquake. The sound sounded like someone shouting the words, “Praise our glorious Yahweh in the temple!”
\v 13 I also heard the noise of the wings of the four creatures as they began to flap and brush against the other creatures’ wings, along with the noise of the wheels advancing alongside them. All of this sounded as if it were a massive earthquake.
\v 14 Then God’s Spirit carried me up and took me away from the river. Yet, as I went, I was resentful and angry, and Yahweh was powerfully authorizing me to declare his message.
\v 15 So, I went to the people whom the Babylonians had forcibly removed from Judah and resettled in the town of Tel Aviv near the Kebar River in Babylon. There, where they were living, I sat down and did not move for seven days. I sat there among them, completely overwhelmed.
\p
\v 16 After those seven days had ended, Yahweh spoke this message to me,
\v 17 “Human, I am appointing you to give warnings of danger to the Israelite people like the watchman at the gate of a city. So, when you hear the message that I speak to you directly, you must declare it to the people to warn them.
\v 18 Now, I might declare that a sinful person is assuredly going to die because of his sin. And you might choose not to warn him or inform him that, if he desires to save his life, he must cease his sinful lifestyle. If you do this, the sinful person will die because of his sinful behavior. However, I will consider that you are responsible for his death, {since you did not act to prevent it}.
\v 19 However, you might choose to warn that sinful person, and yet he might not cease his sinful lifestyle. If this happens, that person will die because of his sinful behavior. Yet, as for you, you will have prevented me from punishing you.
\b
\p
\v 20 Alternatively, a just person might cease his honest behavior and begin acting unjustly. When that happens, I may cause his life to become difficult. That person will die because of his sin. However, you might choose not to warn him that he deserves to die. If you do this, the formerly just person will die nevertheless, because of his recent unjust behavior. I will not credit him for the just things that he once did. However, I will consider that you are responsible for his death, {since you did not act to prevent it}.
\v 21 However, you might choose to warn that previously just person, so that he ceases his sinful behavior and returns to his just lifestyle. If this happens, that individual will continue to live because you warned him that he would die because of his sin. Furthermore, as for you, you will have prevented me from punishing you.”
\b
\p
\v 22 Then, Yahweh was powerfully present with me there. He spoke to me, “Stand up, and travel to the valley outside of town. At that place, I will talk to you.”
\v 23 So, I stood up and went out to the valley. There, I suddenly saw the powerful and glorious vision of Yahweh standing before me. It was just like the glorious vision that I had seen when I was beside the Kebar River. When I saw it, I bowed with my face to the ground.
\p
\v 24 Then, God’s Spirit came upon me and enabled me to stand up. Yahweh said to me, “Go into your house and lock yourself inside it.
\v 25 As for you, human—pay attention! The people who live near you will tie you up with ropes, so that you will be unable to go out among them.
\v 26 And I will make you unable to speak, as if you were mute and your tongue was stuck to the top of your mouth. So, I will make it impossible for you to pronounce my rebukes against these people. After all, every one of the Israelite people continuously refuses to listen to me or obey me!
\v 27 However, whenever I tell you my messages, I will empower you to declare them to the Israelite people. You must declare to the people what I, the Lord Yahweh, am speaking to them directly. The one who is willing to listen to me will listen, but the one who refuses to accept my message will continue to refuse me. After all, they are all people who continuously reject me and disobey me!”
\c 4
\p
\v 1 {Yahweh continued to speak to me,} “As for you, human, find yourself a large clay brick. Then, you should place it in front of you and draw a picture of the city of Jerusalem on it.
\v 2 Then, lay siege to the brick! Build siege walls around it and construct a siege ramp! Make camps for enemy soldiers around the brick! Assemble battering rams around the brick of Jerusalem!
\v 3 Then, find yourself an iron frying pan, and set it so that it resembles a vast iron wall. It should separate you and your drawing of Jerusalem on the clay brick. Then, turn to face it with firm resolve to besiege the city. This will be a warning for the Israelite people [that I am about to judge them}.
\p
\v 4 Next, you should lie down on your left side and carry the entirety of the sins of the people of Israel. You must continue to lie there for a certain number of days, to represent enduring the punishment for the sins of the Israelite people.
\v 5 Now, I myself have determined the exact length of time that you should do this; one day for each year they have sinned against me. So you must stay in this position for 390 days, to represent enduring the punishment for the sins of the Israelite people.
\p
\v 6 When you have finished lying on your left side for 390 days, you should lie down a second time. This time, lie on your right side, and carry the sins of the people of the southern kingdom of Judah. I have determined that you should, once again, lie down one day for each of the years that I will punish the people of Judah for their sins against me: 40 days.
\v 7 So, you should firmly resolve within yourself with regard to your drawing of the siege of Jerusalem, and stretch out your bare arm against it. As you do this, you must proclaim my prophetic messages against the city.
\v 8 Now, pay attention! I am going to tie you down with ropes so you will not be able to turn from one side to the other. You will remain this way until you have finished lying down for the number of days that I have determined for you to do this.
\p
\v 9 As for you, you should acquire portions of wheat, barley, beans, lentils, millet, and rye. You should store these together in a single container and use the mixed grains, seeds, and legumes to bake bread for yourself. You will eat this bread throughout the 390 days during which you lie on your left side.
\v 10 Your meals must be weighed to be eight ounces a day. You must eat it at fixed times throughout the day.
\v 11 Likewise, any water that you drink must be measured to be a pint and a half. You must also drink at fixed times throughout the day.
\v 12 You should eat your meals like you would eat a loaf of barley bread. However, you must bake your food while the Israelite people are watching, using human excrement for fuel.”
\p
\v 13 Yahweh continued to talk to me. He said, “Your carefully weighed and measured meals that you cook over human excrement will symbolize to the Israelite people that they too will resort to eating food that is unacceptable to me when I force them out of their land to live among the people groups that surround them.”
\p
\v 14 But I declared, “Oh, Lord Yahweh! Please, do not force me to eat my meals in this ritually impure manner! I have never done anything that caused a priest to declare that I was not able to worship in your presence. As long as I have been alive, I have never once eaten the disgusting meat of roadkill or carrion, nor have I ever eaten any desecrated meat from a spoiled sacrifice!”
\p
\v 15 Yahweh replied, “Very well. I will allow you to bake your bread using dried cow manure instead of human excrement for fuel.”
\p
\v 16 Then he said to me, “Human, I am about to remove Jerusalem’s supply of food. When I do this, the people who live in Jerusalem will resort to anxiously rationing their meager meals and drinking their carefully allocated portions of water in increasing panic.
\v 17 This will happen because I will make food and water scarce for the people who live in Jerusalem. Every individual will feel a horrified shock of terror. Because I am punishing them, the wrongs that they have committed will cause them to experience life as if they were something that someone has left out to rot.”
\c 5
\p
\v 1 “As for you, human, you must acquire a sharp sword and use it like a barber’s razor to shave your head and your beard. Then, you must acquire a scale and weigh the hair that you removed.
\v 2 After your siege of the drawing of the city of Jerusalem is completed, you must take a third of the hair that you have weighed and burn it in a fire in the middle of your drawing of the city of Jerusalem that you have besieged. Then, you must take another third of the hair and slash at it with your sword so that it falls all around the city. Then throw the final third of the hair so that it disperses in the wind. I myself will cut up the hair that flies away in the wind, as if I unleashed a sword against it.
\v 3 But you must first reserve a few of the hairs from the final third and tie them to your sleeves.
\v 4 Then, you must take a few of those hairs that you have tied to your sleeves and throw them into the fire, so that they burn up. After all, a fire in Jerusalem will soon spread and extend to the entirety of the Israelite people.
\p
\v 5 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘This drawing on the clay brick represents the city of Jerusalem. I have positioned this city in the middle of all the nations, surrounded by other empires.
\v 6 However, by doing wicked acts, the citizens of Jerusalem have rejected the norms by which I have commanded and decreed that they should live. They have done this to a greater degree than any of the people who live in nations and empires outside of Israel! After all, in their lifestyles, they have refused the norms by which I have commanded and decreed that they should live.’
\p
\v 7 For this reason, here is what I, the Lord Yahweh, have to say, ‘You citizens of Jerusalem have been more conceited and disobedient than any of the people of the nations around you. Because of this, you have not lived by the norms that I have commanded and decreed for you. You have not even lived by the norms of the other nations around you!’
\p
\v 8 For this reason, here is what I, the Lord Yahweh, have to say! ‘Pay attention! Even I, your own God, have become an enemy to you, citizens of Jerusalem. So, I will carry out my norms among you by punishing you in the full view of the nations and empires around you.
\v 9 Yes, I am about to do something to you that is unlike anything I have ever done before or ever will do again. It will all happen because of your detestable and disgusting actions.
\v 10 As a result, parents among you will resort to consuming their own children, and children will resort to consuming their own parents. So I will execute the verdict of my just judgments against you. I will forcibly remove anyone who survives from the city and disperse them in every direction.’
\p
\v 11 This is what the Lord Yahweh declares: ‘I swear by myself: You have defiled the holy place in which I live among you with all your disgusting idols and with the other abominable practices that you do. Because of this, I will withdraw my presence from you. I will neither regard you with pity nor will I save you from my imminent judgments against you.
\v 12 The actions you took with the hair that you weighed out symbolize what will happen to the people of Jerusalem. Like the third of the hair that you burned, one-third of the citizens of Jerusalem will die inside the city because of the fatal infectious diseases they will experience or because of a lack of food. Like the third that you slashed with your sword, one-third of the people will die outside the city because of the fatal wounds inflicted on them by their enemies’ swords. Finally, like the third that you threw into the wind, I will forcibly disperse the remaining third of the people of Jerusalem in every direction, but I will cause your enemies to pursue them so that they kill them with their swords.
\p
\v 13 Then I will have fully expressed my anger. I will calm myself from my rage against them, and, since I exacted my just judgments against them, I will console myself. When my wrath against them has subsided, the people who live in Jerusalem will recognize that I, Yahweh, have said everything that I have declared to them because I will never stop loving them as my own people.
\v 14 So, I will destroy Jerusalem until it is nothing but a ruin. I will turn it into such a place that the people of other nations that are around you will mock it as they walk past.
\v 15 Yes, my destruction of the city of Jerusalem will make it known as something people mock and tease. But when I execute the verdict of my just judgments against the citizens of Jerusalem, my furious discipline of my people will also serve as a terrifying warning to the other nations that surround you. This will assuredly happen because I, Yahweh, have said it!
\v 16 Yes, the citizens of Jerusalem will suffer fatal infectious diseases, as if I were shooting deadly arrows against them like a besieging army. I intend these diseases to utterly annihilate the people. When this happens, I will also intensify the severity of the conditions that have resulted in a lethal lack of food. In so doing, I will entirely remove Jerusalem’s supply of bread on which they rely like a walking stick.
\v 17 Yes, I will cause the citizens of Jerusalem to suffer a lack of food. But I will also release wild and deadly animals against them. They will attack them and murder all of their children. I will make these people suffer fatal infectious diseases and the bloodshed of war, and I will enable their enemies to kill them with their swords. This will all assuredly happen because I, Yahweh, have spoken!’”
\c 6
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, turn toward the mountains of Israel and speak against them what I tell you to say.
\v 3 You must proclaim, ‘Mountains of Israel, attend to what the Lord Yahweh declares! Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say to you about the high mountains and hilltops, and to the bottoms of the river streams and the low valleys, “Pay attention to what I am about to do! I will soon dispatch enemy soldiers with swords against you. They will destroy all the elevated ritual places at which you worship other gods.
\v 4 These warring soldiers will smash and defile all your altars—both those you use for offering meat to the idols of other gods and those you use to offer incense. The soldiers I send against you will kill many Israelite people, and their dead bodies will collapse on the ground in front of the ritual statues they used to worship.
\v 5 I will ensure that the defiling dead bodies of Israelite people lie in the presence of their ritual statues, and I will strew their ritually impure bones all about the altars on which they used to offer meat sacrifices to their false gods.
\v 6 Wherever you live, your towns will lie in ruins. The destruction of the coming war will cause you to abandon the elevated places where you used to worship ritual statues, so that they become deserted wastes. The invading armies will obliterate the altars on which you offered meat to other gods. They will crush your ritual statues and hack the altars on which you offered incense to other gods into splinters. In the end, every way that you once devised to honor other gods will become nothing but a useless pile of ruins.
\v 7 This coming destruction will kill many people, and their dead bodies will drop right in front of you. When all of this occurs, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\p
\v 8 However, I will allow a portion of you to remain alive. You who survive the coming destruction will live in other nations like fugitives attempting to escape your armed enemies. Your enemies will forcibly disperse you and relocate you to live among many other empires.
\v 9 When this happens, in the very places in which the other nations forcibly deported you, those of you who remain alive will consider me once again and remember who I am. You will finally realize how much deep sorrow and emotional pain you caused me. Your deepest desires were like an adulterating spouse, and you yearned to leave your relationship with me and belong to other gods! These other gods dominated your attention and devotion, like a woman lusting after a man other than her husband! As a result, you will one day feel shameful disgust toward yourselves when you think about all of the evil things you once did with your disgusting and unacceptable behavior.
\v 10 In the end, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh! You will realize that when I said that I would cause these disastrous calamities to happen to you, I would undoubtedly do them!”
\p
\v 11 So, here is what the Lord Yahweh says, “Ezekiel, clap your hands together and stamp your feet! Exclaim a wail of lament over every disgustingly wicked thing that the Israelite people do! After all, they are all going to die soon—whether by their enemies’ swords, extreme hunger, or rampant infectious diseases.
\v 12 Those who are far from Jerusalem will fatally suffer rampant infectious diseases, and the swords of Israel’s enemies will murder those who are close to Jerusalem. And those who remain alive after all of that will starve to death in extreme hunger. In this way, I will completely vent my furious anger against them.
\v 13 The dead bodies of those who die from this disaster will lie intermingled with the ritual statues that they used to worship. They will lie together on every side of the altars on which they used to offer meat sacrifices to other gods, and on every elevated hilltop and on every mountain peak, under every verdant tree and sacred oak—at every place at which the Israelite people once burned pleasing aromatic incense to ritual statues to garner the favor of other gods. When you see this, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 14 Yes, I will powerfully confront the Israelite people by causing the entire region of Israel to become a ruined, deserted wasteland—everywhere they have ever lived, from the desert in the most southern parts of Israel as far as the town of Diblah in the remote north. When they see this, they will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”’”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Now, as for you, human, here is what I, the Lord Yahweh, have to say to the inhabitants of the land of Israel,
\q1 ‘The end is here! Yes, the end is imminent
\q2 for every single portion of the region.
\v 3 You people of Israel, your demise is now imminent.
\q2 I will soon vent my anger against you.
\q1 I will execute the just sentence that befits your lifestyle
\q2 and hold you responsible for your disgusting and unacceptable behavior.
\q1 \v 4 I will not have such compassion for you
\q2 that I relent from my judgments against you.
\q1 Instead, I will hold you responsible for your lifestyles,
\q2 and you will experience the just consequences that your disgusting and unacceptable behavior deserves.
\q2 Then, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!’
\b
\q1 \v 5 Here is something else that I, the Lord Yahweh, have to say,
\q1 ‘A calamity—an unparalleled calamity!
\q2 Pay attention! It is imminent.
\q1 \v 6 Your demise is impending.
\q2 Yes, your final demise is impending!
\q1 It will soon happen to you, as if you woke it up!
\q2 Pay attention! It is imminent!
\q1 \v 7 Catastrophe is looming upon you,
\q2 you who inhabit the territories of Israel.
\q1 The predetermined moment has arrived! It is nearly Yahweh’s chosen time for judgment!
\q2 At that time, the sound you hear on the mountains will not be the shouts of joyful celebration
\q2 —rather, it will be the panicked shouts of anguish!
\q1 \v 8 Very soon, I will fully express my rage against you
\q2 and vent the anger I feel toward you.
\q1 I will execute the just sentence that befits your lifestyle
\q2 and hold you responsible for your disgusting and unacceptable behavior.
\q1 \v 9 I will not have such compassion for you
\q2 that I relent from my judgments against you.
\q1 I will hold you responsible for your lifestyles,
\q2 and you will experience the just consequences that your disgusting and unacceptable behavior deserves.
\q2 Then, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like. I am Yahweh—the one who is injuring you!
\b
\q1 \v 10 Pay attention! It is nearly Yahweh’s chosen time for judgment!
\q2 Pay attention! It is about to happen!
\q1 Catastrophe is looming, as if it is about to burst forth!
\q2 Just like the branch of a tree that has begun to blossom in the spring,
\q2 your proud behavior is coming into fruition—it is budding!
\q1 \v 11 Just like a burgeoning tree that produces a new branch,
\q2 their violent choices have matured into fully formed wicked lifestyles!
\q1 In the end, none of it will survive the coming destruction—
\q2 not their large population,
\q1 nor their excessive wealth,
\q2 nor their international renown!
\q1 \v 12 Now is the moment!
\q2 The time that I, Yahweh, have chosen for judgment has arrived!
\q1 Customers should not rejoice,
\q2 nor should vendor grieve.
\q2 After all, I, Yahweh, am furiously angry with the entire Israelite people!
\q1 \v 13 This is because, as long as he remains alive,
\q2 the vendor will never return to the place where he used to sell his wares.
\q1 After all, the things that I, Yahweh, have revealed to my prophet pertain to everyone in Israel!
\q2 It will not fail to happen.
\q2 No one who commits injustices will be unable to succeed in staying alive.
\b
\q1 \v 14 Israel’s commanders will blow trumpets
\q2 to ready the army to fight in the war,
\q1 but no one will respond,
\q2 because I, Yahweh, am furiously angry with the entire Israelite people!
\q1 \v 15 The armed army of Israel’s enemies will station itself outside Jerusalem,
\q2 while infectious diseases and a fatal lack of food ravage the city from within.
\q1 So, Israel’s enemies will murder whoever happens to be outside the city in the fields,
\q2 and whoever happens to be inside the city will succumb to the fatal infectious diseases
\q2 as they starve to death in extreme hunger.
\q1 \v 16 Those few who survive the destruction of Jerusalem
\q2 will flee to the mountains.
\q1 Their evil behavior will cause each of them to make wailing noises
\q2 that sound like the cries of pigeons who live in the mountain valleys.
\q1 \v 17 Each survivor will be so afraid
\q2 that their hands will hang limp at their sides
\q2 and they will lose control of their bladders.
\q1 \v 18 They will publicly mourn by wearing clothes made from rough cloth,
\q2 and they will feel such horror, as if it covered them like clothing.
\q1 The expression on every face will show that they feel ashamed,
\q2 and everyone will shave his head in his grief.
\b
\q1 \v 19 They will discard their silver coins into the streets
\q2 and regard their gold coins as if they were made ritually impure with menstrual blood.
\q1 None of their money will be able to deliver them
\q2 when I, Yahweh, unleash my raging anger against them.
\q1 No longer will they use their money to buy food to satisfy their appetites.
\q2 After all, having a lot of gold and silver has led them to do evil things!
\q1 \v 20 The beautiful treasures that the priest stored in my temple
\q2 caused the people of Israel to become arrogant and proud.
\q1 They used the beautiful things that belonged to me, Yahweh,
\q2 to craft disgusting and abhorrent ritual statues of their false gods.
\q1 So I, Yahweh, will cause them to regard my sacred treasure
\q2 as if it had become ritually impure with menstrual blood.
\q1 \v 21 Yes, I will endow the treasures of my temple to strangers
\q2 as a reward for their military victory against the Israelite people.
\q1 I will give it to evil, wicked people who live in the nations surrounding Israel as the profit of their war.
\q2 As a result, these wicked strangers will pollute and ruin it by using it for purposes other than worshiping me.
\q1 \v 22 Then, I, Yahweh, will no longer be present with the Israelite people.
\q2 I will look away as the warring soldiers of Israel’s enemies pollute and destroy my beloved temple.
\q1 Yes, bloodthirsty people will enter that sacred place in which I lived among my people,
\q2 and, as they do so, they will ruin and pollute it.
\b
\q1 \v 23 Forge a set of chains!
\q2 Israel’s enemies will require them
\q1 when they capture and indict the murderous Israelite people who occupy the region
\q2 and the homicidal people who live in Jerusalem!
\q1 \v 24 So I will summon the armies of the most morally repulsive nations
\q2 to capture the houses of the Israelite people forcibly and live in them themselves.
\q1 I will humble those arrogant Israelite people who consider themselves strong,
\q2 as they watch their warring enemies ruin and pollute the sacred places in which they worshiped other gods.
\q1 \v 25 Very soon, when Israel’s enemies terrify them,
\q2 they will desperately attempt to stop the violence,
\q2 but will be utterly unable to do so.
\q1 \v 26 The people will suffer ceaseless catastrophes,
\q2 and rumors about other catastrophes will proliferate endlessly.
\q1 The Israelite people will beg Yahweh’s prophets to inform them of Yahweh’s desires and decisions,
\q2 but the priests will no longer teach people what Yahweh told his people in his instructions to Moses,
\q2 nor will the elderly give wise advice anymore.
\q1 \v 27 Israel’s king will grieve,
\q2 and his son will feel such despair, as if he wore it like clothing;
\q2 everywhere in Israel, people will feel incapacitated by their horror.
\q1 I will do to the Israelite people what their lifestyles deserve;
\q2 I will legally evaluate and condemn them by their own legal standards.
\q2 Then, they will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!’”
\c 8
\p
\v 1 Some time later, on the fifth day of the sixth month of the year—six years after the Babylonian army forcibly removed me from the land of Judah, along with King Jehoiachin and many others—I was sitting inside my house, and the elders of Judah were sitting in front of me. Right there, at that moment, the Lord Yahweh powerfully enabled me to see and proclaim his messages.
\v 2 As I watched, I suddenly saw a humanoid figure resembling a blazing fire. I noticed that what seemed like his waist and below looked as if it were on fire. Above what seemed like his waist, the bodily form of this humanoid figure resembled the radiating glow of molten, white-hot metal.
\v 3 Then, the blazing figure reached out what looked like a hand and grabbed me by a strand of hair at the top of my head. Then the wind of Yahweh’s Spirit lifted me up into the sky, so that I hovered between the earth and the sky. Then, in this vision from God, the wind carried me along to Jerusalem. I arrived at the gated entrance to the inner court of the temple—that is, the entrance that faces north. There, I saw a sculpted ritual statue of a god other than Yahweh. This seated idol incited impassioned jealousy within Yahweh.
\v 4 Then, suddenly, in front of me, I saw the glorious brilliance that showed me that the God of Israel was there with me. It looked just like what I had seen several months before, when I was in the valley along the Kebar River.
\b
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh said to me, “Human, look up now and gaze northwards.” So I looked up and gazed northwards. Then I could see that sculpted ritual image that incited impassioned jealousy within Yahweh, sitting in an area to the north of the gated entrance to the inner courtyard, through which one could see the altar on which priests entirely burned up meat sacrifices to Yahweh.
\b
\p
\v 6 Then Yahweh said to me, “Human, just look at what they are doing! The entire Israelite people are doing extraordinarily disgusting and inappropriate things here! They are alienating me from my own sacred temple! Still, you will soon witness things that are even more extraordinarily disgusting and inappropriate.”
\b
\p
\v 7 Then Yahweh brought me to the entrance of the temple courtyard. When I looked, I noticed a hole in the north wall of the temple.
\v 8 Yahweh said to me, “Human, dig into the wall here.” So I started to dig into the wall, and suddenly an entrance appeared.
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh said to me, “Go in through the entrance and witness the morally repulsive, disgusting things that people are doing inside my temple.”
\v 10 So I went in and looked around. There, I saw that on every part of each wall of the room, someone had engraved depictions of every sort of ritually impure crawling animal imaginable, along with all kinds of disgusting animals, and depictions of every other god that the people of Israel worshiped by honoring ritual statues.
\v 11 In front of all these engravings stood 70 of the elders of the Israelite people. Even Jaazaniah, the son of Shaphan, one of the military commanders of King Zedekiah, was standing among them! Each person was holding his own metal pan for ritually wafting the smoke of burning incense. As they stood, a thick aromatic cloud of incense smoke was rising up.
\v 12 Then Yahweh said to me, “Human, just look at what the elders of the Israelite people are doing in the dark—each one of them standing in an interior room full of engravings of his gods! They reason with themselves: ‘Surely, Yahweh cannot see us; he has left his home in the temple and vacated the land of Israel.’”
\v 13 He continued, “Soon, you will witness again these people doing things that are even more extraordinarily disgusting and inappropriate.”
\p
\v 14 Then he brought me to the opening of the northern gated entrance of Yahweh’s temple. I suddenly noticed a group of women sitting there. They were weeping, performing a ritual lament that grieved for Tammuz, the deceased Mesopotamian god-king!
\v 15 Then Yahweh said to me, “Human, just look at this! Yet still, you will again witness things that are even more extraordinarily disgusting and inappropriate than this.”
\p
\v 16 He brought me into the interior courtyard of Yahweh’s temple. Suddenly, I noticed that there, at the very entrance of Yahweh’s temple, between the entrance’s porch and the altar in the middle of the interior courtyard, stood about 25 men. They had turned their backs to Yahweh’s temple and were instead facing east. I noticed that they were bowing low in an act of reverential worship to the sun, the manifestation of the god Shamash.
\p
\v 17 Then Yahweh said to me, “Human, just look at this! Yet, these disgusting and inappropriate practices that the people of Judah are doing here, in my temple, are the least serious offense they have committed. Everywhere they live, these people behave violently, never ceasing to anger and grieve me. Yes, look! Their arrogant, violent crimes against one another openly insult and disrespect me, as if they were mockingly waving a stick in my face!
\v 18 Therefore, I myself will behave angrily. I will not feel such tender sympathy for these people that I restrain my anger against them. Even when they desperately invoke me and shout my name, I will simply ignore them.”
\c 9
\p
\v 1 Then I heard Yahweh shout, “You who perform public executions within the city of Jerusalem, approach! Arm yourselves with your lethal weapons.”
\v 2 Suddenly, I noticed that six men were arriving from the direction of the upper gated entrance to the temple courtyard—that is, the one that faces north. Each one of them had armed himself with a deadly and destructive weapon. Among them stood one man who wore linen clothing, and he had strapped a writing kit to his waist, such as a scribe carries. They entered the temple courtyard and positioned themselves beside the bronze altar.
\p
\v 3 Then the glorious brilliance that showed that the God of Israel was with me ascended above the winged beings over which it had previously rested, and it moved to the very doorstep of Yahweh’s temple. Then Yahweh addressed the man who wore the linen clothing, who also had strapped the scribal writing kit to his waist.
\v 4 Yahweh said to him, “Proceed throughout the city of Jerusalem. As you do, mark the foreheads of every person who laments and grieves all the disgusting and inappropriate things that the people are doing inside the city with the letter ‘X.’”
\p
\v 5 While I was listening, Yahweh said to the five other men, “Follow that man through the city and kill everyone he does not mark. Do not allow yourself to feel such compassion toward them that you restrain yourself from harming them.
\v 6 Anyone you come across—old people, young people, or children—kill them all! But remember! You must not harm anyone whose forehead has the letter ‘X’ on it. Now, begin at my temple.” So they began with the elders who were prostrating themselves in worship of the sun in front of Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 7 Then Yahweh said to the five men, “Ritually desecrate my temple by filling its two courtyards with the dead bodies of the people you kill! Now, move out!” So they went out and started killing people in the city.
\v 8 And so, as the six men went out killing, I remained there alone. I threw myself face down on the ground and exclaimed, “Oh, Lord Yahweh! Are you going to completely eradicate the Israelite people as you vent your anger against the people of Jerusalem? Those living in this city are all that remain of the Israelite people!”
\p
\v 9 He answered me, “The people of Israel and Judah have committed innumerable heinous injustices. Everywhere they live, these people behave violently, and this very city is brimming with corrupt and perverse people! After all, they speculate by saying, ‘Yahweh has left his home in the temple and vacated the land of Israel.’ They also reason with themselves, ‘Surely, Yahweh cannot see us.’
\v 10 Therefore, I myself also will not have such compassion for them that I restrain myself from justly punishing them. Instead, I will hold them accountable for their lifestyles.”
\p
\v 11 At that moment, the man who wore the linen clothing, who also had strapped the scribal kit to his waist, returned. He reported, “I have done exactly as you commanded.”
\c 10
\p
\v 1 Then, as I continued to look on, I suddenly noticed that a shape appeared above the vaulted, crystalline dome that arched high over the heads of the four cherubim. It looked like a throne made of a very valuable blue stone.
\v 2 Yahweh then spoke to the man who wore the linen clothing. He said, “Go to the spaces in between the whirling array of wheels that are under the cherubim. Take two handfuls of the live coals from the cherubim, and scatter them over the city of Jerusalem.” Then, as I watched, the man went.
\p
\v 3 Now, when the man went in to collect the live coals, the four winged cherubim were standing at attention on the south side of the temple, and a thick cloud suffused the temple’s inner courtyard.
\v 4 Then, Yahweh’s glorious presence ascended over the four cherubim and repositioned itself to the doorway of Yahweh’s temple. The thick cloud suffused the temple, and the brilliant light that came from Yahweh’s glorious presence saturated the temple’s interior courtyard.
\v 5 The noise that the cherubim’s wings generated when they advanced was so loud that people in the outer courtyard of the temple could hear it! It sounded like the speaking voice of the all-powerful God.
\p
\v 6 So, when Yahweh commanded the man who wore the linen clothing to procure live coals from among the whirling array of wheels, from among the winged cherubim, the man went and stood at attention beside one of the four wheels.
\v 7 Then the winged cherubim beside whose wheel the man stood reached out his hand, moving it toward the fire that burned among the four cherubim. He picked up some of the live coals and put them into the two open hands of the man who wore the linen clothing. The man took them and left.
\v 8 Now, it appeared that each cherubim possessed what resembled human hands underneath its four wings.
\p
\v 9 So I continued to watch. Once again, I noticed the four wheels that moved beside the winged cherubim. Each cherubim possessed a single wheel beside it. The wheels themselves appeared to glisten like the gleam of a fine topaz.
\v 10 As for their appearance, all four wheels were identical: Each one resembled a gyroscope, made of a single wheel intersecting another wheel at a right angle.
\v 11 Whenever they advanced, the wheels never moved in a circuitous manner. Instead, they always advanced straight forward in one of the four directions, corresponding to the direction that each of the faces of the four-faced cherubim looked.
\v 12 The entirety of each cherubim—their bodies, their backs, their hands, their wings, and all four wheels—was completely covered with eyes.
\v 13 I heard someone identify the cherubim’s wheels as “the whirling array of wheels.”
\v 14 Now, each winged creature possessed four faces—first, a cherub’s face; then a human face; third, a lion’s face; and fourth, an eagle’s face.
\p
\v 15 Then the four cherubim ascended. (These were the same creatures that I had previously seen beside the Kebar River.)
\v 16 When the winged cherubim advanced, their wheels advanced beside them. Similarly, when the winged cherubim extended their four wings in order to ascend from the ground—never turning about as they went—so also did their wheels remain beside them.
\v 17 Whenever the cherubim stopped moving, their wheels also stopped. And whenever the creatures ascended, so too did their wheels ascend alongside them. This was because the wheels contained the creatures’ spirit that gave them life.
\p
\v 18 Then, the brilliant light of Yahweh’s glorious presence advanced away from the doorway of Yahweh’s temple and halted above the winged cherubim.
\v 19 Before my very eyes, the winged cherubim extended their four wings and ascended from the ground—their wheels ascending beside them as they advanced. Then the procession of Yahweh and the four cherubim paused at the doorway of the eastern gated entrance to Yahweh’s temple, and the brilliant and glorious presence of the very God of Israel hovered above the winged cherubim.
\p
\v 20 (Now, these were the same creatures that I had seen advancing underneath the God of Israel beside the Kebar River. I recognized them as the angelic beings that people call cherubim.
\v 21 Yes, four—each of the cherubim possessed four faces and four wings, and underneath each of their wings, they possessed what resembled human hands.
\v 22 As for the appearance of their faces—they looked as they did when I saw them alongside the Kebar River. Each of them advanced forward in the direction of one of their four faces—either north, south, east, or west.)
\c 11
\p
\v 1 Then the wind of Yahweh’s spirit lifted me up and carried me to the eastern gated entrance of Yahweh’s temple. There, standing at the gate, I suddenly noticed 25 men. Among them, I recognized two public officials: Jaazaniah, the son of Azzur, and Pelatiah, the son of Benaiah.
\p
\v 2 Yahweh said to me, “Human, these are the men who have made thinking about doing corrupt things a habit and consistently give the citizens of Jerusalem immoral and disastrous advice.
\v 3 These men tell the people in their jurisdiction, ‘It is not the right moment to think about building houses. This city keeps us, its most honorable and divinely-favored officials, safe, just as a cooking pot conserves the choice meat that is inside it.’
\v 4 So, declare my prophetic pronouncements against them! Yes, prophesy human!”
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh’s Spirit empowered me, and Yahweh told me to declare these words: “Here is what Yahweh has to say, ‘Israelite people, you have been talking like this in public, but I, Yahweh, know exactly what you are thinking!
\v 6 You have killed innumerable people in this city. Dead bodies lie everywhere on every street in Jerusalem!’
\p
\v 7 Consequently, here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, ‘The corpses that you have strewn throughout this city are like the meat, and this city is like the cooking pot. But as for you—I will forcibly remove you from Jerusalem.
\v 8 You are afraid of dying in the violent conflict of war. So I will dispatch the violent conflict of war against you.’ That is what the Lord Yahweh declares!
\v 9 ‘I, Yahweh, will forcibly remove you from this city, Jerusalem, and consign you to the authority of foreign empires. So I will execute my legal judgments against you.
\v 10 Your armed enemies will kill you with their weapons. I will enforce my legal rulings against you within the tribal territories of the Israelite people. At that time, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 11 This city, Jerusalem, will not preserve you the way a cooking pot conserves meat within it. To the contrary, I will execute my just verdicts against you within the tribal territories of the Israelite people.
\v 12 At that time, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh! It is my instructions and my norms that you have ignored and refused to live by. Instead, you have chosen to live by the norms of the other nations around you!’”
\b
\p
\v 13 Then, as I was prophesying, Pelatiah son of Benaiah suddenly died. I threw myself down with my face to the ground and exclaimed with a shout, “O Lord Yahweh! You are going to completely eradicate the only Israelite people who remain alive!”
\b
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 15 “Human, the people who yet live in the city of Jerusalem are making claims about you and your relatives—your closest family—and about all the rest of the Israelite people whom the Babylonians forcibly removed from Israel. Those left in Jerusalem claim, ‘Let those exiles remain far away from Yahweh. After all, Yahweh has conferred this land to us as our property.’
\p
\v 16 Consequently, inform them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, “Yes, I resettled the exiles among the nations far away and strewed them among the neighboring empires. Nonetheless, wherever they now live, I have been with them. I will lovingly protect them, as if I were a sacred refuge for them.”’
\p
\v 17 Also inform them: ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, “When I bring all of you back from your exile among the non-Israelite peoples, and I regather you from the empires among which I scattered you, I will give you back the Israelite territory.
\p
\v 18 Then those whom the Babylonians forcibly removed from Israel will return to their homelands. They will deface and destroy every detestable ritual statue and every disgusting and indecent idol, entirely removing them from the Israelite territory.
\v 19 At that time, I will unify the entire Israelite people so that they feel, think, and live together in harmony. It will be as if they all have one mind! I will also revive and empower the Israelite people with a new desire to live as I want them to. It will be as if I have given them a new life! I will change the arrogant, stubborn, and ineffective ways they used to feel, think, and live. In their place, I will enable the Israelite people to deeply desire to feel, think, and live as I want them to. It will be as if I removed a dead, rocky heart from them and put a living, responsive heart in its place.
\v 20 This unified mind and renewed life that I will give to the Israelite people will enable them to carefully live their lives by practicing the norms and instructions that I disclosed to them. When this happens, you, Israelite people, will finally and truly live as my special people, and I myself will belong to you as the only God that you worship, love, and trust.
\v 21 Yet, those Israelite people who devote themselves to worshiping other gods by honoring their detestable ritual images and their disgusting and indecent idols—I will hold them accountable for their life choices.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.”
\p
\v 22 Then the winged cherubim raised their four wings, with their wheels beside them, while the brilliant light of the glorious presence of Israel’s God hovered above them.
\v 23 Then, Yahweh’s glorious presence rose from where it had been hovering in the middle of Jerusalem. It halted above the mountain on the eastern side of the city.
\v 24 At that moment, as the Spirit of God showed me what he wanted me to see, the wind of Yahweh’s Spirit raised me up and brought me back to the domains of the Babylonian empire, where the Israelite exiles were currently living.
\b
\q1 Then I stopped seeing the prophetic visions Yahweh had been giving me.
\v 25 Then I began to tell the Israelite exiles everything that Yahweh had shown me.
\c 12
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh spoke another message to me. He said,
\v 2 “Human, you live amidst the Israelite people, who reject my authority and openly defy me! They may have eyes that can see, but the people refuse to perceive their actions the way I do. They may have ears that can hear, but the people refuse to heed my messages to them. After all, these people reject my authority and openly defy me!
\p
\v 3 As for you, human—you must prepare a bag in which you would pack your belongings should an enemy army capture you and forcibly remove you from your homeland. During the daytime, you should act as though enemy armies were forcibly removing you from your home, and, as these people watch, you should travel to another place. Even though these people reject my authority and openly defy me, perhaps your symbolic actions will help them to finally see their actions the way that I do.
\v 4 When it is daytime, you should bring the bag you prepared out of your house while they watch, like an exile would if an enemy army forcibly removed him from his home. But then, as the people continue to watch, you should leave your home in the evening, just as an exile would.
\v 5 Next, as they watch, you must begin to dig a hole through the wall of your house and carry the bag you prepared out through the hole.
\v 6 While they continue to watch, you should hoist your bag onto your shoulder. Then, when it becomes twilight, carry it out through the hole you dug. As you come out of the hole in your wall, you must cover your face so that you are unable to see the land. I want you to perform these symbolic actions because I have made you into an object lesson that will teach the Israelite people.”
\p
\v 7 So I did exactly what Yahweh commanded me to do. In the daytime, I brought the bag that I prepared out of my house, just as an exile might do. Then, in the evening, I used my hands to dig myself a hole in the wall of my house. When it became twilight, I hoisted my bag onto my shoulder and, as the Israelite exiles watched, I carried it back out of my house through the hole in the wall.
\p
\v 8 The next morning, Yahweh spoke this message to me,
\v 9 “Human, as you performed those symbolic actions, the Israelite people—those who reject my authority and openly defy me—have inquired about what you are doing.
\p
\v 10 So, tell them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, “This heavy bag that I carry on my shoulder represents Zedekiah, the current illegitimate ruler of Jerusalem, and all the Israelite people who live in Jerusalem. They are a weighty burden.”’
\v 11 Tell them, ‘Let my symbolic actions teach you something that Yahweh wants you to understand. Notice what I have done. The same thing will happen to the citizens of Jerusalem: Their enemies will march them out of Jerusalem as exiles and prisoners.’
\p
\v 12 Yes, Zedekiah, the illegitimate ruler of the citizens of Jerusalem, will hoist a bag onto his shoulder when it becomes twilight, and he will leave the city. This will happen as the armies of Israel’s enemies dig a hole through the walls of Jerusalem, which they will create to carry Zedekiah out through it. As he departs, the false king will cover his face so that he does not see the place that he is leaving.
\v 13 However, I will trap and capture Zedekiah, as if I caught him in a hunting trap. Then, I will bring him to the city of Babylon, where the Babylonian people live (but he will never see that place, because the Babylonians will blind him). In that place, Zedekiah will die.
\v 14 I will disperse his entire retinue in every direction—his advisors and all his military troops. I will dispatch enemy soldiers with swords to hunt them.
\p
\v 15 Then, when I forcibly relocated them among the many nations and empires, they will all finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 16 Yet, I will allow a few of them to survive the violence of war, the fatal lack of food, and the wasting infectious diseases. As a result, when they settle among the nations where their enemies relocate them, they will report all the disgusting and indecent things that they once did. When this happens, the people who hear these reports will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”
\p
\v 17 Yahweh spoke another message to me. He said,
\v 18 “Human, you should tremble with fear while you eat your food, and shudder anxiously while you drink your water.
\v 19 Tell the people of the regions of Israel, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say to those who still live in Jerusalem about the land in Israel, “They will be anxious while they eat their food, and they will tremble in devastated horror while they drink their water. After all, Israel’s enemies will devastate the land by destroying every kind of crop that grows in Israel. This will happen because everyone who lives in the Israelite regions continues to harm one another violently.
\v 20 Israel’s enemies will raze Israel’s cities in which people live until they are nothing but ruins, and the land itself will become an empty, devastated wasteland. Then, those who remain alive in the Israelite regions will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”’”
\b
\p
\v 21 Yahweh spoke another message to me. He said,
\v 22 “Human, the people who remain alive in Israel keep repeating this saying about the Israelite regions: ‘The days go by, but nothing that the prophets declared in their divine visions ever happens.’
\v 23 So tell them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, “I am making this colloquial saying irrelevant and no longer true. The Israelite people will never again speak it.”’ However, also inform them, ‘Soon, everything that the prophets have declared in their divine visions will happen!
\v 24 From now on, no prophet among the Israelite people will declare deceitful visions that they fabricated, nor will they read divine messages from nature only to flatter people with positive messages.
\v 25 Surely I, Yahweh, still speak! And I will accomplish what I have spoken! It will happen exactly when I declare that it will. Yes, while you—you Israelite people who reject my authority and revolt against me—are still alive, I will accomplish what I have spoken. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’”
\b
\p
\v 26 Then Yahweh spoke another message to me. He said,
\v 27 “Human, pay attention! Notice how the Israelite people keep remarking, ‘The prophetic messages that this man, Ezekiel, sees in his divine visions concern events far into the future. Surely, he is proclaiming prophetic messages that only pertain to generations who live after us!’
\p
\v 28 So, tell them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say about that, “The things that I have spoken about will happen now, exactly when I declared that they will! Yes, I am about to accomplish what I have spoken.” This is what the Lord Yahweh declares!’”
\c 13
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, speak my prophetic messages against those Israelite prophets who speak authoritatively to the people. Some of them tell people things that they have contrived as if I said them! Tell them: ‘Listen to what Yahweh really says!
\v 3 Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say to you: How terrible it will be for those senseless prophets who authoritatively speak like my prophet but only heed their own ideas without having received any messages from me!
\v 4 Israelite people, the prophets who advise you and speak to you have become useless and predatory—like ravenous jackals that scavenge among the ruins of a city.
\v 5 Like masons who fail to repair the cracks in deteriorating defensive walls, you prophets have done nothing to protect your people! You have not prepared the Israelite people to survive the coming destruction that will occur when I, Yahweh, besiege and destroy Jerusalem!
\v 6 The messages that these prophets purport to have received from me are deceitful and fraudulent! Every divine insight they provide is nothing but a lie! Sure, they claim, “This is what Yahweh has declared.” Yet, I did not send them to you! Nevertheless, they fully expect that their contrived prophetic declarations will happen!
\v 7 Isn’t this true? You prophets purport to have received prophetic messages from me, but they are deceitful and fraudulent! You prophets claim to provide divine insight, but it is all lies! You prophets claim, “This is what Yahweh has declared,” but I never spoke to you!
\p
\v 8 So, here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say: You prophets have only spoken deceitfully and fraudulently! You have lied when you reported messages that you said came from me! Consequently, pay attention! I am now your enemy. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 9 I will work against you prophets who deceitfully and fraudulently claim to receive messages from me and who falsely claim to provide divine insight.
\q1 You will no longer be a part of the community of my people, Israel.
\q2 No one will record your names in the civil register of the Israelite people,
\q1 nor will you ever return to the territories of Israel.
\q2 When all this happens, you false prophets will finally understand
\q2 exactly who I am and what I am like—I am the Lord Yahweh!
\b
\p
\v 10 I am the enemy of these false prophets principally because they intentionally deceive my people, Israel, by proclaiming, “Everything is well!” when nothing is well! It is as though the Israelite officials have built a rickety wall without the proper mortar. Then, rather than expose the faults in the walls that the officials have poorly constructed, these false prophets daub the rickety wall with white plaster to make it look stable.
\v 11 Tell those prophets—the ones who, by falsely declaring that all is well, daub the Israelite official’s rickety wall with white plaster—that their wall will soon collapse! It will fall apart under the strain of the flooding rainstorms, the falling hailstones, and the violent windstorms that I, Yahweh, am about to unleash!
\v 12 When your wall collapses, people will surely ask you, false prophets, “What happened to the white plaster that you daubed on it?”
\p
\v 13 So here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say to you, false prophets: I am angrily unleashing violent windstorms, furiously unloading flooding rainstorms, and irately sending falling hailstones to obliterate that rickety wall.
\v 14 I will raze the wall that you daubed with plaster. I will level it to the ground so that everyone can see its foundation. It will collapse, and you will die alongside it. When all this happens, you false prophets will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 15 That is how I will vent my anger against the Israelite officials’ wall and against those false prophets who daubed it with white plaster. At that time, I will say to you, “Your wall is gone, and so are those who daubed it with plaster—
\v 16 those false Israelite prophets who proclaim authoritative prophetic messages about the citizens of Jerusalem and claim to receive divine declarations from me that all would be well for the city, when nothing will be well!” That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’
\p
\v 17 Now you human, turn toward the Israelite women who feign to speak authoritatively to the people. Some of these women tell people things that they have contrived as if I said them! Declare my prophetic messages against them.
\v 18 Tell them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say: How terrible it will be for you women who manufacture magic bands to fasten on people’s wrists, who craft magic talismans that you encourage people of all sizes to wear over their heads! You do these things not to help people but to ensnare and harm them! Yes, you attempt to ensnare my people, Israel, and harm them. But I will ensnare and harm you!
\v 19 You have debased me in my people’s perceptions—and all in exchange for a few handfuls of barley and a few scraps of bread! By lying to my people, who heed your lies, you have murdered people who should not have died, and you have rescued people who should not have lived.
\p
\v 20 So, here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say: Witness me! I am now an enemy to your magic bands, which you use to ensnare people as though they were birds. I will rip those magic bands off your forearms, and I will release the people whom you attempt to ensnare and harm like birds.
\v 21 I will also rip off your magic talismans and thereby deliver my people, Israel, from your control. No longer will you dominate and harm them, as though they were birds that you hunted for sport. Then, you false prophetesses will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 22 You have demoralized honest and integrous people by lying to them—even though I myself did nothing to cause them emotional pain! You have also encouraged dishonest and immoral people! As a result, these people did not stop their corrupt behavior—or else they would have stayed alive. Because of these actions,
\v 23 you will no longer claim to receive deceitful and fraudulent messages from me or provide divine insight to people! Instead, I will deliver my people, Israel, from your control. Then, when all this happens, you false prophetesses will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!’”
\c 14
\p
\v 1 One day, some of the elders of the Israelite people came to me and sat down in front of me.
\v 2 Then Yahweh gave me this message,
\v 3 “Human, these elderly men have devotedly committed themselves to their ritual statues. They have fixed their attention on those idols, which has caused them to behave immorally! Why are these men asking me what to do? They certainly have no right to ask me for advice!
\v 4 So, tell them, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say, “Any Israelite could devotedly commit himself to his ritual statues and fix his attention on those idols that cause him to behave immorally. But whenever such a person then comes to one of Yahweh’s prophets to request advice from me, I, Yahweh, will answer him myself—in such a way that his extensive worship of innumerable idols deserves!
\v 5 I will answer them in this way so that I may capture the devoted attention and affection of the Israelite people—all of whom have alienated themselves from me by worshiping other gods through their ritual statues.”’
\p
\v 6 Consequently, tell the Israelite people, ‘Here is what the Lord Yahweh has to say: “Repent! Stop worshiping your idols, and stop all your other disgusting and indecent practices!
\v 7 Any Israelite, or any foreigner living among the Israelite people, could ritually dedicate himself to another god and disavow me and then begin to commit himself devotedly to his ritual statues and fix his attention on those idols that cause him to behave immorally. But whenever such a person then comes to one of Yahweh’s prophets and asks him for my insight, I, Yahweh, will answer him personally.
\v 8 I will become that man’s enemy and oppose him. I will make what happens to him a warning to others and something that people talk about in their proverbs. I will make that person an outcast from the Israelite people. Then, when all this happens, my people, Israel, will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\p
\v 9 But a prophet may foolishly allow the deceitful idol-worshiper to lure him to speak authoritatively on my behalf. If this happens, then I, Yahweh, have lured that prophet into behaving foolishly. I will use my power to obliterate him and ostracize him from my people, the Israelites.
\v 10 I will hold accountable both the prophet and the person who asks him for my insight. Yes, I will consider them equally guilty.
\v 11 I will do this so that the Israelite people will remain devoted to me, no longer straying away from me. They will no longer rebel against me in such a way that makes them ritually unfit to worship me. They will be my people, and I will be their God.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.’”
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 13 “Human, suppose a group of people disloyally betray their promises to me and so act sinfully. Suppose I used my power against them, and I crippled their food supply on which they rely, and so caused the conditions for a fatal lack of food among them. Suppose I caused the deaths of many people and animals there.
\v 14 If that were to happen, even if the most virtuous people imaginable lived among those people—men like Noah, Daniel, and Job—those men’s virtuous actions could not abate my destruction against the people. Their virtuous actions would only save themselves. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 15 Or suppose I sent rampaging animals throughout the homeland of those people. Suppose the wild animals attacked and killed so many people that those who survived deserted the region, and no one traveled through it because of those animals.
\v 16 I, Yahweh, swear by myself that even if those three men lived in that region—even though they are the most virtuous people you can imagine—they would not be able to prevent the deaths of even their own sons or daughters. Only those three men would remain alive, while the entire region would become ruinous and desolate. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 17 Or suppose I dispatched the armed forces of an enemy empire against that region. Suppose I proclaimed, ‘May enemy soldiers bring their swords and wage war throughout the region!’ and then that they murdered both people and animals there.
\v 18 I, Yahweh, swear by myself that even if those three men lived in that region, they would not be able to prevent the deaths of even their own sons or daughters. Yes, those three men alone would remain alive. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 19 Or suppose I discharged a fatal infectious disease throughout that region and vented my anger against those people, so as to cause the deaths of many people and animals there.
\v 20 I, Yahweh, swear by myself that even if the most virtuous people imaginable lived in that region—men like Noah, Daniel, and Job—they would not be able to prevent the deaths of even their own sons or daughters. Their virtuous actions would only save themselves. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 21 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘It will be far worse for the citizens of Jerusalem! I will cause the deaths of both people and animals in Jerusalem when I unleash all four of my calamitous punishments: Armed soldiers will murder some with their swords, a fatal lack of food in the city will kill some, raging wild animals will kill others, and lethal infectious diseases will kill still others.
\v 22 But pay attention! Some of the citizens of Jerusalem will survive these disasters. Watch for them! I will take the survivors out of the city, both sons and daughters, and they will travel to you in exile in the Babylonian Empire. When you observe their lifestyles and how they have conducted themselves, you will feel relief about all the calamities that I unleashed against the city of Jerusalem.
\v 23 When you observe the lifestyles of those survivors and how they have conducted themselves, you will feel relieved. You will understand that everything that I did to Jerusalem was well-motivated.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, what do people do with the woody stock of grapevines—that is, with the vine branches that people find growing among forested trees?
\v 3 No one uses the woody stock from a grapevine to make anything useful. No one even makes a peg with it on which to hang things!
\v 4 Pay attention! They simply burn it as fuel for fires. The fire burns both ends and eventually destroys even its middle. It is good for nothing else!
\v 5 Think about it! Even when the grapevine branch was still in one piece, no one made anything useful with it. So, now that the fire has burned it up, certainly no one will use it to make anything useful!
\p
\v 6 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘The citizens of Jerusalem will suffer the same fate as the woody stock of the grapevine—the ones that people find growing among the forested trees, which they use as fuel for fires.
\v 7 I have become their enemy. Even though the people who currently live in Jerusalem have escaped destruction before, the coming disasters will annihilate them. When I act as their enemy, you who live in exile in the Babylonian Empire will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 8 I will transform the land into a desolate and ruinous wasteland—all because these people disloyally betrayed their promises to me.’ That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.”
\c 16
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, inform the citizens of Jerusalem of the disgusting and illicit things that they have done.
\v 3 Tell them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh has to say to you citizens of Jerusalem, “Imagine that this city and those who live in it were a woman. Her ancestry was Canaanite, from the regions where the Canaanites lived. Your father was ethnically Amorite, and your mother was ethnically Hittite.
\v 4 Imagine your birth story: On the day your mother gave birth to you, no one cut your umbilical cord; no one bathed you to ritually cleanse you, and no one rubbed you with salt or swaddled you in cloths.
\v 5 No one who saw you felt enough empathy for you to do any of those things for you or to spare you from your condition. Instead, on the day she gave birth to you, your mother despised you so much that she threw you out into an open field.
\p
\v 6 Then I came across you and witnessed you floundering in your own spilt blood. As you lay there, helpless in your blood, I said to you, ‘Live! Yes, live!’
\v 7 I caused you to flourish like a sprouting plant growing in a field. You grew tall and came to a marriageable age, wearing the finest jewelry. Your body matured, and your hair grew long. However, you were still completely naked.
\p
\v 8 Later, when I came across you, I looked at you again. I noticed that you were now old enough to marry. So I covered your naked body with the edge of my clothing to demonstrate that I would marry you and protect you. I made a solemn promise to you and entered into a marriage covenant with you, and you became my wife.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 9 “Then I bathed you, washed the blood off you, and rubbed olive oil onto your skin.
\v 10 Then I dressed you in colorful embroidered clothing and strapped leather sandals to your feet. I outfitted you in fine linen and clad you in silken cloth.
\v 11 I adorned you with fine jewelry: I fastened bracelets on your wrists and a chain necklace around your neck.
\v 12 I gave you a nose ring and earrings and placed a beautiful crown on your head.
\v 13 So you wore gold and silver jewelry, and you dressed in the finest linen clothing, silken cloth, and colorful embroidery. Your food comprised the finest flour, honey, and olive oil. So, you became exceedingly beautiful, and you became a queen.
\v 14 You gained a reputation for being so exceedingly beautiful that people in other nations heard about you. After all, the magnificent radiance that I, your husband, had given you made you perfectly beautiful.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 15 “Yet, your beauty made you self-assured. You used your international renown to act like a prostitute. You freely offered illicit sexual favors to every man you came across, and they enjoyed your beautiful body.
\v 16 You used some of your beautiful clothing to make yourself gaudy, decorated shrines. On them, you behaved like a prostitute. Yes, you had illicit sex with men, and they enjoyed your beautiful body.
\v 17 You also used the beautiful gold and silver jewelry that I had given you to fashion ritual statues with male anatomy, and you had illicit sexual relations with them.
\v 18 You took your embroidered clothes and clothed your ritual statues with them, and you placed my oil and my incense as votive offerings in their presence.
\v 19 You also took the food that I gave you— the fine flour, olive oil, and honey that I fed you—and you presented it in the presence of those ritual statues as pleasingly aromatic votive offerings. That is exactly what happened.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 20 “You even took your sons and your daughters, the children that you had borne to me, and you sacrificed them as food to those idols. Was your prostitution such a small matter to you,
\v 21 that you also butchered my children and burned them alive as offerings to those ritual statues?
\v 22 All the while, when you were committing these disgusting, heinous actions and prostituting yourself, you never once remembered your birth story, when you were completely naked, floundering in your own blood!
\p
\v 23 How terrible it will be for you!” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares! “So, after you did all those heinous acts,
\v 24 you constructed a domed building for yourself and erected lofty shrines in every public square.
\v 25 At every street intersection, you constructed your lofty shrine. So you made your once-beautiful body abominable, offering yourself to every man you came across. So, more and more, you behaved like a prostitute.
\v 26 Then you even had illicit sex with the Egyptians, your well-endowed neighbors, and so you acted more and more like a prostitute, never ceasing to anger and grieve me.
\v 27 So I acted powerfully against you and reduced your apportioned rations. So I let the Philistine people do what they wanted with you. They despised you, and yet your obscene behavior embarrassed them!
\v 28 You also had illicit sexual relations with the Assyrian people because you were insatiable. You behaved like a prostitute with them, and yet even then you could not satisfy your desires!
\v 29 So, you behaved more and more like a prostitute, reaching all the way to the territories of the Babylonian Empire! Yet even that did not satisfy your desires!
\p
\v 30 How weak-willed you are! You love the wrong things!” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares. “You did all these things, acting like an impudent prostitute who does whatever she wants.
\v 31 When you constructed your domed brothel at every street intersection and erected your lofty shrines in every public square, you were not even like an ordinary prostitute, because you refused to accept payment.
\p
\v 32 You are an unfaithful wife, who prefers strangers to her own husband!
\v 33 Prostitutes usually receive gifts from men, but you gave your gifts to all your lovers; you bribed them to come to you from all around to have illicit sexual relations with you!
\v 34 So as you behaved like a prostitute, you were the opposite of other women. No man solicited you; instead, you paid them, and they did not pay you. In this way, you were the exact opposite of a normal prostitute!”
\p
\v 35 So then, you prostitute, heed Yahweh’s message!
\v 36 This is what the Lord Yahweh says, “You lustfully offered yourself and exposed your naked body in your illicit sexual relations with your lovers. Furthermore, you crafted your disgusting and indecent ritual statues, and you offered those idols the blood of your own children!
\v 37 Because of this, I am summoning all your lovers, the ones you found so attractive—everyone you loved, along with everyone you hated. I will assemble them against you from all around, and I will strip you naked in front of them so that they will see you completely naked.
\v 38 I will execute against you the legal sentence reserved for women who commit adultery and women who murder. I will sentence you to an angry and impassioned, bloody death.
\v 39 I will let your enemies dominate you: They will demolish your brothels and smash your lofty places. They will strip off your clothes and take your beautiful jewelry, and they will leave you completely naked.
\v 40 They will bring a mob against you. Then, they will stone you to death and cut your body into pieces with their swords.
\v 41 They will burn down your houses and execute their judgments against you while many women watch. I will put an end to your prostituting behavior, and you will no longer pay your lovers.
\v 42 Then I will vent my fury against you, and my jealous passion will leave you alone. I will calm myself down and cease being angry.
\p
\v 43 Because you did not remember your birth story, but instead you infuriated me with all these deeds, I hereby declare that I will hold you accountable for your behavior. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare! You have added this obscene behavior to all your other disgusting and indecent practices!
\p
\v 44 Now, pay attention! Everyone who quotes proverbs will quote this proverb about you: ‘The daughter is just like her mother.’
\v 45 You are truly your mother’s daughter. She abhorred her husband and her children. You are also just like your sisters. They abhorred their husbands and their children. After all, your mother was ethnically Hittite, and your father was ethnically Amorite!
\v 46 Now, your older sister is named Samaria. She resides with her daughters to the north of you, citizens of Jerusalem. Your younger sister is named Sodom. She resides with her daughters to the south of you.
\v 47 You did not merely practice their lifestyles or do the disgusting things they did. Instead, in a surprisingly short while, in all your habits and practices, you became even more corrupt than they ever were!”
\v 48 This is what the Lord Yahweh declares: “I swear by myself that your sister Sodom and her daughters never did what you, Jerusalem, and your daughters have done.
\p
\v 49 Take note! This is how corrupt your sister Sodom was: She and her daughters were arrogant; they had plenty of food and lived comfortably at ease, but they never helped those who were poor or needy.
\v 50 They were arrogant and did disgusting and illicit things in my presence. So, when I saw what they were doing, I forcibly removed them from my presence.
\v 51 As for Samaria, she did not commit even half the sins that you committed. You have done so many more disgusting and illicit things than either Samaria or Sodom did, so that your abominable acts have made your sisters appear righteous!
\v 52 So now you must feel the emotional disgrace of having interceded for your sisters. Your sins, which were far more disgusting and illicit than theirs, have made your sisters appear more righteous than you! So you also must be ashamed and feel the disgrace of having made your sisters, Sodom and Samaria, appear righteous.
\p
\v 53 Nevertheless, I will restore the well-being of your sisters—that of Sodom and her daughters, and of Samaria and her daughters—and I will restore your well-bring along with theirs,
\v 54 so that you will feel humiliated and ashamed of all that you did when your disgusting behavior consoled your sisters.
\v 55 I will restore all of you—your sisters, Sodom and her daughters, Samaria and her daughters, and you and your daughters as well—to the kind of life you enjoyed before you suffered my punishments.
\v 56 When you were arrogant, you would not even mention your sister Sodom.
\v 57 But that was before I exposed your own corrupt and depraved behavior. Now, you are just like your sister Sodom! The people of Aram and all her neighbors, and the Philistines all around you, mock you and despise you!
\v 58 You yourself have suffered the consequences of your obscene behavior and your disgusting, illicit practices.” That is what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 59 So, this is what the Lord Yahweh says, “When you abrogated the covenant you made with me, you showed no respect for your solemn promises. I will deal with you as these actions deserve.
\v 60 Yet I myself will uphold and fulfill the solemn promise that I made with you when you were young. Yes, I will make a promise with you that will never end.
\v 61 Then you will recall what you once did and feel humiliating shame when you reconcile with your sisters, both those older than you and those younger than you. I will present them to you as your daughters, but not because of my covenant with you.
\v 62 So I myself will make my solemn promise with you. When this happens, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!
\v 63 I will do this so that you will recognize all that you have done and feel ashamed. When I forgive you for all that you have done and restore our relationship, your disgrace will cause you to never again open your mouth to speak.” This is what the Lord Yahweh declares!’”
\c 17
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, tell this ambiguous story to the Israelite people.
\v 3 Tell them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says,
\q1 “There once was a huge eagle with powerful wings and long feathers of many colors.
\q1 It flew to Lebanon and grasped the top of a cedar tree.
\q1 \v 4 It broke off the topmost of its young branches
\q1 and carried it away to the Canaanite territories.
\q1 The eagle set it down in a city of vendors.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Then it took some of the seed of that land
\q1 and planted it in a fertile field,
\q1 in a slip beside abundant water.
\q1 He planted it there like one plants a willow tree.
\q1 \v 6 That seed sprouted
\q1 and became a low, spreading vine.
\q1 Its tendrils grew toward the eagle,
\q1 and its roots extended down beneath it.
\q1 So it became a vine that produced woody branches
\q1 and put out canes.
\b
\q1 \v 7 There was another huge eagle with powerful wings and many feathers.
\q1 Now, the spreading vine bent its roots toward this second eagle.
\q1 From the garden bed where it grew,
\q1 the vine stretched out its tendrils toward the eagle,
\q1 hoping that it would give the vine more water.
\q1 \v 8 The first eagle had planted the vine-plant in a fertile field
\q1 that ran along flowing water
\q1 so that it could grow woody canes
\q1 and produce fruit
\q1 and become a splendid vine.”’
\b
\v 9 Then say, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says,
\q1 “This vine will clearly not flourish!
\q1 Someone will rip up its roots
\q1 and strip off its fruit,
\q1 so that it wilts and dies.
\q1 All its fresh new leaves will wilt and die.
\q1 (And it will not take a strong arm
\q1 or many people to pull it up by its roots!)
\q1 \v 10 Now, consider this:
\q1 Even though the eagle planted it,
\q1 it will clearly not flourish!
\q1 When the hot east wind blows on it,
\q1 it will entirely wither and die.
\q1 Yes, it will wither away,
\q1 right in the garden bed where it once grew.”’”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh gave me this message:
\v 12 “Ask those Israelite people who continually revolt against me, ‘You do not understand what this ambiguous story means, do you?’ Tell them, ‘Remember: The Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar came to Jerusalem, captured the Judahite king Jehoiachin and his public officials, and brought them back to himself in Babylon.
\v 13 Then he took one of the royal family—Jehoiachin’s uncle, Zedekiah—and made a royal treaty with him, coercing him to swear a solemn oath. He also forcibly removed the most powerful local rulers of Jerusalem,
\v 14 so that the Judahite kingdom would remain weak and unable to revolt against him. Instead, it could survive only by honoring its treaty with the Babylonian king, Nebuchadnezzar.
\v 15 However, the man whom the Babylonian king set up, Zedekiah, revolted against Nebuchadnezzar and the Babylonian Empire. Zedekiah dispatched his messengers to Egypt, to Pharoah Hophra, so that the Egyptian Empire would equip him with horses and many soldiers. Clearly, Zedekiah will not flourish! He who does such things will certainly not survive! Zedekiah disregarded the treaty he made with the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar, so he will clearly not survive!
\p
\v 16 I swear by myself that Zedekiah will die in Babylon, in the city of the king who made him king, whose oath he disregarded and whose treaty he violated. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare!
\v 17 Hophra, the king of Egypt, will not employ his massive army to fight alongside Zedekiah in the battle when the Babylonian Empire constructs a siege ramp and erects siege walls to kill many people.
\v 18 Zedekiah, the so-called king of Judah, showed no respect for his oath to Nebuchadnezzar and broke the treaty he had made. Make note: He made a solemn promise, yet he did all these things. So, he will clearly not survive!
\p
\v 19 So, this is what the Lord Yahweh says: “I swear by myself that the oath to Nebuchadnezzar that Zedekiah disregarded, he swore in my name! And the covenant he violated was with me! I will repay him for these actions!
\v 20 I will ensnare Zedekiah and capture him, like an animal that a hunter traps with a net. I will forcibly bring him to Babylon, and there I will legally arraign him for disloyally betraying his promises to me.
\v 21 With their swords, Babylonian soldiers will murder every one of Zedekiah’s soldiers who flees in the battle, and those who survive will disperse in every direction. When all this happens, you will recognize that I, Yahweh, have spoken!”
\v 22 This is what the Lord Yahweh says: “I myself will remove a shoot from the top of a tall cedar tree, and I will plant it. I will break off a newly grown, green twig from the topmost of its young shoots, and I myself will plant it on a towering mountain.
\v 23 I will plant that newly grown green twig on the highest mountain of Israel, where it will grow woody branches and bear fruit and become a magnificent, wide cedar tree. Birds of every kind will nest under it; every kind of winged animal will live in the shade of its branches.
\v 24 Then every tree in the field will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh! I fell tall trees and cause small trees to grow tall; I dry up green trees and make dry trees flourish. I {am} Yahweh—I have spoken, and I will certainly do it!”’”
\c 18
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “What do you people mean when you quote this proverb about the land of Israel? You say,
\q1 ‘The parents ate sour grapes,
\q2 but it was their children who got the sour taste in their mouths.’
\p
\v 3 I swear by myself that you, Israelite people, will no longer quote that proverb in Israel. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare!
\v 4 Pay attention! All lives are mine—parents and their children alike. It is the person who sins who will die.
\p
\q1 \v 5 Suppose there is a virtuous person
\q1 who does what is just and honest.
\q1 \v 6 He does not eat meat that people have sacrificed to ritual statues at the shrines on Israel’s hilltops,
\q2 nor does he pray to the idols that the Israelite people worship.
\q1 He does not defile another man’s
\q2 wife, nor does he have sexual relations with a woman during her monthly period of menstruation.
\q1 \v 7 He does not extort anyone.
\q2 When he borrows money from someone and the person gives him something as collateral for repayment,
\q2 he gives that item back to the lender.
\q1 He does not steal from anyone.
\q2 Instead, he gives his food to hungry people
\q2 and gives his clothing to people who have no clothes.
\q1 \v 8 When he lends someone money,
\q2 he does not charge interest
\q2 or engage in usury.
\q1 He refuses to do what is immoral or corrupt.
\q2 Instead, he judges honestly between one person and another.
\q1 \v 9 He carefully acts according to my regulations
\q2 and lives faithfully by heeding my norms.
\q1 In this case, people should consider this person righteous.
\q2 He will surely continue to live!
\q2 That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 10 But suppose that such a virtuous man has a violent son, who murders people. Then that son does any of these evil things to other people—
\v 11 all the things that his father never did:
\q1 He eats meat that people have sacrificed to ritual statues at the shrines on Israel’s hilltops;
\q2 he defiles another man’s wife.
\q1 \v 12 He extorts people who are poor and helpless.
\q2 He steals from people,
\q1 and, when he borrows money from someone and the person gives him something as collateral for repayment,
\q2 he refuses to return that item to the lender.
\q1 He prays to the idols that the Israelite people worship
\q2 and commits other disgusting and illicit things.
\q1 \v 13 When he lends someone money, he charges
\q2 interest and engages in usury.
\q1 Such a person will surely not go on living!
\q2 Because he has done all these disgusting and illicit things,
\q1 people must execute him
\q2 and hold him responsible for his own death.
\p
\v 14 But pay attention! Suppose that such a violent man has a son who witnesses all the sins that his father commits, considers them, but does not do such things.
\b
\q1 \v 15 He does not eat meat that people have sacrificed to ritual statues at the shrines on Israel’s hilltops,
\q2 nor does he pray to the idols that the Israelite people worship.
\q2 He does not defile another man’s wife;
\q1 \v 16 He does not extort anyone.
\q2 When he borrows money from someone and the person gives him something as collateral for repayment,
\q2 he gives that item back to the lender.
\q1 He does not steal from anyone.
\q2 Instead, he gives his food to hungry people
\q2 and gives his clothing to people who have no clothes.
\q1 \v 17 He refuses to do what is immoral or corrupt.
\q2 When he lends someone money, he does
\q2 not charge interest or engage in usury.
\q1 He lives in a way that heeds my norms
\q2 and carefully acts according to my regulations.
\q1 In this case, such a person will not die because of his father’s sins;
\q2 he will surely go on living!
\b
\v 18 But as for his father, pay attention! He will die because of his own depraved behavior. After all, he extorted people, stole from his relatives, and generally did what is wrong among his people.
\p
\v 19 But you retort, ‘Why would the virtuous son not suffer for the wrong that his father did?’ I answer: The son has done what is just and honest and has carefully lived his life according to all my regulations. For this reason, he will surely go on living!
\v 20 The person who sins is the one who will die. A son will not suffer what his father’s corrupt actions deserve, nor will a father suffer what his son’s corrupt actions deserve. Righteous people will enjoy the consequences of their righteous behavior, and wicked people will suffer the inevitable outcomes of their wicked behavior.
\p
\v 21 But suppose that a wicked person stops doing all the sinful things that he once did and begins to carefully live his life according to all my regulations and do what is just and honest. If this happens, this once-wicked person will surely go on living! I will not put him to death.
\v 22 I, Yahweh, will not treat him according to any of the rebellious things that he once did. Instead, he will go on living because of the virtuous things that he now does.
\v 23 Indeed, I do not enjoy it when wicked people die. I do not want them to die! That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare. So, when they stop behaving wickedly, they will go on living.
\v 24 But suppose a virtuous person stops doing what is just and honest and begins to behave corruptly and to do the same disgusting things that wicked people do. That formerly virtuous person will certainly not go on living! I will not credit him for the just and honest things that he once did. Instead, he will die because he has disloyally betrayed his promises to me and has committed many sins.
\p
\v 25 But you retort, ‘The Lord’s conduct is not fair!’ Listen to this, you Israelite people: It is not what I do that is unfair; rather, it is what you do that is unfair!
\v 26 If a righteous person stops doing what is right and honest and commits sins, he will die because of the sins that he committed.
\v 27 But if a wicked person stops doing wicked things and does what is just and honest, he will save his own life.
\v 28 Because he witnessed all his sinful actions and stopped revolting against me, he will surely go on living; he will not die.
\v 29 But you people of Israel say, ‘The Lord does not act fairly.’ You people of Israel, it is not my ways that are unfair; rather, it is your ways that are unfair.
\p
\v 30 Therefore, Israelite people, I, Yahweh, will judge each one of you according to what you have done. Repent, and stop all your rebellious behavior, so that your sins will not cause you to die! This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare!
\v 31 Stop revolting against me. Remove your rebellious behavior as if it were garbage to throw away! Change the way you think and the way you live. Israelite people, there is no reason for you to die!
\v 32 I do not enjoy it when any person must die! So repent so that you might live! This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare!”
\c 19
\p
\v 1 Yahweh said to me, “Lament over the ruling officials of Israel.
\v 2 Say this to the Israelite people:
\q1 ‘Imagine—your mother was such a lioness;
\q2 she lay herself among male lions
\q2 and bore more and more cubs surrounded by young, virile lions.
\q1 \v 3 She reared one of her cubs until he was a virile lion,
\q2 teaching him to hunt and kill wild animals;
\q2 he even learned to kill and eat people!
\q1 \v 4 When people from other nations learned about him,
\q2 they captured him in a pit.
\q1 They put hooks on him
\q2 and dragged him away to Egypt.
\q1 \v 5 The young lion’s mother waited for him to return,
\q2 but she was utterly disappointed when he never came back.
\q1 So she reared another one of her cubs,
\q2 and made him into another virile lion.
\q1 \v 6 He prowled among the other lions,
\q2 and he too became a virile lion.
\q2 He learned to hunt and kill wild animals,
\q2 and he even killed and ate people!
\q1 \v 7 He consorted with his widows,
\q2 and he razed cities into ruins.
\q1 When he roared,
\q2 everyone who lived in the land ran away.
\q1 \v 8 But then people from the surrounding nations
\q2 arrayed themselves against from every province.
\q2 They spread their net over him;
\q1 they trapped him in a pit.
\q1 \v 9 They shut him in a cage,
\q2 They put hooks on him
\q2 and dragged him away to the king of Babylon.
\q1 They locked him in a prison
\q2 so that the people on the mountains of Israel
\q2 would never again hear him roar.
\q1 \v 10 Imagine—your mother was like a flourishing, verdant vine
\q2 that somebody planted beside flowing water.
\q1 Because there was plenty of water,
\q2 she had many canes and produced many grapes.
\q1 \v 11 It had strong, woody branches,
\q2 suitable to become a ruler’s scepter.
\q1 It towered high overhead,
\q2 into the very clouds,
\q1 and people could see how tall it was
\q2 and how full it had become.
\q1 \v 12 However, Yahweh became so angry that he pulled the vine up by its roots
\q2 and flung it down to the ground.
\q1 A hot east wind dried up its fruit,
\q2 The vine’s strong branches broke off;
\q1 they dried up,
\q2 and a fire burned them up.
\q1 \v 13 Now, Yahweh has transplanted that vine—your mother—
\q2 in a desert, in a dry and arid region.
\q1 \v 14 A fire has spread out from the stem of its shoot,
\q2 which has burned up all its fruit.
\q1 So now, no strong branch remains on it—
\q2 no branch that could become a ruler’s scepter.’
\p This is a lament, and people will indeed sing it to grieve for the Israelite people.”
\c 20
\p
\v 1 In the seventh year after the Babylonians forcibly removed King Jehoiachin and the many other citizens of Jerusalem and relocated them to Babylon—on the tenth day of the fifth month—some of the Israelite elders came to inquire of Yahweh what they should do. So, they sat down in front of me.
\v 2 Then Yahweh gave me this message. He said,
\v 3 “Human, speak to the Israelite elders and tell them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says: “So you have come to inquire of me what you should do! I swear by myself that I refuse to let you ask me anything.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.’
\p
\v 4 Human, you must announce my judgment against those elders! Yes, you must pronounce my judgment! Make them acknowledge the disgusting and illicit things that their ancestors did.
\v 5 Tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘When I chose your ancestors to be my people, I solemnly swore an oath to the descendents of Jacob. While they lived as slaves in Egypt, I demonstrated to them who I am. I solemnly swore to them that my name was Yahweh, and that I was their God.
\v 6 At that same time, I solemnly promised them that I would bring them out of the Egyptian territories into a land that I had explored and selected for them. The soil in that land was very fertile and produced good crops abundantly—it was the most beautiful of all lands.
\v 7 I said to them, “Every one of you must discard the abhorrent idols that attract your attention and devotion, and you must not make yourselves ritually unfit to worship me by revering the ritual statues that the Egyptians worship! I am Yahweh your God!”
\v 8 But they revolted against me and refused to obey me. Not one of them discarded the abhorrent idols that attracted their attention and devotion, nor did they desert the ritual statues that the Egyptians worshiped. So I declared that I would completely vent my furious anger against them—right there in the Egyptian territories.
\v 9 But I did not do this, because I wanted to preserve my reputation. I did not want the citizens of the nations among whom my people were living to think little of me. They had witnessed me disclose myself to my people when I brought them out of the Egyptian territories.
\p
\v 10 So I brought them out of the Egyptian territories and led them into the desert wilderness.
\v 11 I told them how they ought to live, and I taught them my norms. Anyone who heeds these things will live because of them!
\v 12 I also gave them the day of my Sabbath rest. I intended those days to remind them of exactly who I am and what I am like. I am Yahweh, who sets them apart to belong to me.
\v 13 But the Israelite people revolted against me in the wilderness. They ignored my instructions and refused to live by my norms, even though anyone who heeds these things will live because of them! They also completely dishonored my Sabbath days. So I declared that I would completely destroy my people by venting my furious anger against them in the desert wilderness.
\v 14 But I did not do this because I wanted to preserve my reputation. I did not want the citizens of the nations who witnessed it when I brought Israel out of Egypt to think little of me.
\v 15 I also solemnly swore to them in the desert wilderness that I would not bring them into the land that I had given them. The soil in that land was very fertile and produced good crops abundantly—it was the most beautiful of all lands.
\v 16 I swore this because they ignored my instructions, refused to live by my norms, and dishonored my Sabbath days. After all, they deeply desired to worship their ritual statues.
\v 17 But I had compassion on them and did not kill every Israelite person; I did not completely destroy them in the desert wilderness.
\p
\v 18 I said to the children of the Israelite people who had died in the wilderness, “Do not live by the instructions that your parents followed; do not heed their norms, and do not make yourselves ritually unable to worship me by honoring your parents’ ritual statues.
\v 19 Remember: I am Yahweh your God. Live according to my instructions; carefully heed my norms and do them!
\v 20 Honor my Sabbath days as holy, so that they remind you of exactly who I am and what I am like. I am Yahweh, your God!”
\v 21 But their children also revolted against me. They ignored my instructions and refused to live by my norms, even though anyone who heeds these things will live because of them! They also completely dishonored my Sabbath days. So I declared that I would completely vent my furious anger against them in the desert wilderness.
\v 22 But I held back from punishing them and did not carry out my intent to destroy them, because I wanted to preserve my reputation. I did not want the citizens of the nations who witnessed it when I brought Israel out of Egypt to think little of me.
\v 23 I also solemnly swore to them in the desert wilderness that I would scatter them among other nations and territories.
\v 24 I did this because the Israelite people ignored my instructions, refused to live by my norms, completely dishonored my Sabbath days, and focused their devoted attention on the gods that their ancestors worshiped by honoring ritual statues.
\v 25 So I permitted them to follow instructions that were not good for them and norms by which they could not preserve their lives.
\v 26 I let them defile themselves by the sacrifices they offered to other gods when they ritually burned their firstborn children alive. I did that in order to devastate them, so that they would know exactly who I am and what I am like. I am Yahweh!”’
\p
\v 27 Therefore human, tell the people of Israel, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says: “Here is yet another way that your ancestors insulted me! They disloyally betrayed their promises to me.
\v 28 I led them into the land that I had solemnly promised to give to them. But whenever they saw a hill or a verdant tree, they offered their sacrifices to other gods there. At those places, they presented offerings to the other gods that angered me, burning aromatics that gave off a pleasing smell and pouring out their wine offerings.
\v 29 So I asked them, ‘What is this high place that you keep going to?’” (To this day, people call such places Bamah, a name that means ‘high place.’)’
\p
\v 30 Therefore, tell the people of Israel, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says: “You are making yourselves ritually unfit to worship me in the same way that your ancestors did! Likewise, you are being unfaithful to me by worshiping the detestable idols that they worshiped!
\v 31 When you worshiped your other gods, presenting your ritual gifts and burning your children alive as sacrifices to the other gods, you made yourselves unfit to worship me! And you are still doing these things today! So I will certainly not let you ask me for advice, you people of Israel. I swear by myself that I refuse to let you ask me anything.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\v 32 “What you are thinking will never happen. You say to yourselves, ‘We want to become like the other nations, like the empires of other lands, who worship ritual statues that they have crafted from wood and stone.’”
\p
\v 33 This is what the Lord Yahweh declares: “I swear by myself that I will rule over you by my great power and authority. I will show you how furiously angry I am!
\v 34 By my great power and authority, I will lead you out from among the foreign nations and collect you from the territories where I scattered you. And I will show you how furiously angry I am!
\v 35 I will lead you into a desert wilderness that lies among the other nations, and there, I will personally arraign you.
\v 36 I will legally arraign you just as I did with your ancestors in the desert wilderness near the land of Egypt.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares!
\v 37 “I will count each one of you, like sheep passing under a shepherd’s staff, and I will require you to honor the covenant that I made with you.
\v 38 I will expel from among you those who revolt against me and disobey me. I will lead them out of the territories where they have been living as foreigners, but they will never enter the land of Israel. Then you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”
\v 39 As for you, people of Israel, this is what the Lord Yahweh says: “If you refuse to listen to me, then go ahead and worship your idols, every one of you! But you will never again dishonor my holy name by offering your gifts to your idols.
\p
\v 40 Because this is what the Lord Yahweh declares: “On my holy mountain, Zion, Israel’s lofty mountain, all the people of Israel, every one of them, will worship me there in the Israelite territories. There, I will accept them, and there I will require them to bring me their ritual contributions, the firstfruits of their tributes, along with all their holy sacrifices and offerings.
\v 41 When I smell your aromatic sacrifices, I will reconcile myself with you. Then I will lead you out from among the foreign nations and gather you from the territories among which I scattered you. Then the nations will witness what I do among you and will recognize that I am holy.
\v 42 You will finally understand who I am and what I am like when I bring you into the land of Israel, the land that I solemnly swore to give to your ancestors.
\v 43 In that land, you will remember how you formerly behaved and everything you did that made you ritually unfit to worship me. As a result, you will despise yourselves for all the corrupt, wicked things that you did.
\v 44 You will understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!—when I act mercifully toward you to protect my reputation, and not according to what your wicked and corrupt behavior deserves, you people of Israel.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.’”
\p
\v 45 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 46 “Human mortal, turn and face toward the south. Prophesy an ecstatic message concerning the southern region, and tell the Israelite people my prophetic messages concerning the scrub-forest land in the Negev.
\v 47 Tell them about that forested part of the Negev, ‘Listen to the message that Yahweh is sending you! He says, “Pay attention! I, the Lord Yahweh, am about to start a fire in you, and it will ignite every tree in the Negev, both the verdant trees and the dry ones. No one will put out that blazing fire, and it will scorch every part of the land—from the south to the north.
\v 48 Then everyone alive will see that I, Yahweh, started that fire. No one will ever put it out.”’”
\v 49 Then I said, “Oh, Lord Yahweh! People are dismissing me by saying, ‘He is only speaking in figures of speech!’”
\c 21
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, turn and face toward Jerusalem. Speak out an ecstactic prophetic message against the temple there, and declare what I have to say about the people who live in the land of Israel.
\v 3 Tell the people of Israel, ‘This is what Yahweh has to say: “Pay attention! I have become your enemy! I am about to send a warring army against you, and it will kill both the righteous people and the wicked people among you.
\v 4 Because I am going to kill both righteous people and wicked people among you, I will send that army against everyone who lives in the land of Israel—from its southernmost parts to its far north.
\v 5 Then everyone will understand that it was I, Yahweh, who sent that army and that I will not call it back until it has finished.’”
\p
\v 6 So then, as for you, human—exclaim an audible groan of your deep sorrow! Groan in anguish and bitter grief while the people of Israel are watching you.
\v 7 They will ask you, ‘Why are you groaning?’ And you must answer, ‘I am groaning because of the news that I have heard about what is about to happen. When other people learn of it, all of them will lose their courage; they will become dismayed and terrified, and they will urinate themselves. Pay attention! A disaster is coming, and it will certainly happen. That is what the Lord Yahweh declares!’”
\p
\v 8 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 9 “Human, declare my message! Tell the people that this is what I, the Lord, say:
\q1 ‘I see a sword! A sword!
\q2 Someone has sharpened it and polished it.
\q1 \v 10 He sharpened it in order to slay many people;
\q2 he polished it so that it gleams brightly, as if it were lightning!
\q1 We have no reason to celebrate!
\q2 This sword will cut down every wooden rod, even the royal scepter of my son Judah.
\q1 \v 11 The sword’s owner polished it
\q2 so that a warrior could grip the sword in his hand.
\q1 He sharpened and polished it,
\q2 in order to give it to the man who will use it to kill.
\q1 \v 12 Human, mourn and loudly weep!
\q2 After all, this sword will attack my people and all the local officials of Israel!
\q1 I have decided that my people and their leaders will die by that sword.
\q2 So slap your hand against your thigh to show how horrified you are.
\q1 \v 13 I am putting my people to the test,
\q2 and the sword will destroy the royal power of Judah, which shuns my warnings.
\q2 That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’
\b
\q1 \v 14 So then you human, speak my messages,
\q2 and clap your hands together to show that I have decided to punish them.
\q1 Let that sword strike them once more—
\q2 even a third time!
\q1 That sword kills people;
\q2 it is a sword that slaughters great numbers of them,
\q2 closing in on them from every side.
\q1 \v 15 I am doing this so that they will lose their courage
\q2 and so that many of them will fall over and die.
\q1 At every city gate,
\q2 I have stationed soldiers with pointed blades.
\q1 How terrible this is! Someone made that sword to flash about as it strikes;
\q2 the soldiers will pull out their swords to massacre people with them.
\q1 \v 16 Go on, soldiers! Slash your swords to the right!
\q2 Again! Slash them to the left!
\q2 Thrust your swords wherever your sword’s edge happens to go!
\q1 \v 17 Even I myself will clap my hands to announce that I have finished judging them,
\q2 and then I will no longer be angry.
\q1 This will assuredly happen because I, Yahweh, have said it would!”
\p
\v 18 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 19 “As for you, human, draw a map on which you have mapped out two routes for the army of the king of Babylon to march along. Both routes must start out from the same territory. Then, fashion a signpost and place it at the place where each route turns toward a major city.
\v 20 Map out a route for that army to travel to the city of Rabbah, where the Ammonite people live, and map out another route for it to travel to Judah, to the city of Jerusalem, which possesses fortified walls.
\v 21 The king of Babylon will stop and stand at attention at the place where the road divides, where the two roads begin. There, he will ask the gods to provide him with insight as to which way he should go. He will shake marked arrows and see which one falls out; he will ask his household idols what to do; and he will examine the liver of an animal that he has killed for messages from the gods.
\v 22 He will receive insight that suggests he should go to Jerusalem and attack the city. It tells him to assemble battering rams to knock down its outer gates. It tells him to give the order to kill the people. It tells his soldiers to shout the battle cry. It tells them to array the battering rams against the city gates, and to construct a siege ramp that allows soldiers to climb over the outer walls. It tells them to build siege walls around the city.
\v 23 The citizens of Jerusalem will think that the insight he receives from his gods means nothing. After all, they had sworn oaths of loyalty to him. But the king of Babylon will remind them of the violations of their oath that they have committed, and his soldiers will capture them.
\p
\v 24 So then, tell the people of Israel that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘The king of Babylon has reminded everyone how guilty you are. You have openly rebelled, and everything that you do shows how sinfully you have lived. Since he has reminded everyone of this, your enemies will forcibly capture you.’
\p
\v 25 As for you, you evil and ungodly ruler of Israel—the moment has arrived during which Yahweh will finally punish your sinful behavior!
\v 26 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Take off your royal turban! Remove your crown! Things must change! Consider the important unimportant, and the unimportant important.
\v 27 I will utterly destroy the city of Jerusalem! Jerusalem will remain in ruins until the one comes who has the right to rule it; then I will deliver the city over to him.’
\p
\v 28 As for you, human, declare my messages and tell the Israelite people that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say about the people of Ammon and about how they mocked my people. Tell them, ‘Soldiers have drawn their swords to slaughter the Ammonite people; they have polished their swords in order to destroy people, so that they brightly flash back and forth as they kill with them.
\v 29 Although your priests and prophets saw deceitful visions about you and gave you false insight from the gods, those swords will kill you just like any evil and ungodly person. The moment has arrived when Yahweh will finally punish your sinful behavior!
\v 30 Soldiers, put your swords back into their sheaths! I will judge you in the very place where I created you, in the land from whence you came.
\v 31 I will show you how indignant I am with you. I am so extremely angry that I will punish you, and I will deliver you to bloodthirsty, brutal men who are experts at destroying people.
\v 32 Fire will destroy you like kindling, and you will die violently there in your own land. No one will ever remember you again. After all, I, Yahweh, have said this will happen!’”
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “As for you, human—you must pronounce my legal verdict against the city of Jerusalem. It is a city in which people murder and oppress other people! Inform the citizens of the city about all the despicable and indecent things that they have done.
\v 3 You must tell them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says: ‘You citizens of Jerusalem make people who live in the city bleed by murdering and oppressing them! By doing that, you ensured that it is now the proper moment for me to punish you. You have crafted idols for yourselves that harm you, and by doing that you have made yourselves unfit to worship me.
\v 4 When you murdered and oppressed people, you made yourselves guilty. When you crafted your idols, you became unfit to worship me. Because of your actions, the moment when I will punish you will happen very soon. You will die soon! That is why I have caused the people of other nations to insult you and the people in every territory to laugh at you.
\v 5 Both people who reside near you and far away from you will mock you. Your reputation is disgraceful, and your city is full of dismayed panic.
\p
\v 6 Pay attention! Notice how every one of the Israelite local officials who lives in Jerusalem uses his own power to murder and oppress people!
\v 7 These local officials living in Jerusalem treat their fathers and mothers with contempt. They oppress the foreigners who live among them, and they extort orphans and widows.
\v 8 Your local officials treat the things that the priests have set apart for service to me as if they were worthless! They dishonor my Sabbath days!
\v 9 These men who live among you in Jerusalem spread false rumors about others in order to oppress and harm them. These people eat sacrificial food that they have offered to ritual statues at the shrines on Israel’s hilltops. They have done obscene things right in front of you, citizens of Jerusalem!
\v 10 Israel’s local officials who live in Jerusalem have had sexual relations with their fathers’ wives. They have raped and abused women during their monthly menstruation.
\v 11 One man despicably has sexual relations with his neighbor’s wife. Another man commits ritually impure obscenities with his daughter-in-law. Still another man in Jerusalem rapes and abuses his own half-sister, his father’s daughter!
\v 12 Some of these local officials in Jerusalem accept illicit payments to oppress and harm people. They charge excessive interest when they lend money, and they profit by extorting their fellow Israelites. They have even forgotten all about me.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 13 Now, pay attention! I have clapped my hands together to show how grieved I am about the profit that these local officials have extorted and about the people whom they have murdered among you, citizens of Jerusalem.
\v 14 You will not be so brave, nor will you remain calm when I deal with you. I, Yahweh, have said this, and I will certainly do it.
\v 15 I will forcibly disperse you among many nations in many different territories, and I will make you once again fit to worship me.
\v 16 The people of other nations will see you bring disgrace upon yourselves. When that happens, you will finally understand exactly who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”’”
\p
\v 17 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 18 “Human, I consider the Israelite people to be like slag. All of them are like the bronze, tin, iron, and lead that people throw into a furnace in order to purify silver. They have become like the slag that remains after metalworkers purify silver.
\v 19 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘All of you are like slag. So, pay attention to what I am about to do! I am going to gather all of you together inside Jerusalem.
\v 20 People gather metals like silver, bronze, iron, lead, and tin into a smelter in order to melt them by the blast of the smelter’s heat. In the same way, in my fierce anger against you, I will gather you together inside Jerusalem. I will deposit you there, and you will suffer things that will feel as though I am melting you.
\v 21 Yes, I will gather you together, and because I am furiously angry with you, it will be as though I am blasting you with a hot fire. You will feel as though I am melting you inside that city.
\v 22 Just as a metalworker melts silver in his smelter, so I will melt you inside that city. At that time, you will understand that I, Yahweh, have vented my fierce anger against you.’”
\p
\v 23 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 24 “Human, tell the Israelite people, ‘You live in a land that remains in need of ritual cleansing. As long as I am indignantly angry with you, there will be no rain.
\v 25 The prophets in the city of Jerusalem are conspiring! They murder people, acting just like vicious, prowling lions. Moreover, they seize people’s money and their valuables, and they murder so many men that many women in Jerusalem have become widows.
\v 26 The priests in Jerusalem abuse my laws! They disrespect and disregard the spaces and items that belong to me, Yahweh. They do not make any distinction between holy spaces or things and ordinary, common ones, nor do they teach people the difference between what is ritually impure and what is ritually pure! They deliberately ignore my Sabbath days. By doing all these things, they publicly dishonor me among the citizens of Jerusalem.
\v 27 Jerusalem’s local officials savagely harm the people who live in the city, as though they were vicious wolves. They harm people and even kill them in order to use violence to make a profit for themselves.
\v 28 Your prophets intentionally deceive the people. They claim that I, Yahweh, give them visions, but their visions never happen! They tell lies when they claim to possess divine insight into a situation. They say, “This is what the Lord Yahweh says,” even though I never spoke to them!
\v 29 Everyone else in Israel extorts one another and robs them. They violently harm people who are poor and destitute, and they unjustly treat the foreigners who live among them.
\v 30 I searched among all the Israelite people for someone who would defend the land and plead with me on its behalf, as if they were repairing a broken wall. If I found that person, I would not destroy the territories of Israel. However, I did not find anyone like that.
\v 31 So I have vented my indignant anger against them. Because I was furiously mad, I have destroyed them. I have made them suffer the consequences that their behavior deserves. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’”
\c 23
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, {listen to this story.} There once were two sisters.
\v 3 While they lived in Egypt, they engaged in prostitution. Even though they were young, they acted like prostitutes. Their lovers fondled their breasts there in Egypt and caressed their virgin nipples.
\v 4 The older sister’s name was Oholah, and her sister’s name was Oholibah. I married them, and they gave birth to sons and daughters. Oholah represents the city of Samaria, and Oholibah represents the city of Jerusalem.
\v 5 However, Oholah acted like a prostitute while she was still my wife. She desired other lovers, including the Assyrians. They were warriors
\v 6 who wore extravagant blue clothing. They were governors and commanders—all of them handsome young men who rode horses.
\v 7 She acted like a prostitute with all of them, the most desirable men of Assyria. She also worshiped all the ritual statues of the men she so desired, and so she made herself unacceptable to me.
\v 8 She never ceased acting like a prostitute in Egypt, where men had previously had sexual relations with her when she was young. They caressed her virgin nipples and freely satisfied their own promiscuous desires with her.
\v 9 So I let her lovers, the Assyrians, whom she had desired so much, dominate her.
\v 10 Her Assyrian lovers stripped her naked and publicly shamed her. They apprehended her children and murdered her with a sword. Her lovers executed such judgment on her that women everywhere talked about her.
\p
\v 11 Her sister Oholibah witnessed all of this. Nonetheless, her illicit desires grew even greater than her sister Oholah’s. She acted even more like a prostitute than her sister had!
\v 12 She desired the Assyrian men—those governors, commanders, and warriors who wore opulent uniforms and rode on horses, all of them handsome young men.
\v 13 I observed that she had made herself unacceptable to me; both sisters behaved in the same way!
\v 14 Oholibah increasingly acted like a prostitute. She saw depictions of men that someone had carved onto a wall—engravings of Babylonian men that an artist had outlined in bright red.
\v 15 The engravings depicted men wearing belts around their waists and flowing turbans on their heads. They all looked like Babylonian officers native to Chaldea.
\v 16 As soon as she saw them, she desired those men. So she sent messengers to them in Chaldea.
\v 17 The Babylonian men came to her and had sexual relations with her. Their promiscuity made her unacceptable to me. After she had done this, she became disgusted with them and rejected them.
\v 18 She openly flaunted her promiscuous behavior and shamelessly exhibited her naked body. So, I became disgusted with her and rejected her, just as I had become disgusted with and rejected her sister.
\v 19 Yet she increasingly acted like a prostitute, recalling the days when she was young, when she acted like a prostitute in Egypt.
\v 20 There, she desired her Egyptian lovers, men whose genitalia were like those of donkeys and whose seminal emissions were like those of horses.
\v 21 So you, Oholibah, longed to behave as obscenely as you did when you were young—when the Egyptians caressed your nipples and fondled your young breasts.
\p
\v 22 For this reason, Oholibah, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Pay attention to what I am about to do! I am about to incite your lovers to attack you—the very men you became disgusted with and turned away from. I will bring against you all of those who surround you:
\v 23 the Babylonians and all the people of Chaldea, along with the men of Pekod and Shoa and Koa, and all the Assyrians with them. They are all handsome young men—governors and commanders, chariot officers and famous men, all of them riding on horses.
\v 24 They will attack you with their weapons, riding chariots and wagons, and with a huge army. They will surround you, arraying large infantry shields and smaller shields against you and wearing their war helmets. I will let them decide what you deserve, and they will treat you according to their own customs.
\v 25 I will act toward you in my zealous love for you. As a result, your lovers will treat you cruelly. They will cut off your noses and your ears, and those of you who survive will die by the sword. Your lovers will take away your children, and fire will destroy those of you who yet survive.
\v 26 They will strip off your clothing and take away your beautiful jewelry.
\v 27 So I will put an end to your obscene behavior and to the promiscuous ways that you began in Egypt. You will no longer expect help from the Egyptians or any of your other lovers, nor will you think about Egypt anymore.’
\p
\v 28 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Pay attention to what I am about to do! I am about to let the people you hate dominate you, the ones you became disgusted with and turned away from.
\v 29 They will treat you hatefully and take away everything that you worked hard to gain. They will leave you completely naked, so that everyone will see plainly how promiscuous and obscene you have behaved.
\v 30 They will treat you this way because this is what your promiscuous behavior toward the nations deserves! You made yourself unacceptable to me by worshiping their idols.
\v 31 You have behaved just as your sister behaved! As a result, I will make you suffer the same punishment that she suffered.’
\p
\v 32 This is what the Lord Yahweh has to say: ‘You will suffer just as your sister suffered—as if I forced you to drink from a deep and wide cup. Your cup will contain so much that people will laugh at you and deride you.
\v 33 You will suffer so much, drinking deeply from this cup, that your sorrow will overcome you as if it made you drunk. You will suffer the horrifying punishment that your sister Samaria suffered when I destroyed her land.
\v 34 You will experience all of your punishment, as though I forced you to drink your cup down to the very last of it and eat the broken pieces of the cup. You will suffer such agony that you will tear at your own body. I, Yahweh, have said this!’ That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 35 So this is what the Lord Yahweh says: ‘You have forgotten about me and completely rejected me! As a result, you yourself must suffer the consequences that your obscene and promiscuous behavior deserves.’”
\p
\v 36 Yahweh gave me another message. He said, “Human, you must pronounce my verdict against Oholah and Oholibah. Tell them about the disgusting and illict things that they have done.
\v 37 This is because they have committed adultery, and they are guilty of murdering people. They have been unfaithful to me by worshiping the ritual statues of other gods. They have even sacrificed the children that they bore for me by burning them alive and offering them to their idols as food.
\v 38 They have even done this to me: On that same day, they desecrated my temple, and they dishonored my Sabbath days.
\v 39 On the very same day that they butchered their children as sacrifices to their idols, they entered into my temple so that they could dishonor it! Moreover—pay attention!—they did this right inside my temple.
\p
\v 40 They even dispatched messengers to invite people who lived far away—and, look!—they came! For them, you bathed yourself, applied your makeup, and put on your jewelry.
\v 41 You sat on a splendid couch. In front of it, someone had set a table on which you placed the incense and the olive oil that belonged to me.
\v 42 A noisy, carefree crowd surrounded her. Along with the ordinary men in that crowd, people brought in drunken men from the desert wilderness. The two sisters, Oholah and Oholibah, put bracelets on their wrists and beautiful crowns on their heads.
\v 43 Then I said about that woman, who had exhausted herself with her many adulteries, ‘Now one of these men will treat her as a prostitute—even her!’
\v 44 Then one man had sexual relations with her as men do with a prostitute. That is how they treated both Oholah and Oholibah, those women who do obscene things.
\v 45 However, virtuous men will condemn Oholah and Oholibah. They will sentence them the way people sentence women who commit adultery and women who murder people. After all, these women both have committed adultery and are guilty of murder.
\p
\v 46 This is what the Lord Yahweh says: ‘I will incite a mob against them, and I will let that mob terrify them and ransack their possessions.
\v 47 That mob will publicly execute them by throwing stones at them and slashing them with their swords. They will kill their children and burn down their houses.
\v 48 In this way, I will put an end to obscene behavior in the territories of Israel. Your punishment will warn all the other women, so that they will not behave as obscenely as you have behaved.
\v 49 You, people of Israel, will suffer the consequences of your obscene behavior; you will accept the punishment for the sins that you committed by worshiping idols. At that time, you will know exactly who I am and what I am like—I am the Lord Yahweh!’”
\c 24
\p
\v 1 In the ninth year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the tenth day of the tenth month, Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, write down today’s date, the exact date. Do this because, on this very day, the king of Babylon has besieged the city of Jerusalem with his army.
\v 3 Quote a proverb to these rebellious people. You must tell them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says:
\q1 “Put a cooking pot on the fire; do it!
\q2 Pour water into it also.
\q1 \v 4 Put pieces of meat into the pot,
\q2 all the best pieces—
\q1 the thigh and the shoulder.
\q2 Fill up the pot with the best bones!
\q1 \v 5 Take the best sheep from the flock,
\q2 and pile logs under the pot for fuel.
\q1 Boil the meat thoroughly,
\q2 until you have simmered the bones in the pot as well.”
\p
\v 6 So then, this is what the Lord Yahweh says:
\q1 “How terrible it will be for Jerusalem, the city
\q2 where people murder one another!
\q1 That city is like the proverbial pot,
\q2 whose rust stays inside it and never comes off.
\q1 Take the pieces of meat out of the pot one piece at a time;
\q2 do not throw lots to decide which pieces to take out.
\b
\q1 \v 7 Do this because the blood of the people whom
\q2 the citizens of Jerusalem murdered,
\q2 remains inside the city.
\q1 They spilled that blood on bare rock;
\q2 they did not even show respect for it by pouring it out on the ground
\q2 and covering it over with dirt.
\q1 \v 8 I will spill her own blood on the bare rock,
\q2 where no one will cover it up in respect.
\q1 I will do this so that I can rouse my anger
\q2 and redress the citizens of Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 9 So this is what the Lord Yahweh says:
\q1 “How terrible it will be for Jerusalem, that city
\q2 in which people murder one another!
\q1 I myself will increase my punishment against it,
\q2 as though I were piling the firewood high under that pot.
\q1 \v 10 Pile on more wood and light the fire!
\q2 Cook the meat until it is done;
\q1 Stir in the appropriate mixture of spices,
\q2 and char the bones for the stock.
\q1 \v 11 Then set the empty pot on the coals.
\q2 Do this so that it becomes very hot and its bronze glows,
\q1 Let the fire melt away everything that is dirty inside it
\q2 and destroy all its rust.
\q1 \v 12 That city, the rusty pot, has become worn
\q2 from so many efforts to clean it,
\q1 yet its thick rust still will not come off.
\q2 Consign it to the fire!
\m
\v 13 Citizens of Jerusalem, the dirty rust in that pot represents the obscene things that you have done. I have tried to purify you by ceasing such behavior among you, but you refused to stop. As a result, I will purify you only once I have finished punishing you in my anger.
\v 14 I, Yahweh, have spoken. These things will happen soon, and I will certainly do what I have said. I will not hold back; I will not pity you, nor will I choose to do something else. I will hold you accountable for the way you have lived and for the things that you have done.’ That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.”
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 16 Human, pay attention to what I am about to do. I am about to take your wife away from you—she who brings you such delight. She will die because of an infectious disease. However, you must not mourn for her or weep, and you must not show your sorrow.
\v 17 Moan in your grief, but do so quietly. Do not wail out loudly for your wife. Instead, keep your funerary headdress wrapped on your head and your sandals strapped on your feet, and do not cover the lower part of your face, as grieving people do. Do not eat the food that friends bring to those who are mourning.”
\v 18 Then, one morning I spoke to the people, and that evening my wife died. The next morning, I did what Yahweh told me to do.
\v 19 Then the people said to me, “Tell us what these things mean for us, the things that you are doing.”
\v 20 So I said to them, “Yahweh gave me this message.
\v 21 He told me to tell you, the people of Israel, ‘This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: “Pay attention to what I am about to do! You are so confidently proud of my temple in Jerusalem; it brings you delight to look at, and you cherish it deeply. Regardless, I am about to allow enemies to destroy and defile it. While that happens, your children, whom you left behind in Jerusalem, will die in the conflict.
\v 22 Then you will do exactly what Ezekiel has just done: you will not cover the lower part of your faces, as grieving people do, nor will you eat the food that friends bring to those who are mourning.
\v 23 You will keep your funerary headdresses wrapped on your heads and your sandals strapped on your feet. You will not wail loudly in your grief or weep. Instead, the wrongs that you have committed will cause you to experience life as if you were something that someone has left out to rot. As a result, each one of you will groan to one another in your grief.
\v 24 Ezekiel will serve as an object lesson to you. You will do everything that you have seen him do. When all this happens, then you will finally understand who I am and what I am like—I am the Lord Yahweh!”’
\p
\v 25-26 {Yahweh also said to me}, ‘As for you, human, I will soon take my temple away from these people—the building that made them feel safe and strong, the beautiful building that made them rejoice, in which they delighted to look at and longed for. I will take it away along with their own children. When that happens, someone will escape from Jerusalem and will come to you in Babylonia and tell you what has happened there.
\v 27 When this all happens, I will enable you to speak once again. You will converse with the person who has escaped from Jerusalem, and you will no longer remain silent. In this way, you will serve as an object lesson to your people, and then they will finally understand who I am and what I am like—I am Yahweh!”
\c 25
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, turn toward the land where the people of Ammon live and prophesy against them.
\v 3 Tell them that they must listen to my message. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘You shouted in triumph when the Babylonians defiled my temple, when they ruined the land of Israel, and when they took the people of Judah away to Babylonia.
\v 4 So I am about to let the peoples who live in the desert east of you conquer you. They will set up their tents in your territory and live in your land. They will eat the fruit that grows in your land, and they will drink the milk from your herds.
\v 5 I will turn your capital city, Rabbah, into a pasture for camels, and I will turn the rest of the land of Ammon into a resting place for sheep. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.’
\p
\v 6 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘You clapped your hands and stamped your feet to celebrate, and you gloated because you utterly despised the land of Israel and everyone who lived there.
\v 7 So I am about to use my power to punish you. I will let other nations carry you off as plunder. I will destroy you completely, so that you no longer exist as a people in any land. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.’
\p
\v 8 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘The people of Moab and the people of Seir said that the people of Judah were no different from all the other nations.
\v 9 So I am about to leave Moab’s frontier defenseless. The cities that guard its border, the finest cities in that land: Beth Jeshimoth, Baal Meon, and Kiriathaim,
\v 10 I will give to the peoples who live in the desert to the east. They will possess it along with the land of the people of Ammon. I will do this so that the nations will no longer remember that the people of Ammon ever existed.
\v 11 I will also punish the people of Moab. Then they will know that I am Yahweh.’
\p
\v 12 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘The people of Edom hurt the people of Judah because of what Judah did in the past. Because they hurt them so cruelly for what they did, the people of Edom sinned greatly.’
\v 13 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘I will use my power to punish the people of Edom. I will destroy both the people and the animals that live there. I will ruin their land completely, from Teman all the way to Dedan, and their enemies will kill them with swords.
\v 14 I will use my people Israel to punish the people of Edom. My people will treat Edom in a way that shows how extremely angry I am. Then the people of Edom will know that I am the one punishing them.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say.
\p
\v 15 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘The Philistines attacked the people of Judah because of what Judah did in the past. Because they utterly despised my people, they tried to destroy them completely because of what Judah did in the past, for they had hated my people for a very long time.’
\v 16 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Because they did that I am about to use my power to punish the Philistines. I will destroy the Cherethites, and I will kill everyone who still lives along the seacoast.
\v 17 I will punish them again and again because I am very angry about what they did in the past. When I punish them for what they did, they will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\c 26
\p
\v 1 In the eleventh year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the first day of the month, Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, the people of Tyre gloated over the city of Jerusalem. They said, ‘Aha! Jerusalem was the city that controlled the trade of all the peoples, but now enemies have destroyed her! The trade that used to pass through her has turned toward us instead. Now that Jerusalem lies in ruins, we will grow rich!’
\v 3 Therefore this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Listen, Tyre! I am now your enemy. I will cause many nations to attack you continuously, one after another.
\v 4 Their armies will destroy the walls of Tyre and tear down her towers. I will scrape away even the soil of that city until nothing remains there but bare rock.
\v 5 The sea will surround the place where the city was and become a place where fishermen do nothing but spread out their nets {to dry}. I myself have said this. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare. The soldiers of those nations will carry off everything valuable that she owns.
\v 6 Enemy soldiers will attack the villages on the mainland that depend on Tyre, and they will kill the people who live there in battle. Then the people of Tyre will know that I am Yahweh.’
\p
\v 7 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘I am about to bring King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon to attack Tyre. He is the greatest king of all, and he will march against you from the north with horses, chariots, horsemen, and a huge army of soldiers.
\v 8 He will kill the people who live in your villages on the mainland. He will build a wall of earth all around your city to shut everyone inside, he will pile up a ramp of earth against your city wall so that his soldiers can climb up it, and he will set up large shields to protect his soldiers as they attack.
\v 9 He will bring up heavy beams and pound them against your walls to smash them, and he will tear down your towers with iron tools.
\v 10 He will have so many horses that when they charge in, the dust that their hooves stir up will settle over your whole city. Your walls will shake from the thundering noise of his horsemen and his wagons and his chariots when he rides in through your gates after his soldiers have broken through your walls.
\v 11 His horses will trample every one of your streets with their hooves. His soldiers will kill your people with their swords, and the sacred stone pillars that you were so proud of will crash to the ground.
\v 12 His soldiers will carry off your wealth and the goods that your merchants sell. They will break down your walls and demolish the beautiful houses that you love. Then they will throw your stones and your timbers and your rubble into the sea.
\v 13 I will put an end to the noise of your singing, and no one will hear anyone playing your harps ever again.
\v 14 I will make you nothing but bare rock, a place where fishermen spread out their nets. No one will ever rebuild you, because I, Yahweh, have spoken.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 15 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say to Tyre: ‘When you fall, the people who live along the coasts will certainly tremble, because they will hear your dying people groaning and your enemies slaughtering people all through your city.
\v 16 Then all the rulers of {the nations along} the sea will step down from their thrones. They will take off their royal robes and strip off their embroidered clothes {to show that they are mourning}. Instead they will tremble with fear. They will sit on the ground, shuddering over and over, and they will shake with horror at what has happened to you.
\v 17 Then they will sing a funeral song about you. They will sing,
\q1 “How completely you have perished, you city where seafarers lived!
\q2 Everyone praised you, and you were powerful out on the sea, you and the people who lived in you.
\q2 You terrified everyone who lived along the coast!
\q1 \v 18 Now, on the day when you fall, the people along the coasts tremble;
\q2 the people on the islands in the sea shake with terror because you are gone.”’
\p
\v 19 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘I will make your city a ruin, like the cities where no one lives anymore. I will overwhelm you completely with disaster, as though the deep sea had risen up and its vast waters had covered you.
\v 20 Then I will send you down to join the people who died long ago and went down into the grave. I will make you stay in the world far below the earth, among places that have lain in ruins since ancient times, together with everyone else who has gone down into the grave. You will never again have a place among living people. However, I will give glory to the land where living people dwell.
\v 21 I will bring you to a horrifying end, and you will exist no longer. People will search for you, but they will never find you again.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\c 27
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 2 “As for you, human, sing a funeral song about the city of Tyre.
\v 3 Say this to Tyre, the city that is where ships come in from the sea, the city full of people that trade with peoples who live along many coasts. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say:
\q1 ‘People of Tyre, you yourself boasted that you were perfectly beautiful.
\q1 \v 4 Your territory lies out in the middle of the sea;
\q2 the ones who built you made you completely beautiful.
\q1 \v 5 You were like a magnificent ship. Your builders made all your planks out of cypress trees from Senir;
\q2 they took a cedar tree from Lebanon to make your mast.
\q1 \v 6 They made your oars out of oak trees from Bashan;
\q2 they made your deck out of pine wood from the coasts of Kittim, and they set ivory into it.
\q1 \v 7 They made your sail out of fine embroidered linen from Egypt, so that people far away could tell that it was you;
\q2 they made your awning out of blue and purple cloth from the coasts of Elishah.
\q1 \v 8 Men from Sidon and Arvad rowed you;
\q2 your own skilled men, Tyre, were on board,
\q2 and they sailed you.
\q1 \v 9 The elders of Gebal and that city’s skilled workers were on board to seal up your leaking seams.
\q2 Sailors from every ship that sailed the sea came to you to trade for your goods.
\q1 \v 10 Men from Persia, Lud, and Put served as soldiers in your army.
\q2 They hung their shields, helmets{, and all their other armor} inside your city;
\q2 they are the ones who made you look magnificent.
\q1 \v 11 Men from Arvad and your own soldiers stood guard on your walls all around, and men from Gammad guarded your towers.
\q2 They hung their shields all around on your walls;
\q2 they are the ones who made you completely beautiful.
\p
\v 12 Merchants from Tarshish traded with you because you had such an enormous amount of goods. They gave you silver, iron, tin, and lead in exchange for your wares.
\v 13 Merchants from Javan, Tubal, and Meshech traded with you. They gave you slaves and bronze objects in exchange for your goods.
\v 14 Men from Beth Togarmah gave you workhorses, warhorses, and mules in exchange for your wares.
\v 15 Men from Dedan traded with you, and you controlled the trade of the people who lived along many coasts. Those people brought you ivory tusks and ebony{, a valuable black wood,} as payment.
\v 16 Merchants from Aram traded with you because you produced so many things. They gave you emeralds, purple cloth, embroidered cloth, fine linen, coral, and rubies in exchange for your wares.
\v 17 Men from Judah and from the land of Israel traded with you. They gave you wheat from Minnith, a food called pannag, honey, olive oil, and balm in exchange for your goods.
\v 18 Merchants from Damascus traded with you because you produced so many things and had such great wealth. They brought you wine from Helbon and white wool.
\v 19 Men from Vedan and from Javan near Uzal also traded with you. They brought wrought iron and the spices cassia and sweet cane.
\v 20 Merchants from Dedan traded with you. They brought saddle blankets that people put on horses before riding them.
\v 21 Merchants from Arabia and all the rulers of Kedar traded with you. They gave you lambs and rams and male goats in exchange for your goods.
\v 22 Merchants from Sheba and Raamah traded with you. They gave you the finest of all the spices, all kinds of precious stones, and gold in exchange for your wares.
\v 23 Merchants from Haran, Kanneh, Eden, Sheba, Assyria, and Kilmad also traded with you.
\v 24 In your marketplace those merchants sold you fine clothing, cloaks of blue cloth and embroidered cloth, and multicolored rugs that they had rolled up and tied tightly with cords.
\v 25 Ships from Tarshish carried your goods from place to place.
\q1 People loaded you full of cargo,
\q2 and you rode very low in the water far out in the middle of the sea.
\q1 \v 26 Your rowers took you out into deep water,
\q2 but a wind from the east will break you apart far out in the middle of the sea.
\q1 \v 27 On the day when you sink, all of these will go down into the middle of the sea:
\q2 your wealth, your wares, and your merchandise,
\q2 your sailors and your pilots and the men who repair your leaking seams,
\q1 the merchants who trade your goods, all your soldiers who are on board,
\q2 and the whole crowd of people who are in you.
\q1 \v 28 When your pilots cry out,
\q2 the people who live along the shore will tremble with fear.
\q1 \v 29 All the men who row will come down from their ships;
\q2 the sailors and all the pilots who sail the sea
\q2 will stand on the shore.
\q1 \v 30 They will cry out loudly about you and weep bitterly;
\q2 they will throw dust on their heads
\q2 and roll in ashes {to show how deeply they grieve}.
\q1 \v 31 They will shave their heads completely bald and put on rough sackcloth, to show that they are mourning for you;
\q2 they will weep for you because they feel terrible sorrow, and they will grieve for you very deeply.
\q1 \v 32 As they wail, they will sing a funeral song for you. They will sing,
\q1 “No city was ever like Tyre;
\q2 now she {has perished, and she} lies silent in the middle of the sea.”
\q1 \v 33 When your goods went out across the seas,
\q2 you gave many peoples everything that they wanted.
\q1 With your great wealth and all your merchandise
\q2 you made kings all over the earth rich.
\q1 \v 34 Now you will break apart far out at sea and sink into deep water;
\q2 all your merchandise and the whole crowd of people in you will go down with you.
\q1 \v 35 All the people who live along the coasts shake with horror at what has happened to you;
\q2 their kings shudder violently,
\q2 and their faces show how terrified they are.
\q1 \v 36 Merchants among the nations hiss at you to show their scorn and horror.
\q2 What has happened to you will horrify people everywhere, and you will never exist again.’”
\c 28
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, say to the ruler of the city of Tyre that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘You have become very proud, and you have boasted that you are a god and that you sit on a god’s throne out in the middle of the sea. Yet you are only a human being; you are not a god, even though you think that you are as wise as a god.
\v 3 You think that you are wiser than Daniel was and that no one can keep any secret hidden from you.
\v 4 Because you are so wise and understand so much, you have made yourself wealthy; you have acquired gold and silver and stored them in your treasuries.
\v 5 You have traded very skillfully because you are so wise, and so you have become even wealthier. Now, because you are so wealthy, you have become very proud.’
\p
\v 6 Therefore, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘Because you think that you are as wise as a god,
\v 7 I am about to bring foreigners to attack you, soldiers from the nations that are the most ruthless of all. They will destroy the beautiful things that you created by being so wise with their swords, and they will make everything that made you look magnificent ugly.
\v 8 They will bring you down to the grave. You will die violently, the way soldiers die in battle, there in your city out in the middle of the sea.
\v 9 When the men who are about to kill you come to you, you will certainly not claim that you are a god. You are only a human being, not a god, and the men who stab you will have you completely in their power.
\v 10 Foreigners will kill you, and you will die a shameful death like people who do not belong to God. This will surely happen because I myself have said it.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\p
\v 11 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 12 “Human, sing a funeral song about the king of the city of Tyre. Say to him that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘You were perfect in every way. You were completely wise and perfectly handsome.
\v 13 It was as though you lived in Eden, the garden of God. You wore precious stones of every kind: ruby, topaz, and diamond, beryl, onyx, and jasper, sapphire, emerald, and garnet. Craftsmen mounted those stones in settings of gold. I prepared all of them for you on the day when I created you.
\v 14 I anointed you and appointed you as a winged creature who guards. I placed you on my own holy mountain, and you walked about there among the glowing stones.
\v 15 Everything that you did was perfect from the day when I created you, until you began to do wicked things.
\v 16 Because you traded on such a huge scale, your merchants acted violently everywhere in your city, and you sinned. So I treated you as something unclean and drove you away from my mountain. You winged creature who guards, I destroyed you and removed you from among the glowing stones.
\v 17 You became very proud because you were so handsome. Because you loved your own splendor, you ruined the wisdom that you had. So I brought you down and disgraced you completely; I put you on display in front of other kings, and they all stared at you.
\v 18 Because you sinned so many times and traded so dishonestly, you made your own temples unholy. So I caused a fire to break out inside your city, and it burned you up completely. I reduced you to ashes lying on the ground, and everyone who was watching saw it happen.
\v 19 All the peoples who knew you shake with horror at what has happened to you. You have come to a terrifying end, and you will never exist again.’”
\p
\v 20 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 21 “Human, turn toward the city of Sidon and prophesy against it.
\v 22 Say that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘You people of Sidon, I am your enemy! By what I do among you I will show how glorious I am. When I punish you and show that I am holy, then people will know that I am Yahweh.
\v 23 I will send a deadly disease among you, and enemy soldiers will kill people in your streets. They will attack you from every direction, and they will kill your people inside your city. Then people will know that I am Yahweh.
\v 24 The nations all around the people of Israel despise them, but those nations will no longer hurt them or cause them pain. Then the people of Israel will know that I am the Lord Yahweh.’
\p
\v 25 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘I will gather the people of Israel from the peoples among whom I scattered them, and by what I do for them I will show the nations that I am holy. After that, they will live in their own land, the soil that I gave to my servant Jacob.
\v 26 They will live there safely. They will build houses and plant vineyards. They will live safely when I punish all the nations around them that despise them. Then they will know that I am Yahweh their God.’”
\c 29
\p
\v 1 In the tenth year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the twelfth day of the tenth month, Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, turn toward Pharaoh, the king of Egypt and prophesy against him and against everyone who lives in the land of Egypt.
\v 3 Tell him that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘Listen, Pharaoh, king of Egypt! I am now your enemy. You think that you are powerful and safe, like a huge monster that lies among the channels of the Nile River. You boast that the Nile River belongs to you and that you made it for your own benefit.
\v 4 So I will capture you. It will be as though I put hooks in your jaws and made all the fish in your channels stick fast to your scales, and then dragged you up out of the middle of your channels with all those fish still clinging to you.
\v 5 I will throw you out into the desert, you and all {your people who are like} the fish from your channels. You will fall on the open ground, and no one will pick up your body or bury it. I will give your body to the wild animals and the birds for them to eat.
\v 6 Then everyone who lives in Egypt will know that I am Yahweh. I will punish you because you Egyptians failed the people of Israel when they relied on you to support them.
\v 7 When the Israelites asked you for help, you failed them, and you wounded them badly. When they depended on you to hold them up, you could not, and you left them too weak to stand.’
\p
\v 8 Therefore, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘I am about to bring an army to attack you with swords, and those soldiers will kill both your people and your animals.
\v 9 The land of Egypt will become completely deserted and ruined. Then the people of Egypt will know that I am Yahweh. I will do this because you, their king, boasted that the Nile River belongs to you because you made it.
\v 10 Therefore, listen! I am against you and against the channels of your river. I will ruin the land of Egypt completely and leave it deserted, from Migdol in the north to Syene in the south, as far as the border of the land of Cush.
\v 11 No person will walk through that land, and no animal will walk through it. No one will live there for forty years.
\v 12 For forty years I will keep the land of Egypt and all its cities as desolate as the other lands and cities that enemies have devastated. I will scatter the people of Egypt among the other nations and force them to live in foreign lands.’
\p
\v 13 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘After 40 years have passed, I will gather the people of Egypt back from the nations where I scattered them.
\v 14 I will cause them to prosper again. I will bring them back to the region of Pathros {in southern Egypt}, the land that their ancestors came from. There they will be a weak and unimportant kingdom.
\v 15 Egypt will be the least important kingdom of all, and it will never again make itself greater than the other nations. I will make its people so few that they will never again rule over other nations.
\v 16 The people of Israel will never again trust the Egyptians to help them. Instead, whenever the Israelites remember how they sought help from Egypt, they will remember that they sinned by doing that. Then they will know that I am the Lord Yahweh.’”
\p
\v 17 In the twenty-seventh year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the first day of the first month, Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 18 “Human, King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon made his army work extremely hard when they attacked the city of Tyre. The soldiers carried such heavy loads that they rubbed all the hair off their heads and rubbed the skin off their shoulders. Yet neither Nebuchadnezzar nor his army received anything from Tyre to pay them for all the hard work that they did there.
\p
\v 19 Therefore, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘I am about to give the land of Egypt to King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon. He will carry away all the wealth of Egypt and take everything valuable in that land as plunder. That wealth will be the pay for his army.
\v 20 I have given him the land of Egypt as the pay for the hard work that his army did when they attacked Tyre, because they did that work for me.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 21 On that day I will cause the people of Israel to become strong again, and I will enable you, Ezekiel, to speak freely among them. Then they will know that I am Yahweh.”
\c 30
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, prophesy and say that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘Wail and cry out loudly, because a terrible day is coming!
\v 3 That day is near; the day when I, Yahweh, will punish people is very close. It will be a time of gloom and disaster, a time when I judge the nations.
\v 4 An enemy army will come to attack Egypt with swords, and the people of Cush will suffer terribly. When those soldiers kill many people in Egypt, they will carry away the crowds of Egyptians as captives, and they will tear down everything that holds the nation together.
\v 5 The soldiers of Cush, Put, and Lud, all the foreign troops who fight for Egypt, the people of Cub, and the Judeans who live in Egypt will all die in battle along with the Egyptians.’
\p
\v 6 I, Yahweh, say this, ‘The nations that help Egypt will fall, and the Egyptians will no longer be able to boast about how strong they are. Enemy soldiers will kill them in battle everywhere in Egypt, from Migdol in the north to Syene in the south.’ That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 7 ‘I will make the land of Egypt as desolate as the other lands that I have devastated, and its cities will lie in ruins like the other cities that enemies have destroyed.
\v 8 Then the people of Egypt will know that I am Yahweh, because I will set fire to Egypt and defeat every nation that helps her.
\v 9 On that day I will send messengers out from me in ships to terrify the people of Cush, who until then think that they are safe. They will tremble with fear when I punish Egypt. Be sure of this: that day is coming!’
\p
\v 10 I, the Lord Yahweh, say this, ‘I will use King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon to put an end to the crowds of people in Egypt.
\v 11 I am bringing Nebuchadnezzar and his army, soldiers who are more ruthless than any other people, to ruin the land. They will attack Egypt with their swords, and they will kill so many people that dead bodies will lie all over the land.
\v 12 I will dry up the channels of the Nile River, and I will hand the land over to evil men. I will use foreigners to ruin the land and everything in it.’ I, Yahweh, have spoken.
\p
\v 13 I, the Lord Yahweh, say this, ‘I will completely destroy the worthless idols that people worship in the city of Memphis. No one will ever rule as a prince in the land of Egypt again, and I will terrify everyone who lives in Egypt.
\v 14 I will make the region of Pathros in southern Egypt desolate. I will set fire to the city of Zoan, and I will punish the people of the city of Thebes.
\v 15 Because I am extremely angry, I will punish the city named Sin, the fortress that protects Egypt, and I will kill the crowds of people in the city of Thebes.
\v 16 I will set fire to Egypt. The people who live in the city named Sin will suffer agonizing pain, enemies will break through the walls of the city of Thebes, and enemies will attack the city of Memphis every day.
\v 17 Enemy soldiers will kill the young men of the cities of Aven and Pi Beseth, and they will take the people of those cities away as captives.
\v 18 In the city of Tahpanhes it will be a day of disaster, because there I will put an end to Egypt’s power to dominate other nations, and the Egyptians will no longer be able to boast about how strong they are. The people there will experience constant trouble, and enemies will take away the people of Tahpanhes and the surrounding communities as captives.
\v 19 That is how I will punish Egypt, and then people will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\p
\v 20 In the eleventh year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the seventh day of the first month, Yahweh gave me a message. He said,
\v 21 “Human, I have destroyed the military power of Pharaoh, the king of Egypt. No one has done anything to restore that power, just as no one puts medicine on a broken arm or wraps a bandage around it so that it can heal and grow strong enough to grip a sword again. Egypt will never recover its strength for war.
\p
\v 22 Therefore, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘I am now the enemy of Pharaoh, the king of Egypt. I will {take away all the power that he still has, as though I} broke both of his arms, the arm that is still strong and the arm that I have already broken, and I will make the sword drop out of his hand {so that he can fight no longer}.
\v 23 I will scatter the people of Egypt so that they live among many other nations, far away in foreign lands.
\v 24 I will make the king of Babylon strong, and I will use him to carry out my judgment. I will shatter the power of Pharaoh, and Pharaoh will groan loudly in front of the king of Babylon, the way a man groans when someone has wounded him so badly that he is about to die.
\v 25 I will make the king of Babylon strong, but Pharaoh will become completely helpless. Then people will know that I am Yahweh, because I will make the king of Babylon the one who carries out my judgment, and he will attack the land of Egypt.
\v 26 I will scatter the people of Egypt so that they live among many other nations, far away in foreign lands. Then they will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\c 31
\p
\v 1 In the eleventh year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the first day of the third month, Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, say to Pharaoh the king of Egypt and to the crowds of people he rules, ‘You think that no one has ever been as great as you are.
\v 3 Consider Assyria. That nation was once like a cedar tree in Lebanon, a tree with beautiful branches that gave shade to everything under it. It grew very tall, and its top rose above the thick foliage around it.
\v 4 Plenty of water made that tree grow big, and deep underground springs made it grow tall. Those springs sent their streams flowing all around the ground where the tree grew, and they sent channels of water out to every other tree in the field.
\v 5 So that tree grew taller than all the other trees in the field. It put out many limbs, and its branches grew long and spread out, because it had so much water.
\v 6 Birds of every kind built their nests in its limbs, and wild animals of every kind gave birth to their young under its branches. The people of many great nations used to live in its shade{, safe under its protection}.
\v 7 That tree was magnificent, because it was so big and its branches were so long. Its roots reached down to plenty of water.
\v 8 Not even the cedar trees in God’s garden could match it. No cypress tree had limbs like its limbs, and no plane tree had branches like its branches. No tree in God’s garden was as beautiful as that one.
\v 9 I made it beautiful, giving it many branches, and {the rulers of all the other nations, who were like} the other trees in Eden, the garden of God, envied it.
\p
\v 10 So this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, “That tree grew very tall and pushed its top up above the thick foliage, and it became arrogant because it was so tall.
\v 11 Therefore I handed it over to a ruler who was mightier than all the other nations, and he certainly did to it what it deserved. I drove that nation out because it had acted so wickedly.
\v 12 Foreigners, soldiers more ruthless than any other people, chopped that tree down and left it lying there. Its branches fell on the mountains and in all the valleys, and those soldiers broke its boughs to pieces in every ravine in the land. All the peoples who had lived in its shade went away and abandoned it.
\v 13 Birds of every kind came and settled on the trunk of that fallen tree, and wild animals of every kind stayed among its broken branches.
\v 14 I did all this so that no other nation that flourishes like a well-watered tree would ever grow so tall again, or push its top up above the thick foliage, and so that not even the mightiest of those trees, none of the ones that drink plenty of water, would ever stand so high again. I have appointed them all to die and to go down to the world below the earth, along with human beings who go down into the grave.”
\p
\v 15 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, “On the day when that tree went down to the place where dead people go, I caused people to mourn for it. I covered over the deep springs that watered it and held back their rivers, so that the abundant water stopped flowing. Because that tree fell, I made the whole land of Lebanon mourn, and I caused all the other trees of the field to wither.
\v 16 When the nations heard that it had fallen, I made them tremble with fear, for I brought that tree down to the place where dead people go, together with all those who go down into the grave. Down in the world below the earth, the other trees of Eden, the choicest and finest trees of Lebanon, all the ones that had plenty of water, found comfort when it came down to join them.
\v 17 Those other nations also went down with it to the place where dead people go, to join those whom enemies had killed with swords. They had been its strength; they were its allies, and they had lived under its protection among the nations.
\v 18 You Egyptians think that no tree in Eden was ever as glorious and as great as you are. However, I will bring you down, along with those trees of Eden, to the world below the earth. You will lie there dead, among men who do not have the mark of circumcision on their bodies, together with those whom enemies have killed with swords. This whole parable about the great tree describes Pharaoh and all the crowds of people he rules.” That is what the Lord Yahweh declares.’”
\c 32
\p
\v 1 In the twelfth year {after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia}, on the first day of the twelfth month, Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, sing a funeral song about Pharaoh the king of Egypt. Say this to him, ‘You think that you are like a young lion that hunts among the nations, but really you are like a monster that lives in the sea. You thrash about in the streams of your river, churning up the water with your feet and making those streams muddy.
\v 3 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, “I will gather the armies of many peoples, and I will spread my net over you, and those armies will haul you up in my dragnet.
\v 4 Then I will fling you down on the ground and toss you out in an open field. I will let every kind of bird come and settle on you, and I will let wild animals from everywhere eat your dead body until they are full.
\v 5 I will spread your flesh over the mountains, and I will fill the valleys with your rotting remains.
\v 6 Your blood will soak the ground all the way to the mountains, and it will fill the ravines.
\v 7 When I put an end to you, I will cover the sky and darken the stars in it. I will hide the sun behind a cloud, and the moon will not shine.
\v 8 I will darken every shining light in the sky above you, and I will bring darkness and disaster on your land.” That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 9 “I will deeply disturb many peoples when I carry the news of how I shattered you out among the nations, even to lands that you have never known about.
\v 10 I will horrify many peoples because of what happens to you. Their kings will shudder violently because they will be afraid that I will do the same to them. On the day when you fall, they will tremble constantly, each one fearing for his own life.”
\p
\v 11 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, “The army of the king of Babylon will come to attack you.
\v 12 I will let mighty warriors, the most ruthless soldiers of all the nations, kill your huge crowds of people with their swords. Those warriors will ruin everything that the people of Egypt boast about, and they will destroy all the crowds of people there.
\v 13 I will also destroy all the cattle that graze beside Egypt’s abundant waters. Never again will any person or any animal walk in those waters and stir up the mud.
\v 14 Then I will let the waters of Egypt settle, and I will let its rivers flow smoothly and quietly.” That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 15 “The people of Egypt will know that I am Yahweh because I will turn the land of Egypt into an empty wasteland and strip the land of everything that fills it, and I will strike down everyone who lives there.
\v 16 This is a funeral song, and the women of the nations will chant it. They will chant it as they mourn for Egypt and for all its crowds of people.” That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’”
\p
\v 17 In the twelfth year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the fifteenth day of the twelfth month, Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 18 “Human, cry loudly for the crowds of people in Egypt. Command Egypt and the people of other mighty nations to go down to the world below the earth, to join those who go down into the grave.
\v 19 {Taunt the people of Egypt like this,} ‘You are no more delightful than anyone else! Go down and lie down there with godless men!’
\v 20 The people of Egypt will fall down dead among all those whom enemies have killed with swords. I have handed Egypt over to enemies who carry swords; those enemies will drag Egypt away, along with all its crowds of people.
\v 21 From the place where dead people go, the mightiest of the warriors will speak to Pharaoh and to those who helped him. They will say, ‘They have come down here, and now they lie here, godless men, whom enemies killed with swords.’
\v 22 Assyria is there, along with its whole army, and the graves of its soldiers lie all around it. Enemies killed every one of them with swords.
\v 23 Their graves lie in the deepest part of the grave, and the graves of Assyria’s army surround its burial place. Enemies killed every one of them with swords, those soldiers who once terrified people while they were still alive.
\v 24 Elam is there, along with all its crowds of people, and their graves surround its burial place. Enemies killed every one of them with swords. They went down to the world below the earth without God, and while they were still alive they terrified people. Now they suffer disgrace along with those who go down into the grave.
\v 25 People have made a place for Elam to lie down among all those whom enemies killed, together with all its crowds of people, and their graves surround it. They are all godless, and enemies killed them all with swords, because while they were still alive they terrified people. So now they suffer disgrace along with those who go down into the grave. People have laid Elam among all those whom enemies killed.
\v 26 Meshech and Tubal are there, along with all their crowds of people, and their graves surround them. They are all godless, and enemies stabbed them all to death with swords, because while they were still alive they too terrified people.
\v 27 They do not lie among the mighty godless warriors who fell in battle and went down to the place where dead people go carrying their weapons of war. People laid the swords of those warriors under their heads, yet their guilt remained with their bones even after they died, because while they were alive those warriors terrified people.
\v 28 As for you, Pharaoh, I will shatter you, and you will lie down dead among godless men, together with those whom enemies killed with swords.
\p
\v 29 Edom is there, with its kings and all its leaders. In spite of all their strength, people have laid them among those whom enemies killed with swords. They lie there with godless men, along with those who go down into the grave.
\v 30 All the princes of the north are there, and every Sidonian is there too. They went down with those whom enemies killed. They were strong and terrorized people but now they are ashamed. They lie there without God, together with those whom enemies killed with swords, and they suffer disgrace along with those who go down into the grave.
\p
\v 31 Pharaoh will see all of them, and he will find comfort in knowing that others suffered the same fate as all his crowds of people— Pharaoh and his whole army, whom enemies killed with swords.” That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 32 “I allowed Pharaoh to terrify people while they were still alive, so now I will lay him down among godless men, together with those whom enemies killed with swords— Pharaoh and all his crowds of people. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\c 33
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, speak to your fellow Israelites and say this to them, ‘Suppose I send an enemy army to attack a country, and the people of that country choose one of their men and appoint him to be their watchman.
\v 3 Suppose he sees the enemy army coming to attack the country, and he blows a ram’s horn to warn the people.
\v 4 Then if someone hears the sound of the horn but pays no attention to the warning, and the enemy soldiers come and kill him, he is responsible for his own death.
\v 5 He heard the sound of the horn, but he paid no attention to the warning, so he is responsible for his own death. If he had paid attention to the warning, he would have saved his own life.
\v 6 But suppose the watchman sees the enemy army coming and does not blow the horn to warn the people, and the enemy soldiers come and kill one of them. That person will die because of his own sin, but I will hold the watchman responsible for that person’s death.’
\p
\v 7 Now as for you, human, I have appointed you to be like a watchman for the people of Israel. So listen to what I say, and warn them for me.
\v 8 Suppose I tell a wicked person that he will certainly die because of his sins, and you say nothing to warn that wicked person to stop behaving wickedly. Then that wicked person will die because he has sinned, but I will hold you responsible for his death.
\v 9 But suppose you do warn that wicked person to stop behaving wickedly, and he refuses to stop. Then he will die because he has sinned, but you will have saved your own life.
\p
\v 10 Now you human, tell the people of Israel that they keep saying, ‘We feel great emotional and physical pain because we are guilty because we sinned by rebelling against God. How can we go on living?’
\v 11 Say this to them: ‘I, the Lord Yahweh, solemnly declare that as surely as I am alive, I am certainly not pleased when wicked people die. Instead, I am pleased when they stop behaving wickedly and so go on living. So repent! Stop your evil behavior! You people of Israel, there is no reason at all for you to die!’
\p
\v 12 Now you human, say this to your fellow Israelites: ‘If righteous people start to rebel, the righteous things they did before will not rescue them. And if wicked people stop behaving wickedly, the sins that they committed before will not ruin them. Righteous people who start to sin will not go on living just because they were righteous before.
\v 13 Suppose I tell a righteous person that he will certainly go on living, but then he relies on the righteous things he has done and starts doing what is wrong. I will not remember any of the righteous things that he did. He will die because of the wrong things that he is doing.
\v 14 But suppose I tell a wicked person that he will certainly die, but then he stops sinning and starts doing what is just and right.
\v 15 Suppose he gives back what someone handed him to guarantee a loan, and he returns what he stole, and he obeys my rules that cause people to flourish, so that he no longer does what is wrong. Then he will certainly go on living; he will not die.
\v 16 I will forgive any of the sins that he committed. He has done what is just and right, so he will certainly go on living.
\p
\v 17 Yet your fellow Israelites say that I, the Lord, do not act fairly. But it is what they do that is not fair.
\v 18 If a righteous person stops doing what is right and starts doing what is wrong, he will die because of those wrong things.
\v 19 But if a wicked person stops behaving wickedly and starts doing what is just and right, he will go on living because of those good things.
\v 20 But you people still say that I, the Lord, do not act fairly. I will judge each one of you people of Israel according to how you have behaved.’”
\p
\v 21 In the twelfth year after the Babylonians took us people of Israel away to Babylonia, on the fifth day of the tenth month, a man who had escaped from Jerusalem came to me and said, “Our enemies have captured the city!”
\v 22 The evening before that man arrived, the power of Yahweh came over me. By the time the man reached me the next morning, Yahweh had enabled me to speak again, so I could no longer keep silent.
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 24 “Human, the people who are living among the ruins in the land of Israel keep saying, ‘Abraham was only one man, yet Yahweh gave him this whole land to possess. We are many, so Yahweh has surely given this land to us to possess.’
\p
\v 25 So tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘You eat meat that still has the animal’s blood in it. You trust your idols and worship them. You murder people. So you will certainly not go on possessing this land!
\v 26 You rely on your swords to get what you want. You do detestable things. Each of you has sexual relations with another man’s wife. So you will certainly not go on possessing this land!’
\p
\v 27 Also tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘As surely as I am alive, enemy soldiers will kill with their swords those who are living among the ruins. I will let wild animals eat those who are out in the open country. Those who are hiding in fortresses and caves will die from a deadly disease.
\v 28 I will make the land completely empty and ruined. The people will no longer be proud of how strong their country is. The mountains of Israel will become so desolate that no one will travel through them.
\v 29 Then, when I have made the land empty and ruined because of all the detestable things that they have done, they will know that I am Yahweh.’
\p
\v 30 As for you, human, your fellow Israelites are talking about you as they stand beside the walls and in the doorways of their houses, where people gather to talk. They keep saying to one another, ‘Come on, let us go and hear the message that Yahweh is giving!’
\v 31 So my people come to you in large crowds, and they sit down in front of you and listen to what you say. But they do not do what you tell them. All they talk about is how they are planning to become richer.
\v 32 Indeed, they think of you as nothing more than an entertainer, someone who sings love songs with a beautiful voice and plays a musical instrument well. They listen to what you say, but they do not do it.
\v 33 When everything that you have prophesied happens, and it certainly will happen, then they will realize that a true prophet was living among them.”
\c 34
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, prophesy against the leaders of Israel, the men who should be caring for my people the way that shepherds care for their sheep. Say to these men who should be like shepherds that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Terrible things will happen to you leaders of Israel, because you have been taking care of only yourselves! You should be like shepherds who take care of their flocks.
\v 3 {Instead, you use my people to enrich yourselves. It is as though} you eat the fat of the sheep and make your clothes from their wool and slaughter the healthiest animals, but you do not take care of the flock at all.
\v 4 {It is as though} you have not strengthened the weak sheep. You have not healed the sick ones. You have not bandaged the injured ones. You have not brought back the ones that became scattered. You have not searched for the ones that got lost. Instead, you have ruled my people harshly and cruelly.
\v 5 So my people scattered, because they were like sheep without a shepherd. Then their enemies attacked them and plundered them, as wild animals devour sheep that have no shepherd. So they scattered {more}.
\v 6 My people wander like sheep over every mountain and on every high hill. They scatter all across the land, and no one at all searches {for them}.
\p
\v 7 Therefore, you leaders who should be shepherding my people, listen to what I, Yahweh, say:
\v 8 I swear by my own life—this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare—that enemies have carried my people away as plunder and destroyed them as wild animals devour sheep because they had no shepherd. You who should have shepherded my people did not search for them. Instead, you fed only yourselves and did not take care of my people.
\p
\v 9 Therefore, you leaders who should be shepherding my people, listen to what I, Yahweh, say:
\v 10 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: I am now the enemy of you leaders! I will demand that you give my people back to me. I will remove you from taking care of them, so that you can no longer feed only yourselves. I will rescue my people from you, and you will never again plunder and destroy them as wild animals devour sheep.
\p
\v 11 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: Watch what I am about to do! I myself will search for my people, and I myself will take care of them.
\v 12 Just as a shepherd takes care of his sheep when he is among his sheep that have scattered, so I will take care of my people. I will rescue them from every place where enemies scattered them during that terrible time.
\v 13 I will bring my people out from the other peoples and gather them from the other lands, and I will bring them back to their own land. I will care for them like a shepherd on the mountains of Israel, alongside the streams, and in every place in the land where people live.
\v 14 I will take care of them as a shepherd takes care of his sheep by giving them good pastures, and giving them a home on the high mountains of Israel. I will give them a good home in which to rest, and it will be as though they are feeding in rich pastures among the mountains of Israel.
\v 15 I myself will take care of my people, and I myself will enable them to rest. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 16 I will search for the ones that got lost, and I will bring back the ones that enemies chased away. I will bandage the injured ones and strengthen the sick ones. However, I will destroy the fat and strong ones. Those, I will take care of justly.
\p
\v 17 As for you who are like my flock of sheep, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: I am about to judge between each of you who are like sheep, and between each of you who are like rams and male goats.
\v 18 You {powerful ones} are like strong animals that should be satisfied with feeding on the good pasture; and yet you also trample down the rest of the pasture with your feet. You should be satisfied with drinking the clear water; and yet you also muddy the rest of the water with your feet.
\v 19 So {it is as though} the rest of my people are like the flock that must feed on the grass that you trampled and drink the water that you muddied.
\p
\v 20 Therefore, this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say to my flock: I myself am the one who is about to judge between those of you who are {strong like} fat sheep and those of you who are {weak like} thin sheep.
\v 21 {It is as though} you are the powerful sheep who shove the weak sheep aside with your sides and your shoulders and gore all of them with your horns, until you have driven them away {from the pasture}.
\v 22 I will rescue my people, and no one will ever plunder them again. I will judge between each one of my people.
\v 23 I will appoint over them one leader who will care for them the way that a shepherd cares for his flock. He will be {a descendant of} my servant David. He himself will take care of my people, and he himself will be {as} their shepherd.
\v 24 I, Yahweh, will be their God, and {that descendant of} my servant David will be the ruler among them. {This will surely happen because} I, Yahweh, have said this.
\p
\v 25 I will promise my people that they will live peacefully. I will remove every kind of dangerous animal from the land, so that my people can live safely {even} in the wilderness and sleep {safely} {even} in the forests.
\v 26 I will bless my people and all of the land around my {sacred} hill. I will send rain at the proper times, and by that rain I will bless them.
\v 27 The trees in the fields will produce their fruit, and the ground will produce its crops, and my people will live safely in their land. When I rescue them from the people who have caused them to be slaves, they will know that I, Yahweh, {have done this for them}.
\v 28 The people of other nations will never plunder them again, and wild animals will no longer devour them. They will live safely, and there will be no one to frighten them.
\v 29 I will make them famous for how securely I establish them in the land. They will never again need to leave the land because they lack food, and people of other nations will never again humiliate them.
\v 30 Then they will know that I, Yahweh their God, am with them, and that they, the people of Israel, are my people. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 31 You are my people, like a flock of sheep that I take care of in my own pasture. You are human beings, and I am your God. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’”
\c 35
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 2 “Human, turn toward Mount Seir {in the land of Edom} and prophesy against the people there.
\v 3 Tell them, ‘This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: Listen, you people who live around Mount Seir! I am your enemy! I will punish you by turning your land into an empty wasteland where no one lives.
\v 4 I will turn your cities into heaps of rubble, and as for your land, it will become empty. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 5 You have always hated the Israelites, and you delivered them over to soldiers who killed them with swords. You did that when terrible things were happening to them, at the time when I finally punished them for their sins.
\v 6 Therefore, I swear by my own life— this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare—that I will deliver you over to people who will kill you, and killers will pursue you. {I swear that} because you hated the life of your kinsmen, killers will pursue you.
\v 7 I will make the region of Mount Seir a completely desolate area. I will even destroy anyone who travels back and forth through it.
\v 8 I will cover your mountains with the corpses of people whom your enemies will kill. Those whom soldiers kill with their swords will lie dead on your hills, in your valleys, and in all your stream beds.
\v 9 I will make your land empty forever; no one will ever live in your cities again. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 10 You said that the two nations, {Israel and Judah,} and their two lands would belong to you, and that you would take possession of them. You said that even though I, Yahweh, was present there.
\v 11 Therefore, I swear by my own life— this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare—that I will treat you {as harshly} as you treated my people when you were angry at them and envied them because you hated them. The Israelites will know who I am by how I judge you.
\v 12 Then you will {also} know that I am Yahweh. I have heard all of the insulting things that you said about the land of Israel. You said that no one lived there anymore and that you could now take it and everything in it for yourselves.
\v 13 You Edomites boasted arrogantly against me and said many things to insult me, and I myself heard all of it.
\p
\v 14 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: When I devastate your land, people all over the world will rejoice.
\v 15 Just as you rejoiced when the land that I gave to the Israelites became desolate, I will do the same thing to you. Mount Seir and all the rest of Edom will become completely empty. Then people will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\c 36
\p
\v 1 “ As for you, human, prophesy about the mountains in the land of Israel. Tell the Israelites to listen to this message from me, Yahweh, about the mountains of Israel!
\v 2 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, Israel’s enemies gloated over them and claimed that these age-old mountains now belong to them.’
\v 3 Because of that, prophesy and say, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says: I say these things because the survivors from various nations all around greatly desired these mountains and they took possession of them. People everywhere gossiped about them and spread malicious reports about them.
\v 4 Therefore, listen to what I, the Lord Yahweh, say about the mountains of Israel. I, the Lord Yahweh, am speaking about the mountains and the hills, the ravines and the valleys, the ruins where no one lives any longer, and the cities that people abandoned, cities that survivors of the surrounding nations plundered and ridiculed.
\v 5 Because of these things, I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: I swear that I am fiercely jealous {for what is mine} and that is why I have declared that I will punish the nations that have survived, and especially all the people of Edom. They joyfully and scornfully seized my land for themselves so that they could plunder its pastures.’
\v 6 Therefore prophesy about the land of Israel. Say about the mountains and the hills, the ravines and the valleys, that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Listen! I have spoken because I am jealous and very angry, since the other nations have humiliated those who should possess them.
\v 7 Therefore, I, myself, the Lord Yahweh, say this: I take a solemn oath and swear that I will humiliate those very nations that are around the land of Israel.
\p
\v 8 But the mountains of Israel will grow trees that produce plenty of fruit for my people Israel, because my people will soon come home.
\v 9 Indeed, watch what I will do for Israel; I will bless the land. {Soon} people will plow its soil and plant seed in it.
\v 10 I will greatly increase the number of people who live there. They will be all the people of Israel, every one of them. People will live in the cities again, and they will rebuild the broken buildings.
\v 11 I will increase the number of people and animals there, and they will all have many offspring. I will cause people to live there as they did long ago, and I will make them more prosperous than they were at the beginning. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.
\v 12 I will cause my people Israel to go back and forth on the land. They will possess it, and it will belong to them permanently. Never again will they have to leave there.
\v 13 I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: People are saying about the land of Israel that it destroys those who live there and that it loses its citizens.
\v 14 Therefore, from now on, the land will not destroy the people who live there, or cause its citizens to leave there. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 15 Never again will I let the nations insult this land or the peoples revile it, and never again will this land fail the people of Israel. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.’”
\p
\v 16 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 17 “Human, when the Israelite people were living in their own land, they made it a disgusting place by the way that they lived and by the things that they did. I considered the way that they behaved to be as repulsive as a filthy menstruation rag.
\v 18 So I punished them severely, because they had murdered many people in the land and because they had made the land a disgusting place by worshiping idols there.
\v 19 I scattered them among many nations and lands. I punished them for the {evil} way that they had lived and for the {evil} things that they had done.
\v 20 Yet wherever they went among those nations, they caused people to think badly of me, the one who is holy. The people in those places said about them, ‘These people belong to Yahweh, and yet he was not able to keep them in his land.’
\v 21 But I cared that people thought that I was not holy because the Israelite people had caused people to believe that among the nations wherever they went.
\p
\v 22 Therefore, tell the Israelite people that I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: ‘You Israelite people, I will do what I am about to do not because you deserve it. No, I will act in order to show people that I am the holy one, since you caused people to think that I am not powerfully holy among the nations wherever you went.
\v 23 I will show that I am the powerful and holy one, rather than the one whom the people of the nations scorn because you caused them to think that I was profane {like you}. Then, when they see me show that I am powerfully holy by what I do for you, the people of those nations will know that I am Yahweh. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 24 I will take you out of those nations. I will gather you from all those lands and bring you back to your own land.
\v 25 I will remove from you everything that made you unacceptable to me. I will cause you to stop worshiping all of your idols. It will be as though I sprinkled clean water on you and made you completely clean.
\v 26 I will give to you a completely new way of thinking and a completely new attitude. I will cause you to stop being stubborn, and I will cause you to want to obey me.
\v 27 I will put my Spirit within you and I will make it possible for you to live in the way that I have commanded and for you to carefully obey my instructions.
\v 28 Then you will live in the land that I gave to your ancestors. You will be my people, and I myself will be your God.
\v 29 I will rescue you from everything that made you unacceptable to me. I will make your grain grow plentifully, and I will never again cause you to lack food.
\v 30 I will cause your fruit trees and your fields to produce plenty of food, so that the people of other nations will never again ridicule you because you do not have enough to eat.
\v 31 Then you will think about how evil your behavior was and about the wicked things that you did, and you will hate yourselves because you sinned so much by doing those detestable things.
\p
\v 32 I want you to know that I will do these things, but not because you deserve for me to do them. I, the Lord Yahweh, declare this. You Israelite people should feel deep shame and disgrace because of the way that you have behaved!
\v 33 I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: At the time when I forgive you all of your sins, I will cause people to live in your cities again, and they will rebuild the ruined places.
\v 34 People will farm the land that was empty. It will no longer be desolate for everyone who passes by to see.
\v 35 Then people will say, “This land was empty but now it has become {as fertile} as the garden of Eden, and the cities that enemies had ruined and torn down and left empty are now strong and full of people!”
\v 36 Then the nations that I have allowed to remain around you will know that I, Yahweh, have enabled you to rebuild what enemies had torn down and to plant crops again where the land had been empty. I, Yahweh, have promised this, and I will do it.
\p
\v 37 I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: Once again I will respond when the Israelite people ask me to do this for them: I will make their people as numerous as a flock of sheep.
\v 38 They will be like a flock of sheep that belong to Yahweh, like the flock of sheep that fills Jerusalem {when people bring them there to sacrifice} during the yearly festivals. The cities that are now in ruins will be full like that, but of people. Then they will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\c 37
\p
\v 1 Yahweh took hold of me, and by his Spirit he brought me out {from where I was} and set me down in the middle of a valley. This valley was full of {human} bones.
\v 2 He led me all around among the bones. I saw that there were a huge number of bones lying on the ground all through the valley, and I saw that they were completely dry.
\v 3 He asked me, “Human, can these bones become alive again?” I answered, “Lord Yahweh, only you know that.”
\v 4 Then he said to me, “Prophesy to these bones. Say to them, ‘You dry bones, listen to this message from Yahweh!’
\v 5 Tell these bones that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say to them: ‘Watch this! I am going to cause breath to enter you, and you will become alive again.
\v 6 I will attach tendons to you, and I will cause flesh to grow on you, and I will cover you with skin. I will put breath in you, and you will become alive again. Then you will know that I am Yahweh.’”
\v 7 So I prophesied just what Yahweh had commanded me {to say}. While I was prophesying, I heard a noise, and I realized that it was the rattling of the bones coming together, each bone joining to the bone that belonged next to it!
\v 8 As I watched, I saw tendons appear on the bones, and then flesh grew on them, and then skin covered over the surface of them. However, they were not breathing.
\v 9 Then he said to me, “Human, prophesy to the breath. Tell the breath that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Breath, come from every direction and enter into these dead people, so that they may become alive again.’”
\v 10 So I prophesied just what he had commanded me {to say}, and breath entered them. They became alive and stood up on their feet, and became a vast, enormous army.
\p
\v 11 Then he said to me, “Human, these bones represent all the people of Israel. Listen; they keep saying that they have lost all of their strength, that they have nothing left to hope for, and that they have ended as a nation.
\v 12 So prophesy and tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘Listen carefully to this, my people: {It will be as though} I will open your graves and bring you up out of them {and make you live again} when I bring you back to the land of Israel.
\v 13 When {it is as though} I am opening your graves and bringing you up out of them, then you my people will know that I am Yahweh.
\v 14 I will put my Spirit in you, and {it will be as though} you will become alive again, and I will settle you in your own land. Then you will know that I, Yahweh, am the one who has promised this and that I am the one who has done it. That is what I, Yahweh, declare.’”
\p
\v 15 Yahweh gave me another message. He said,
\v 16 “Now you human, get a shaft of wood and write on it, ‘This represents Judah and the Israelite tribes that belong with Judah.’ Then get another shaft of wood and write on it, ‘This represents the Israelite tribes associated with Joseph, that is, it is the shaft of the tribe of Ephraim, and all of the Israelite tribes that belong with Ephraim.’
\v 17 Then align the two pieces of wood with each other as one piece of wood so that they become like one shaft of wood in your hand.
\v 18 When your fellow Israelites ask you to explain to them what you mean by doing this,
\v 19 tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say, ‘Listen well! I am going to take the piece of wood that represents Joseph, the part of Israel that the tribe of Ephraim controls, along with the Israelite tribes that belong with Ephraim. I will take them together, and then I will join them to the piece of wood that represents Judah. I will make them to be one piece of wood, and I will hold them as one piece of wood in my hand.’
\v 20 {As you say this,} hold in your hand the pieces of wood on which you wrote, where your fellow Israelites can see them.
\v 21 Then tell them that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Listen well! I am going to take the Israelite people out of the nations where they have gone. I will gather them from every direction and bring them back to their own land.
\v 22 I will make them to be one nation in that land, on the mountains of Israel. One king will rule over all of them. They will not be two nations any more, and they will never again separate into two kingdoms.
\v 23 They will no longer make themselves unacceptable to me by {worshiping} their idols and their disgusting images, or by their many ways of rebelling against me. I will rescue them from all the places where they have been living and sinning, and I will forgive them for sinning. They will be my people, and I myself will be their God.
\p
\v 24 {A descendant of} my servant David will be their king, and he alone will rule over all of them, taking care of them as a shepherd takes care of his flock. They will live in the way that I have said is right, and they will carefully obey all of my laws.
\v 25 They will live in the land that I gave to my servant Jacob, the land where their ancestors lived. They and their children and their grandchildren will live there forever, and {the one who is the descendant of} my servant David will be their ruler forever.
\v 26 I will make an agreement with them that will make a peaceful relationship possible between us; it will be an agreement that lasts forever for them. I will establish them {in that land} and cause them to become very numerous, and I will put my temple among them forever.
\v 27 I will live among them; I will be their God, and they themselves will be my people.
\v 28 When my holy dwelling place is among the Israelite people forever, then the other nations will know that I, Yahweh, am the one who makes Israel holy.’”
\c 38
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave me another message. He said to me,
\v 2 “Human, turn toward Gog, who is from the land of Magog and who is the most important ruler over the regions of Meshech and Tubal, and prophesy against him.
\v 3 Tell him that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Gog, you who are the most important ruler over Meshech and Tubal, be aware that I am your enemy!
\v 4 I will turn you around and {force you to go wherever I want you to go, as though I had} put hooks in your jaws. I will bring you out {for battle}, you and your whole army, your horses and the men who ride them. All of them will be wearing full armor, a huge crowd of men carrying both large shields and small shields. Every one of them is skillful at using his sword.
\v 5 Soldiers from Persia, Cush, and Put will go along with your army, and all of them will carry shields and wear helmets.
\v 6 The army of Gomer with all of its many soldiers will go with you, and so will the army of Beth Togarmah, which lies in the far north, with all of its groups of soldiers. {The armies from} many nations will go with you.
\v 7 Get ready! Prepare yourself well, you and all the troops that you have gathered around you, and be ready to command them.
\v 8 A long time from now I will summon you for battle. After many years have passed, you will attack a land where the people have recovered from war. They are the people whom I will have gathered from many nations and settled again all through the land of Israel, which has been in ruins for a very long time. Those are the very people whom I will have brought out from among the nations, and all of them will be living there peacefully.
\v 9 You will advance {as suddenly and violently} as a storm. Your troops and the many peoples who come with you {will be so many that they} will spread like a cloud over the whole land.
\p
\v 10 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say will happen: At that time, you will think about doing something evil, and you will make a plan to do it.
\v 11 You will say {to yourself}, “{With my army} I will attack {the people of} this land whose villages have no walls around them. I will attack people who are living quietly and who feel secure, all of them living in places that have no walls, no gates, and no bars.
\v 12 I will take away everything valuable that they own. I will attack the towns that soldiers had destroyed but where people are living once again. I will attack these people who have come back from many nations and who have acquired livestock and other possessions. I will attack these people who live at this significant place in the world.”
\v 13 The people of Sheba and Dedan and the merchants of Tarshish and its bold traders will say to you, “You are certainly coming in order to steal everything valuable. You have certainly gathered your army together in order to carry off silver and gold, to seize livestock and other possessions, and to take away a huge amount of goods.”’
\p
\v 14 So then human, prophesy to Gog. Say to him that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘At that time, when my people Israel are living peacefully, you will certainly notice it.
\v 15 Then you will come from your home in the far north, and many peoples will come with you. They will be an enormous army, and all of them will be riding horses.
\v 16 Your army will attack my people Israel, and your soldiers {will be so many that they} will spread over the whole land like a cloud. This will happen in a far future time. Gog, I will bring you to attack my land so that the nations will come to know me. They will watch what I do to you, and in that way I will show them that I am holy.’
\p
\v 17 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Do not think that you are the person about whom I spoke long ago through my servants, the prophets of Israel, when for many years during that time they prophesied that I would bring someone to attack the people of Israel.
\v 18 No, what will happen at the time when you, Gog, step onto the ground belonging to Israel, is this: I will become furious. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 19 Because I am fiercely protective of my people and because I am extremely angry, I solemnly promise that at that time there will be a great earthquake in the land of Israel.
\v 20 The fish in the sea, the birds in the sky, the wild animals, every creature that crawls on the ground, and every person living on the earth will tremble before me. Mountains will fall down, cliffs will collapse, and walls everywhere will fall to the ground.
\v 21 I will make men fight against you, Gog, on all my mountains. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare. Every one of your soldiers will attack his fellow soldier with his sword.
\v 22 I will punish you by causing a deadly disease among you and by causing many people to die violently. I will send heavy rain, hailstones, fire, and burning sulfur down on you and on your troops and on the many peoples who are with you.
\v 23 In that way I will show that I am great and that I am holy, and people of many nations will come to know who I am as they watch. They will acknowledge that I am Yahweh{, the one who has power over everything}.’”
\c 39
\p
\v 1 {Yahweh also said to me,} “Now you human, prophesy against Gog. Tell him that this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: ‘Gog, you who are the most important ruler over Meshech and Tubal, be aware that I am your enemy!
\v 2 I will turn you around and drive you along. I will bring you up from the far north, and I will send you {to fight} on the mountains of Israel.
\v 3 Then I will knock your bow out of your left hand and make your arrows drop from your right hand.
\v 4 You and all of your troops and all of the peoples who come with you will die on the mountains of Israel. I will give your dead bodies as food to birds of every kind that feed on flesh and to the wild animals.
\v 5 You will die out in the open country, because I myself have said {that it will happen}. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 6 I will cause fires to burn throughout the land of Magog and among the people who live in the lands along the seacoasts and who think that they are safe there. Then they will know that I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 7 I will clearly display that I am the powerfully holy one among my people Israel, and I will never again allow people {to have any reason} to say that I am not powerfully holy. Then the people of other nations will know that I am Yahweh, the holy one who lives among the people of Israel.
\v 8 Mark my words, the time when these things will happen is certainly coming. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare. It is the time that I have spoken {to you} about.
\p
\v 9 Then the people who live in the cities of Israel will go out, gather up the weapons, and burn them. They will burn the small shields and the large shields, the bows and the arrows, the clubs that soldiers carry in their hands, and the spears. They will use those weapons to make fires for seven years.
\v 10 They will not need to gather wood from the fields or cut wood in the forests, because they will make their fires with those weapons. They will actually be taking plunder from the people who came to plunder them! That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 11 At that time I will give Gog a place in Israel; it will be his grave, in the Valley of the Travelers, which is east of the sea. That grave will block the people who travel through it. There the people will bury Gog and the whole crowd of soldiers who came with him, and they will call it the Valley of Hamon Gog.
\v 12 For seven months the people of Israel will be burying those dead bodies, so that the dead bodies will no longer defile the land {by remaining unburied}.
\v 13 All of the people of the land {of Israel} will {work together to} bury them, and doing that will cause people to honor them. At that time everyone will see how glorious I am. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 14 At the end of the seven months, the people will appoint men to continue to work at this task. Those men will travel through the land searching for and burying the bodies of the invaders that still lie on the ground, in order to make the land acceptable to me again.
\v 15 As those men travel through the land, whenever any one of them sees a human bone, he will set up a marker beside it. When the gravediggers see the markers, they will pick up the bones and bury them in the Valley of Hamon Gog.’
\v 16 (There is also a city named Hamonah.) ‘In this way the people will make the land acceptable to me again.’
\p
\v 17 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say to you human: Call out to every kind of bird and to every wild animal. Say to them, ‘Gather yourselves together from everywhere and come to the feast that Yahweh is preparing for you. He will slaughter a great many people on the mountains of Israel. There you will eat {men’s} flesh and drink their blood.
\v 18 You will eat the flesh of mighty soldiers, and you will drink the blood of the rulers of the nations. {Yahweh will slaughter them as though they were the} rams and lambs and goats and bulls {that people sacrifice}, all of them {like} the well-fed animals from {the region of} Bashan.
\v 19 At the sacrifice that Yahweh is preparing for you, you will eat fat until you are completely full, and you will drink blood until you can drink no more.
\v 20 {It will be as though} you are eating at a table that I have prepared for you. You will eat all you want {of the flesh} of horses and of the men who ride them, of mighty soldiers and of every kind of fighting man. {That is what I,} the Lord Yahweh, declare.’
\v 21 I will show the {people of many} nations how glorious I am, and all of those people will see how I have justly punished them.
\v 22 Starting at that time and then always afterward, the people of Israel will know that I am Yahweh their God.
\v 23 The {people of many} nations will also know that I banished the people of Israel from their land because they sinned, that is, they acted unfaithfully toward me. So I refused to help them and instead let their enemies capture them and kill very many of them.
\v 24 I punished them in the way that they deserved, because they had become disgusting to me and had rebelled against me. So I refused to help them.
\p
\v 25 Therefore this is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: Now I will make the descendants of Jacob prosperous again, and I will act mercifully toward all of the people of Israel. I will work passionately to keep anyone from having a reason to say that I am not {powerfully} holy.
\v 26 When the people of Israel are living safely in their own land with no one making them afraid, then they will feel ashamed of the disgraceful things that they did {previously} and of all of the ways that they had acted unfaithfully toward me.
\v 27 I will gather them from among the other peoples and bring them back from the lands of their enemies {to their own land}. When I do that for them, then the people of many nations will understand that I am {powerfully} holy.
\v 28 Then the people of Israel will know that I am Yahweh their God, because I sent them into exile among the nations and I will gather them back into their own land again. I will not leave any of them in those nations any longer.
\v 29 I will never again refuse to help them after I generously give my Spirit to the people of Israel. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\c 40
\p
\v 1 It was 24 years after {the Babylonians} forcibly removed us Israelites from our land. It was 13 years after {the Babylonians} conquered the city of Jerusalem. It was the tenth day of the first month. On that day, Yahweh acted powerfully toward me and took me somewhere.
\v 2 God did this in something like a dream. He took me to the area where the Israelites lived and had me stand on a very tall mountain. On the south side of the mountain were buildings that together looked like a city.
\v 3 Then Yahweh took me to those buildings. There, I saw a man! He was standing at an entrance, and he shone like bronze. He was holding a linen rope and a stick for measuring things.
\v 4 The man told me, “Human, God took you to this place so that you would learn things. So, you must look at, listen to, and think carefully about everything that I will show you. Later, you must tell the Israelites everything that you learned.”
\p
\v 5 Now the stick that the man was holding was for measuring things using longer measurements than normal. It was about 3.2 meters long. Then I saw a wall that went all the way around the area where the {temple} building was. The man used the stick to measure the wall. It was 3.2 meters thick and 3.2 meters tall.
\v 6 Then the man went to the gatehouse on the east side {of the wall} and climbed up its stairs. He measured the threshold. It was 3.2 meters deep.
\v 7 {Inside the gatehouse}, there were small side rooms. They were 2.7 meters apart. Each side room was 3.2 meters deep and 3.2 meters wide. Where the gatehouse opened toward the {temple} building, there was an entryway. Inside the entryway, there was a threshold. It was 3.2 meters deep.
\v 8 The man measured the entryway where the gatehouse opened toward the {temple} building.
\v 9 It was 4.3 meters deep. The walls around its opening were 1.1 meters deep. {That was what the man measured} for the entryway where the gatehouse opened toward the {temple} building.
\v 10 Inside the gatehouse on the east side, there were three small side rooms on each side {of the hallway}. Each of them was the same size. The walls around each of their openings were the same size.
\v 11 The man measured the open space for the gatehouse’s door. It was 5.4 meters wide. The gatehouse’s hallway was seven meters wide.
\v 12 In front of each small side room, there was a half wall. Each half wall was 54 centimeters {wide}, and each small side room was 3.2 meters {on each side}.
\v 13 Then the man measured along the top of the gatehouse from the back of one small side room to the back of the small side room on the other side {of the hallway}. It was 13.5 meters wide.
\v 14 He measured all the walls around the gatehouse’s openings, ending at the walls around the opening to the courtyard. They were 32.4 meters high.
\v 15 {He measured} from the front of the gatehouse, where people entered, to the end of the entryway on the inside. It was 27 meters.
\v 16 There were niches on the inside of the small side rooms, on the walls around their openings, and in the entryway all around the inside of the gatehouse. Carvings of palm trees were on the walls around the gatehouse’s openings.
\p
\v 17 Then the man had me walk into the courtyard that was between the outer wall and the inner wall. I saw stone flooring all the way around {the inside of the outer wall}. I also saw 30 rooms. {Their back walls were the outer wall,} and they opened {to the inside} onto the stone flooring.
\v 18 The stone flooring stretched out into the courtyard as far as each of the gatehouses did. This flooring was lower {than the courtyard inside the inner wall}.
\v 19 The man measured from the inner side of the gatehouse {in the outer wall} that was lower {than the courtyard inside the inner wall}. He measured to the outside of {the gate that led to} the courtyard that was inside the inner wall. It was 54 meters long. {That was what the man measured for the gatehouse on} the east {side}. {Then, we went to the gatehouse on} the north {side}.
\p
\v 20 He measured the gatehouse on the north side of the outer wall. He measured how deep and how long it was.
\v 21 It had three small side rooms on each side {of the hallway}, walls around its openings, and entryway. They were all exactly the same size as the ones in the gatehouse on the eastern side. The gatehouse was 27 meters long and 13.5 meters wide.
\v 22 It had niches, an entryway, and carvings of palm trees. They were all exactly the same size as the ones in the gatehouse on the eastern side. People had to walk up seven steps to enter the gatehouse. When they did, the entryway was directly in front of them.
\v 23 Just as there was on the east side, on the north side there was a gatehouse through the inner wall in line with the outer gatehouse. The man measured from this inner gate to the gate through the outer wall on the north side. It was 54 meters.
\p
\v 24 Then, the man had me walk to the south side {of the courtyard}. There, I saw the gatehouse on the south side {of the wall}. He measured the walls around its openings and its entryway. They were the same size as the ones in the other gatehouses.
\v 25 There were niches all around the inside of the gatehouse and its entryway. They were the same as the niches in the other gatehouses. This gatehouse was 27 meters long and 13.5 meters wide.
\v 26 People had to walk up seven steps to enter the gatehouse. When they did, the entryway was directly in front of them. On each of the walls around its openings there were carvings of palm trees.
\v 27 On the south side there was a gatehouse through the inner wall. The man measured from this inner gate to the gate through the outer wall on the south side. It was 54 meters.
\p
\v 28 Then the man had me walk into the courtyard that was inside the inner wall. We went through the gatehouse on the south side {of the inner wall}. He measured that gatehouse. It was exactly the same size as the other gatehouses.
\v 29 It had small side rooms, walls around its openings, and an entryway. They were exactly the same size as the ones in the other gatehouses. There were niches all around the inside of the gatehouse and its entryway. The gatehouse was 27 meters long and 13.5 meters wide.
\v 30 The entryways to each of the gates through the inner wall were 13.5 meters long and 2.7 meters wide.
\v 31 The south gatehouse’s entryway opened to the courtyard between the inner and outer walls. Carvings of palm trees were on the walls around its openings. People had to walk up eight steps to enter it.
\p
\v 32 Then the man had me walk to the east side of the courtyard that was inside the inner wall. He measured the gatehouse there. It was exactly the same size as the other gatehouses.
\v 33 It had small side rooms, walls around its openings, and an entryway. They were exactly the same size as the ones in the other gatehouses. There were niches all around the inside of the gatehouse and its entryway. The gatehouse was 27 meters long and 13.5 meters wide.
\v 34 Its entryway opened to the courtyard between the inner and outer walls. Carvings of palm trees were on each of the walls around its openings. People had to walk up eight steps to enter it.
\p
\v 35 Then the man had me walk to the gatehouse on the north side {of the inner wall}. He measured it. It was exactly the same size as the other gatehouses.
\v 36 It had small side rooms, walls around its openings, and an entryway. There were niches all around the inside of the gatehouse. The gatehouse was 27 meters long and 13.5 meters wide.
\v 37 Its entryway opened to the courtyard between the inner and outer walls. Carvings of palm trees were on each of the walls around its openings. People had to walk up eight steps to enter it.
\p
\v 38 There was a room next to each inner gatehouse. Each had a door that opened into the entryway of that gatehouse. That was where people washed their wholly burned sacrifices.
\v 39 Inside the north gatehouse’s entryway, there were two tables on each side {of the hallway}. People killed the animals for their wholly burned sacrifices, purifying sacrifices, and restoring sacrifices on those tables.
\v 40 Outside the north gatehouse’s entryway, next to its front walls, there were two tables on each side of the steps that went up to the entryway.
\v 41 So, there were four tables on each side of the gatehouse. People killed animals for sacrifices on those eight tables.
\v 42 Someone had shaped stones to make the four tables {inside the gatehouse} on which people killed their wholly burned sacrifices. Each table was 81 centimeters long, 81 centimeters wide, and 54 centimeters tall. People put on these tables the tools they used to kill the wholly burned sacrifices and any other kind of sacrifice.
\v 43 People also put on those tables the meat from their sacrifices. There were ledges {on the inside walls} of the {entryway} structure all around. Each ledge was 8 centimeters deep.
\p
\v 44 Within the courtyard that was inside the inner wall and its gatehouses, there were two rooms. One room was next to the north gatehouse. Its door opened to the south. The other room was next to the east gatehouse. Its door opened to the north.
\v 45 The man told me, “The room whose doors open to the south is for the priests who guard the {temple} building.
\v 46 The room whose doors open to the north is for the priests who guard the altar. All those priests are descendants of Zadok. They are the only Levites who can approach Yahweh to serve him.”
\v 47 Then he measured the courtyard {inside the inner walls}. It was square, 54 meters long on each side. There was an altar in front of the {temple} building.
\p
\v 48 Then the man had me walk into the {temple} building’s entryway. He measured the walls around the gateway opening. They were 2.7 meters thick on each side. The gateway opening was 7.6 meters across. The front walls that spanned from the gateway to the side walls were 1.6 meters wide on each side.
\v 49 The entryway was 10.8 meters wide and 6.5 meters deep. People had to walk up stairs to enter it. There were two large posts {just outside} the walls around the entryway’s opening, one on each side.
\c 41
\p
\v 1 Then the man had me walk into the Holy Place. He measured the walls around its opening. They were 3.2 meters thick on each side.
\v 2 The opening into the Holy Place was 5.4 meters across. The walls that spanned from the opening to the side walls were 2.7 meters wide on each side. Then the man measured the Holy Place. It was 21.6 meters long and 10.8 meters wide.
\v 3 Then the man went into the inner room. He measured the walls around its opening. They were 1.1 meters thick. The opening into the room was 3.2 meters across. {The walls that spanned} from the opening {to the side walls} were 3.8 meters wide {on each side}.
\v 4 He measured that inner room, which was at the far end of the Holy Place. It was 10.8 meters long and 10.8 meters wide. He told me, “This room is the Most Holy Place.”
\p
\v 5 Then the man measured the wall for the temple building. It was 3.2 meters thick. There were rooms along the outside of the temple building’s wall on its {north, south, and west} sides. Each of these side rooms was 2.2 meters wide.
\v 6 There were 30 side rooms around the temple building, and each one had two more side rooms directly above it. There were ledges all around the temple building’s wall to support these rooms. That way, the temple building wall itself did not support these rooms.
\v 7 The side rooms were deeper at the top than at the bottom all around the temple building. So, the outside of the temple building was wider at the top than at the bottom. There were steps that went around and up {the inside of the front side rooms}. That way, people could walk up to the highest room from the lowest room through the middle room.
\v 8 I noticed that the temple building stood on a platform on all sides. The part of the platform that the side rooms stood on was 3.2 meters {high}.
\v 9 The outer wall for the side rooms was 2.7 meters thick. Outside the side rooms that were along the outer wall of the temple building, between them
\v 10 and the rooms {that were further away from the temple building}, there was an open space 10.8 meters wide around the whole temple building.
\v 11 There were two doorways into the side rooms. One faced north and one faced south. They opened into an open space at the edge of the platform. This open space was 2.7 meters wide all around {the temple building}.
\p
\v 12 On the west side of the courtyard {that went around the temple building}, there was another building. Its wall was 2.7 meters thick all the way around. {The inside} of the building was 38 meters east to west and 49 meters north to south.
\v 13 Then the man measured the temple building from east to west. It was 54 meters long. He measured the courtyard {on the western side of the temple building}, the other building, and the other building’s walls from east to west. In total, they were 54 meters long.
\v 14 The courtyard on the east side of the temple building was 54 meters from north to south.
\v 15 The man measured the other building, the one that was on the west side of the western courtyard. He included its decorative shelves on each side. It was 54 meters from north to south.
\p As for the Holy Place, the Most Holy Place, and the entryway out into the courtyard,
\v 16 those three rooms all had thresholds, niches, and decorative shelves all around. Starting at the threshold, there were long, thin pieces of wood covering the walls, niches, and decorative shelves all around, from the floor to the niches. The niches had coverings over their openings.
\v 17 The long, thin pieces of wood covered everything from above the entrance into the temple building to the Most Holy Place. They covered all the walls, inside and outside the temple building, all the way around. Each piece of wood was exactly the same size.
\v 18 Workmen had carved winged creatures and palm trees on the pieces of wood. There was always one palm tree between two winged creatures. Each winged creature had two faces.
\v 19 A human face looked toward the palm tree on one side. A lion’s face looked toward the palm tree on the other side. Workmen had carved this pattern on the walls all around the temple building.
\v 20 From the floor to the top of the entrance, including the walls of the Holy Place, workmen had carved the winged creatures and palm trees.
\p
\v 21 The sides of the door frame into the Holy Place had faces at right angles to each other. The door frames into the Most Holy Place were exactly the same as those.
\v 22 {In the Holy Place,} there was a wooden altar. It was 1.6 meters high and 1.1 meters long {and wide}. Its corner pieces, base, and sides were wooden. Then the man told me, “This is a table. It is in Yahweh’s presence.”
\v 23 The entrance into Holy Place and the entrance into the Most Holy Place each had two sets of doors.
\v 24 Each of the two sets of doors {at each entrance} had two panels that swung on hinges.
\v 25 Workmen had carved winged creatures and palm trees on the sets of doors into the Holy Place. They were just like the carvings on the temple building’s walls. There was a wooden covering over the area just outside the entryway {into the temple building}.
\v 26 On the two walls that spanned from the entryway opening to its sidewalls, there were niches and carvings of palm trees. The rooms on the outside of the temple building also had coverings.
\c 42
\p
\v 1 Then the man had me walk out into the courtyard that was between the inner and outer walls. We went through {the gate} on the north side of the inner walls. He led me to some rooms. These rooms were next to the courtyard and building that were on the west side of the temple building. The rooms were on the north side of that courtyard and building.
\v 2 All together, those rooms were 54 meters from east to west and 27 meters from north to south. There was an entrance on their north side.
\v 3 The courtyard that was 10.8 meters wide to the west of the temple building was on the southeast side of those rooms. The stone flooring around the inside of the outer walls was on the northwest side of those rooms. The outside of the rooms had two decorative shelves that divided them into three stories.
\v 4 On the north side of the rooms, there was a walkway 5.4 meters wide and 54 meters long. The rooms’ doors opened on the north side.
\v 5 The rooms on the third story were narrower {north to south than the rooms on the lower stories}. That is because the decorative shelves above the first and second stories used space {that would have been part of the rooms on the floor above}.
\v 6 So, there were three stories of rooms. However, although the rest of the rooms in both courtyards had large posts, these rooms did not. Because of that, the highest story was narrower than the middle story, which was narrower than the lowest story.
\v 7 Along the north side of the rooms, {at the north edge of the walkway,} there was a low wall. It was 27 meters long from east to west.
\v 8 {That low wall was only 27 meters long} because the rooms along the outer wall were 27 meters deep. So, {the length of the room next to the western outer wall and the length of the low wall added up to} the length of these rooms that were nearer to the Holy Place. They were 54 meters long.
\v 9 On the ground floor of these rooms, there was an entryway on the eastern side. A person could enter from the courtyard between the inner and outer walls
\v 10 where the low wall began.
\p There were more rooms next to the courtyard and building that were on the west side of the temple building. These rooms were on the south side of that courtyard and building.
\v 11 There was a walkway on the {south} side of those rooms, just like the one on the {north side of} the northern rooms. They were exactly the same size as the northern rooms, and they had the same measurements, exits, and entrances.
\v 12 Those rooms had entrances that opened to the south. The entrance to the walkway was like those entrances. A person could enter it at the eastern end of the walkway and the low wall {that was on the south side of the walkway}.
\p
\v 13 Then the man told me, “The northern and southern rooms that are next to the courtyard {to the west of the temple building} are sacred. Because they are sacred, the priests who approach Yahweh set the most sacred offerings there, including grain offerings, purifying offerings, and restoring sacrifices. They also eat the most sacred offerings there.
\v 14 Now the clothes that the priests wear when they are serving as priests in sacred areas are also sacred. So, when the priests are ready to leave those sacred areas, they must first leave their clothes in the rooms {to the north or the south of the temple building}. When they have changed into other clothes, they can go into the courtyard between the inner and outer walls and to where the rest of the people are.”
\p
\v 15 The man had now completed measuring everything inside the temple area. Next, he had me walk outside the temple area through the gatehouse on the east side. Then, he measured the area all around the outside.
\v 16 He measured the east side with the stick for measuring things. It was 270 meters long.
\v 17 He measured the north side with the stick for measuring things. It was 270 meters long.
\v 18 He measured the south side with the stick for measuring things. It was 270 meters long.
\v 19 Then he went to the west side and measured it with the stick for measuring things. It was 270 meters long.
\v 20 {In summary,} the man measured the temple area on all four sides. There was a wall all the way around it, and it was 270 meters long on each side. This wall divided the sacred area from the ordinary area {outside it}.
\c 43
\p
\v 1 After that, the man had me walk to the gatehouse that went through the {outer} eastern wall.
\v 2 Then I saw where the God whom we Israelites worship was making himself gloriously present! It was like a very bright light, which lit up the ground around it. It was as loud as crashing waves. It was moving from the east toward us.
\v 3 What I saw looked exactly like what I saw previously when God arrived to destroy Jerusalem. It also looked exactly like what I saw previously when I was at the Kebar River. So, I {fearfully and respectfully} bowed with my face to the ground.
\v 4 The place where Yahweh made himself gloriously present went through the gatehouses on the east side of the temple area. It arrived at the temple building.
\v 5 Then {Yahweh sent} a wind {that} picked me up and set me back down in the courtyard inside the inner walls. Then I saw that Yahweh had made himself gloriously present throughout the temple building.
\p
\v 6 The man {who had measured everything} was standing next to me. Then I heard someone inside the temple building speaking to me.
\v 7 That person told me, “Human, this is where I will rule as king. I will make myself present here with the Israelites forever. The Israelite people and their kings will no longer cause people to dishonor my holy self. They used to do that when they worshiped other gods, performed rituals to appease their dead kings at the high places,
\v 8 and built houses against the temple walls. They did disgusting things, which made people dishonor my holy self. So, I was angry with them and destroyed them.
\v 9 At this time, they must stop worshiping other gods and performing rituals to appease their dead kings. Then, I will make myself present here with them forever.
\p
\v 10 As for you, human, describe the temple area to the Israelites. That will shame them for what they have done wrong. Have them study carefully how I have designed it.
\v 11 Suppose that everything {wrong} that they have done does shame them. In that case, tell them about the design of the temple building and where everything in it is. Tell them about its exits and entryways. Tell them about the rules and instructions for it. Write it all down for them. Then, they must do everything that I have instructed them to do.
\v 12 Listen to this very important instruction concerning the temple building. The area all around the top of this mountain is very sacred.
\p
\v 13 Here is what the altar’s parts measure, using longer measurements than normal. Each unit of measure is 54 centimeters long. There is a gutter {around the base of the altar}. It is 54 centimeters deep and 54 centimeters across. There is a rim all around {the outside of} the gutter. It is 23 centimeters high. What follows are the parts that make up the altar’s bottom part.
\v 14 There are side walls from the bottom of the gutter to a ledge 1.1 meters above the ground. The ledge is 54 centimeters deep. There are more side walls from this lower ledge to a higher ledge 2.2 meters above the lower ledge. This higher ledge is also 54 centimeters deep.
\v 15 The flat area for burning things is 2.2 meters {above the higher ledge}. There are four projections upward, {one from each of the four corners} of the flat area.
\v 16 The flat area for burning things has corners that are right angles. Each side is 6.5 meters long.
\v 17 The {highest} ledge has corners that are right angles. Each side is 7.6 meters long. Around {the flat area for burning things} is a gutter. At the outer edge of the gutter, there is a rim that is 27 centimeters high. The gutter is 54 centimeters deep. There are steps {to the altar} on the east side.”
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh told me, “Human, I, the Lord Yahweh, say this: These are the instructions about the altar. When {workmen} finish building the altar, {you must follow these instructions before the Israelites regularly} offer wholly burned sacrifices on it and splatter blood on it.
\v 19 You{, Ezekiel,} must provide the priests with a young bull as a purifying sacrifice. Those priests are descendants of Zadok. They are the Levites who can approach me to serve me. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 20 {After slaughtering the bull and catching its draining blood in a bowl,} you must smear some of the blood on the four projections {from the top of the altar}, the four corners of the {highest} ledge, and the rim that goes around {that ledge}. In this way, you will cleanse the altar and make it pure.
\v 21 Then, you must retrieve the dead body of the bull that you used for the purifying sacrifice. You must have someone burn the body outside the sacred area in the proper place.
\v 22 The next day you must offer a male goat as a purifying sacrifice. It must be completely healthy. The priests must cleanse the altar with it in the same way they cleansed the altar with the bull.
\v 23 At that point, you {and the priests} have completed cleansing {the altar}. After that, you must offer a young bull and a ram. They must be completely healthy.
\v 24 You must bring them to my presence. The priests must sprinkle salt on them. Then, they must offer them as a wholly burned sacrifice to me.
\v 25 On each of the next five days, you must offer a male goat as a purifying sacrifice. The priests must also offer a young bull and a ram. Those animals must be completely healthy.
\v 26 In summary, for seven days in total the priests must make the altar pure. As they do that, they will dedicate it {to me}.
\v 27 Once that is done, from the eighth day {after they began} and onward, the priests will offer you Israelites’ wholly burned sacrifices and fellowship sacrifices on that altar. Then, I will be happy with you. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare..”
\c 44
\p
\v 1 Then the man took me back to the gatehouse that opened through the outer wall on the eastern side. Someone had closed it securely.
\v 2 Yahweh told me, “I, Yahweh, the God whom you Israelites worship, passed through this gatehouse. So, this gatehouse must stay closed at all times. No one may open it or pass through it.
\v 3 The man who leads {the Israelites} is the only one who may go inside this gatehouse. He may eat special food there in my presence. He must both enter and exit the gatehouse only through the entryway.”
\p
\v 4 Then the man had me walk through the gatehouse on the north side {of the inner wall}. We walked to the area in front of the temple building. When I looked at Yahweh’s temple building, I saw that Yahweh had made himself gloriously present throughout it! So, I {fearfully and respectfully} bowed with my face to the ground.
\v 5 Then Yahweh told me, “Human, I am about to tell you all the instructions and rules concerning my temple building. So, pay close attention. Listen very carefully. Pay attention to the temple building’s entryway and all the exits from the temple area.
\v 6 Here is what I want you to tell the Israelites, who openly defy me. This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say to them. The Israelites have done too many disgusting things!
\v 7 They invited people who were not Israelites into my temple area while they were offering fat and blood from sacrifices to me. No one had circumcised those people, and they did not want to obey me. So, they defiled my temple building. When the Israelites did many disgusting things like that, they disobeyed the agreement that I made with them.
\v 8 They did not guard the sacred things that belong to me. Instead, they had people who were not Israelites guard my temple area.
\p
\v 9 Here is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say. All people who are not Israelites are not allowed to enter the temple area. No one has circumcised those people, and they do not want to obey me. That rule applies to all people who are not Israelites but who live among the Israelites.
\v 10 However, as for the descendants of Levi, they stopped worshiping and obeying me. They did that when the rest of the Israelites did what was wrong by no longer worshiping me and instead worshiping other gods. So, I will punish the Levites for what they did wrong.
\v 11 They must serve in the temple area. They must guard the gatehouses that lead to the temple building, and they must take care of the temple building. They are the ones who must kill {the animals that the Israelites offer} as wholly burned sacrifices or as any other kind of sacrifice. They must help the rest of the Israelites.
\v 12 Here is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare. The Levites helped the rest of the Israelites worship other gods. They caused the Israelites to do what was wrong. So, I have solemnly promised that I will punish them for what they did wrong.
\v 13 They must not come near me to serve me as priests, and they must not come near any of my holy things or the most holy offerings. They must feel ashamed because they acted shamefully and did detestable things, and accept that I must punish them for what they did.
\v 14 Instead, I will assign them to take care of the temple and to do all of the work that anyone needs to do in it.
\p
\v 15 However, the priests who are Levites and who descend from Zadok faithfully served in my temple when the rest of the people of Israel abandoned me. So they are the {only} ones who may come near to me to serve me. I permit {only} them to stand before me and present to me the fat and the blood {of the sacrifices}. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 16 I permit {only} them to enter my temple, and I permit {only} them to come near my altar to serve me. I assign them to do everything that has to do with serving me.
\p
\v 17 Whenever they enter the gateways of the inner courtyard, they must put on linen clothes. They must not wear anything made of wool while they serve within the gateways of the inner courtyard or inside the temple.
\v 18 They must wear linen cloths wrapped around their heads and they must wear linen undergarments around their private parts. They must not wear anything that makes them perspire.
\v 19 Before they go back out to the outer courtyard where the people gather, they must take off the clothes in which they were serving and leave those clothes in the holy rooms. Then they must put on other clothes, so that they do not endanger the people {by touching them} with the holy clothes.
\v 20 These priests must not shave their heads, and they must not let their hair grow long. They must always keep {the hair on} their heads trimmed.
\v 21 No priest may drink wine when he goes into the inner courtyard.
\v 22 Priests must not marry widows {of men who were not priests} or women whose husbands have divorced them. They may marry only virgins who descend from the people of Israel, or widows whose husbands were priests.
\v 23 The priests must teach my people the difference between what is holy and what is ordinary, and they must show them the difference between things that are acceptable to me and things that are not acceptable to me.
\v 24 When there are disputes {between people}, the priests are the ones who must judge {what is right and wrong}. They must settle the dispute according to my laws. They must obey my regulations concerning all of the festivals that I have appointed, and they must keep my Sabbath days holy.
\v 25 {As a general rule,} a priest must not go near a corpse, because touching a corpse would defile him. However, he may become defiled in order to touch the dead body of his father or his mother, his son or his daughter, his brother, or a sister who has never married.
\v 26 {A priest who has become defiled must perform the rituals to make himself acceptable to me again.} After he has made himself acceptable again, he must wait seven days {before serving in the temple}.
\v 27 Then on the day when he goes into the inner courtyard of the holy place to serve me there, he must present an offering to take away his sin. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 28 Serving me will be the inheritance for the priests; I myself will provide everything that they need. You people must not give them any property in Israel, because they must rely on me.
\v 29 The priests are the ones who will eat the offerings {that people bring} of grain, the offerings that take away people’s sin, and the offerings that remove people’s guilt. Everything in Israel that the people dedicate completely to me will belong to the priests.
\v 30 The best of all of the first crops that you people harvest and every gift that you present to me will belong to the priests. You must also give to the priest the first batch of your dough. If you do that, I will bless {everyone who lives in} your homes.
\v 31 The priests must not eat any bird or animal that died on its own or that wild animals killed.”
\c 45
\p
\v 1 When you divide the land of Israel among the tribes and give each tribe its permanent share, you must set apart one portion of the land and present it to me, Yahweh, as a holy gift. That portion will be 13.5 kilometers long and 10.6 kilometers wide. The whole area inside its borders will be holy.
\v 2 Inside that area, a square plot 270 meters on each side will be for the temple, and an open space 27 meters wide will surround that plot on every side.
\v 3 Inside the holy area you must measure off a section 13.5 kilometers long and 5.3 kilometers wide. The sanctuary, the most holy place of all, will stand in{the square plot which will be inside}that section.
\v 4 That section will be the holy portion of the land, and it will belong to the priests, the ones who serve in the sanctuary and who come near to me, Yahweh, to serve me. There they will build their houses, and part of it will be a holy place for the sanctuary building.
\v 5 The other section that is 13.5 kilometers long and 5.3 kilometers wide will belong to the descendants of Levi who serve at the temple. That land will be their own property, and they can build cities to live in there.
\v 6 Alongside the holy portion that you set apart, you must give an area 2.7 kilometers wide and 13.5 kilometers long for the city. It will belong to all the people of Israel.
\v 7 The ruler will have land in two areas, on either side of the area set aside for the holy portion and the city portion. On the west of these portions, his land will begin where those portions end and extend westward to the sea. On the east side of these portions, his land will begin where those portions end and extend eastward to the eastern border of the country. So from west to east his land will be just as long as the territory that each tribe receives, from the sea to the eastern border, {with the holy and city portions dividing it in the middle}.
\v 8 That land will be the ruler’s own property in Israel. Then the rulers whom I appoint will no longer oppress my people. Instead, they will let the people of Israel have the rest of the land, and each tribe will receive its own share.
\p
\v 9 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: I am angry with you, rulers of Israel, because of what you have been doing! Stop acting violently and oppressing {the people}. Instead, do what is right and govern my people justly. Stop taking land from my people! That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 10 You must use accurate scales, accurate baskets for measuring dry things, and accurate containers for measuring liquids.
\v 11 The basket that you use for dry things and the container that you use for liquids must hold the same amount. Each of them must hold one tenth of a homer, that is, 22 liters{, because a homer holds 220 liters}. You must base both measures on the homer.
\v 12 Your shekel must weigh 20 gerahs (11 grams). Combining 20 shekels, 25 shekels, and 15 shekels must equal one mina.
\v 13 This is the offering that you must present {to the ruler of Israel}: out of every 60 liters of wheat {that you harvest}, you must give one liter, and out of every 60 liters of barley {that you harvest}, you must give one liter.
\v 14 This is the rule about olive oil: out of every 100 liters that you produce, you must give 1 liter. You may do this by giving the tenth part of a bath out of every homer, since a bath is the tenth part of a homer.
\v 15 You must also give one sheep out of every 200 sheep from the flocks that graze in the well-watered pastures of Israel. The priests will use all these things for the grain offerings, for the sacrifices that they burn completely, and for the offerings that the people make to maintain fellowship with me, so that I will forgive the people for their sins. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\v 16 All of the people of the land must give these things to the ruler in Israel.
\v 17 {From these things} the ruler then must provide {what the priests need} for the sacrifices that they burn completely, for the grain offerings, and for the drink offerings. He must do this at the festivals, at the celebrations of the new moon, and on the Sabbath days—at every time that I have appointed for the people of Israel to worship. He himself must provide what the priests need for the offerings that take away sin, the grain offerings, the sacrifices that the priests burn completely, and the offerings that maintain fellowship with me, so that I will forgive the people of Israel for their sins.
\p
\v 18 This is also what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: On the first {day} of the first month {of the year}, you must take a young bull that has no defects, and you must {offer it in order to} make the sanctuary acceptable to me.
\v 19 The priest must take some of the blood from that sacrifice that takes away sin and smear it on the doorposts of the temple, on the four corners of the ledge that is around the altar, and on the posts of the gateway into the inner courtyard.
\v 20 You must do the same thing on the seventh day of that month on behalf of anyone who sins without intending to sin and anyone who sins because he does not know that what he does is a sin. In this way you will make the temple acceptable to me.
\v 21 On the fourteenth day of the first month {of the year} you must begin to celebrate the Passover festival. It is a festival that lasts a full week of seven days. During this festival you must not eat bread that has yeast in it.
\v 22 On that first day {of the Passover festival} the ruler must provide a bull as an offering to take away his own sin and the sin of all of the people of the land.
\v 23 On each of the seven days of the festival he must provide seven bulls and seven rams that have no defects, and the priests will burn them completely as sacrifices to me, Yahweh. He must also provide a male goat each day as an offering to take away sin.
\v 24 For the grain offerings the ruler must provide 22 liters of flour with each bull and 22 liters with each ram, and 3.7 liters of olive oil with every 22 liters of flour.
\v 25 Then on the fifteenth day of the seventh month, when the people celebrate the Festival {of Shelters,} the ruler must provide on each of the seven days exactly what I have just told you to provide {at the Passover festival}: the same offerings to take away sin, the same sacrifices that the priests burn completely, the same grain offerings, and the same amount of olive oil.
\c 46
\p
\v 1 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: The gateway of the inner courtyard {of the temple} that faces east must remain closed during the six days each week when people work. But on every Sabbath day the gatekeeper must open it, and on every day when the new moon appears he must open it.
\v 2 The ruler must enter through the porch of that gateway from the outer courtyard, and he must stand beside the gatepost there. While he stands there, the priests must offer the animal that he brings for them to burn completely and the animals that he brings for the offering to maintain fellowship with me. Then he must bow down at the threshold of the gateway {to worship me}, and after that he may go out. The gatekeeper must leave that gateway open until evening.
\v 3 On the Sabbath days and on the days when the new moon appears, the ordinary people of the land must also gather at the entrance of that gateway and bow down before me there to worship me.
\v 4 On each Sabbath day the ruler must bring six lambs and one ram for the priests to burn completely as an offering to me, Yahweh. All of those animals must have no defects at all.
\v 5 Along with the ram he must bring 22 liters of flour as a grain offering, and along with the lambs he may bring as much flour as he chooses to give. With every 22 liters of flour he must bring 3.7 liters of olive oil.
\v 6 On each day when the new moon appears, he must bring a young bull from those in the herd that have no defects. He must also bring six lambs and a ram. All of those animals must have no defects at all.
\v 7 As a grain offering he must provide 22 liters of flour with the bull and 22 liters of flour with the ram, and with the lambs he may provide as much flour as he can afford. With every 22 liters of flour he must provide 3.7 liters of olive oil.
\v 8 Whenever the ruler comes in, he must enter through the porch of the gateway, and when he goes out, he must go out by that same way.
\v 9 Whenever the ordinary people come before me to worship me at the festivals that I have appointed, whoever enters through the north gateway must go out through the south gateway, and whoever enters through the south gateway must go out through the north gateway. None of them may turn around and leave through the gateway that he entered. Each of them must walk straight ahead and go out through the opposite gateway.
\v 10 The ruler must be among the people. He must go in when they go in, and he must go out when they go out.
\v 11 At the festivals and at the other times that I have appointed, the grain offering must be 22 liters of flour with the bull and 22 liters of flour with the ram, and with the lambs the ruler may bring as much flour as he chooses to give. With every 22 liters of flour he must bring 3.7 liters of olive oil.
\p
\v 12 Sometimes the ruler may want to bring to me, Yahweh, an offering that I have not required of him, whether an animal for the priests to burn completely or animals for an offering to maintain fellowship with me. When he wants to do that, the gatekeeper must open for him the gateway on the east side {of the temple}. Then he must present those offerings just as he does on a Sabbath day. Then after he goes out, the gatekeeper must close the gateway behind him.
\v 13 Every morning one of you must provide a lamb that is one year old and that has no defects at all, and the priests must burn it completely as an offering to me, Yahweh, for that day.
\v 14 Every morning one of you must also provide a grain offering to go with that lamb. The offering must be 3.7 liters of flour, and 1.2 liters of {olive} oil to moisten the flour. This rule about this grain offering to me, Yahweh, does not end.
\v 15 So every morning the priests must offer the lamb, the grain offering, and the {olive} oil, and they must burn that offering completely. They must do this every day.
\p
\v 16 This is what I, the Lord Yahweh, say: If the ruler gives {some of his land as} a gift to one of his sons, that land will belong to that son permanently. The son’s descendants will receive it after him, and they will own it permanently.
\v 17 However, if the ruler gives some of the land that I have allotted to him to one of his servants, that servant may keep it only until the year when everyone must return property to the family that first owned it. Then the land will belong to the ruler again. Only the ruler’s sons can inherit the ruler’s land.
\v 18 The ruler must not seize any of the land that any of the people own and thus force any of them from his own property. The ruler must give to his sons only land that he already owns himself, so that no one among my people has to leave the land that belongs to him.
\p
\v 19 Then the man {who was showing me the temple area} brought me through the entrance at the side of the gateway into the holy rooms that are on the north side {of the temple}. They are the rooms that belong to the priests. There I saw a place at the west end of those rooms.
\v 20 He said to me, “This is the place where the priests must boil the meat of the offerings that remove people’s guilt and the offerings that take away people’s sin, and where they must bake the flour from the grain offerings. They must do all of that here so that they do not carry those holy things out into the outer courtyard where people might touch them and thus become a holy sacrifice themselves.”
\v 21 Then the man brought me out to the outer courtyard and led me past its four corners. I saw that in each corner of that courtyard there was a smaller courtyard.
\v 22 Each of the four small courtyards in the four corners of the outer courtyard had walls around it. Each one was 21.5 meters long and 16 meters wide. All four of them were the same size.
\v 23 Around the inside of each of those four small courtyards was a {stone} ledge. Underneath the ledges, all the way around, workers had built places for cooking fires.
\v 24 The man said to me, “These are the kitchens where the men who serve in the temple must boil {the meat of} the sacrifices that the people bring.”
\c 47
\p
\v 1 Then the man brought me back to the entrance of the temple. I saw water there! It was flowing out from under the doorway of the temple and running toward the east, because the temple faced east. The water was flowing down from under the south side of the temple, south of the altar.
\v 2 Then the man led me out through the north gate and around the outside to the outer gate that faces east. There I saw water trickling out on the south side of that gate.
\v 3 As the man walked on toward the east, he held a measuring line in his hand. He measured off 540 meters {from the temple} and then led me through water {there} that came up to my ankles.
\v 4 Then he measured off {another} 540 meters and led me through water that came up to my knees. Then he measured off {another} 540 meters and led me through water that came up to my waist.
\v 5 Then he measured off {another} 540 meters, and {at that place} the water had become a river that I could not cross, because the water had risen so deep that anyone {who wanted to get to the other side} would have to swim. No one could walk through it.
\v 6 Then he said to me, “Human, think carefully about what you have seen.” Then he led me back to the bank of the river.
\v 7 As I came back, I was surprised to see that there were very many trees {that had grown up} on both sides of the river.
\v 8 Then he said to me, “This water flows out toward the eastern region and down into the Arabah Valley, and then it enters into the {Salt} Sea. When this water that flows out {from the temple} reaches that sea, it will make the salty water there fresh.
\v 9 This will happen: wherever this river flows, every kind of living creature that swarms {in the water} will live. There will be very many fish, because this water will come there and make the salty water fresh. Everywhere the river flows, every creature will live.
\v 10 This will also happen: fishermen will stand along the shore of that sea.{All the way around} from En Gedi to En Eglaim they will spread out their fishing nets. There will be very many kinds of fish there, as many kinds as there are in the Great Sea.
\v 11 But the swamps and the pools along the shore {of the Salt Sea} will stay salty, so that people can get salt from them.
\v 12 Fruit trees of every kind will grow along both banks of the river. Their leaves will never wither, and they will never stop producing fruit. They will produce fresh fruit every month, because the water that makes them grow flows out from the sanctuary. People will eat their fruit, and they will use their leaves to heal sick people.”
\p
\v 13 This is what the Lord Yahweh says: “I will tell you the boundaries of the land that you must divide among the twelve tribes of Israel as their permanent possession. The descendants of Joseph will receive two shares of it {and the Levites will not receive any}.
\v 14 You must divide it so that each {other} tribe receives a fair share. I solemnly promised your ancestors that I would give them this land, and now this land will become yours to own permanently.
\v 15 These will be the boundaries of the land. On the north, the border will begin at the Great Sea and extend along the road to Hethlon, past Lebo, and on to Zedad,
\v 16 and then to Hamath, Berothah, and Sibraim, which is between the territory of Damascus and the territory of Hamath, and on to Hazer Hattikon, which is on the border of Hauran.
\v 17 So the northern boundary will extend from the Great Sea to Hazar Enon. The boundary will be north of the territory of Damascus and the territory of Hamath will be north of the boundary. That will be the northern boundary {of the land}.
\v 18 On the east side, the boundary will extend {south} between {the territories of} Hauran and Damascus and then {turn west} to the Jordan River, which separates Gilead from the land of Israel. It will follow the Jordan River {south} to the eastern {salt} sea. You must measure it from the {northern} border down to the eastern sea{and to Tamar}. That will be the eastern border.
\v 19 On the south side, the boundary will extend from Tamar to the springs at Meribah Kadesh, and then along the streambed {at the border of Egypt} out to the Great Sea. That will be the southern boundary.
\v 20 On the west, the Great Sea will be the boundary. This boundary will extend from the southern boundary to a point opposite Lebo Hamath. That will be the western boundary.
\v 21 You must divide this land among yourselves, giving a share to each of the tribes of Israel.
\v 22 This is important: you must divide this land so that it becomes a permanent possession for yourselves and also for the foreigners who live among you and who have had children among you. You must consider those foreigners to be citizens, just like native Israelites. They must receive a share of land along with you, among the tribes of Israel.
\v 23 You must give each foreigner a share of land within the territory of the tribe among whom he has been living. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.”
\c 48
\p
\v 1 These are the names of the tribes {and the shares of land that each of them will receive}. The northern border of the country will run alongside the road to Hethlon, then on to Lebo Hamath and to Hazar Enan, which lies on the border of Damascus, with the region of Hamath to the north. The tribe of Dan will receive one share of land there, and it will stretch from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 2 South of Dan’s land, the tribe of Asher will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 3 South of Asher’s land, the tribe of Naphtali will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 4 South of Naphtali’s land, the tribe of Manasseh will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 5 South of Manasseh’s land, the tribe of Ephraim will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 6 South of Ephraim’s land, the tribe of Reuben will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 7 South of Reuben’s land, the tribe of Judah will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 8 South of Judah’s land, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea, is where the tract of land that you must set apart as a holy gift will be. It will measure about 13.5 kilometers from north to south, and from east to west it will be as long as one of the shares that the tribes receive. The sanctuary will be in the middle of it.
\v 9 The tract of land that you must set apart as a holy gift for me, Yahweh, will be about 13.5 kilometers long and about 5.4 kilometers wide.
\v 10 This holy tract of land will belong to the priests. It will be about 13.5 kilometers along its northern side, about 5.4 kilometers along its western side, about 5.4 kilometers along its eastern side, and about 13.5 kilometers along its southern side. My sanctuary will be in the middle of it.
\v 11 It will belong to the priests who are descendants of Zadok, the ones whom I set apart to serve me. They faithfully performed the duties that I assigned to them, and they did not abandon me when the rest of the people of Israel abandoned me, as the other descendants of Levi did.
\p
\v 12 So when you divide the land, this special tract will belong to the priests, and you will take it out of the holy gift that you set apart from the land. It will be the most holy part of all, and it will be next to the land that belongs to the descendants of Levi.
\v 13 The descendants of Levi will receive land alongside the territory of the priests, and their land will be about 13.5 kilometers long and about 5.4 kilometers wide. So each of these two tracts will be about 13.5 kilometers in length and about 5.4 kilometers in width.
\v 14 The descendants of Levi must never sell any of this land, and none of them may trade it away or transfer it to anyone else, because it is the best part of the land and it belongs to me, Yahweh, as something holy.
\v 15 The remaining strip of land, which will be about 2.7 kilometers wide and will be alongside the tract that is about 13.5 kilometers long, will not be holy. Ordinary people will use it, building their houses there and keeping their animals in the pastureland there, and the city will be in the middle of it.
\v 16 These will be the measurements of the city: about 2.4 kilometers along its northern side, about 2.4 kilometers along its southern side, about 2.4 kilometers along its eastern side, and about 2.4 kilometers along its western side.
\v 17 Pastureland will surround the city: about 135 meters on the north, about 135 meters on the south, about 135 meters on the east, and about 135 meters on the west.
\v 18 What remains of the strip alongside the holy tract will be about 5.4 kilometers to the east of the city and about 5.4 kilometers to the west of the city, and it will be alongside the holy tract. The people who work in the city will eat the crops that grow there.
\v 19 Workers from all the tribes of Israel will come to work in the city, and they are the ones who will farm that land.
\v 20 The entire tract that you set apart will be a square measuring about 13.5 kilometers on each side. In this way you will set apart the holy tract along with the property that belongs to the city.
\p
\v 21 The land on both sides of the holy tract and of the property belonging to the city will belong to the ruler. On the east, his land will begin at the edge of that square, which is about 13.5 kilometers wide, and goes to the eastern border of the country. On the west, his land will begin at the edge of that square and go to the Great Sea. These two areas together will be as long as one of the shares that the tribes receive, and the holy tract with the temple in it will be between them.
\v 22 The land of the ruler will be between the territory of the descendants of Judah and the territory of the descendants of Benjamin, and the property of the descendants of Levi and the property of the city will be in the middle of that.
\p
\v 23 The rest of the tribes will each receive one share of land that is from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea. {South of the holy tract,} the tribe of Benjamin will receive one share.
\v 24 South of Benjamin’s land, the tribe of Simeon will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 25 South of Simeon’s land, the tribe of Issachar will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 26 South of Issachar’s land, the tribe of Zebulun will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 27 South of Zebulun’s land, the tribe of Gad will receive one share, stretching from the eastern border of the country west to the Great Sea.
\v 28 South of Gad’s land will run the southern border of the country. It will go from Tamar to the waters of Meribah Kadesh, and then along the streambed at the border of Egypt out to the Great Sea.
\v 29 That is how you must divide the land among the tribes of Israel, and each tribe will own its share permanently. Those will be the portions that each tribe receives. That is what I, the Lord Yahweh, declare.
\p
\v 30 These will be the exits from the city. Its northern side will measure about 2.4 kilometers.
\v 31 You will name the gates of the city after the tribes of Israel. On the north side there will be three gates: name one gate for Reuben, name one gate for Judah, and name one gate for Levi.
\v 32 The eastern side will measure about 2.4 kilometers, and it will have three gates: name one gate for Joseph, name one gate for Benjamin, and name one gate for Dan.
\v 33 The southern side will measure about 2.4 kilometers, and it will have three gates: name one gate for Simeon, name one gate for Issachar, and name one gate for Zebulun.
\v 34 The western side will measure about 2.4 kilometers and it will have three gates: name one gate for Gad, name one gate for Asher, and name one gate for Naphtali.
\v 35 The distance all around the city will be about 9.7 kilometers. From that day on, the name of the city will be “Yahweh Is There.” 